At the end of trading, the Prague Stock Exchange collapsed to the minus
After a sharp slump in the morning, the Prague Stock Exchange corrected losses.
Transactions with CEZ shares reached almost halve of the normal daily trade.
The Prague Stock Exchange has continued its slump right from the start of Tuesday's trade, falling by nearly six percent.
This time behind the downturn is a slump in shares on Wall Street.
In our country, too, there is a reaction of the market to the outcome of the vote of the U.S. House of Representatives, which refused to support a plan to stabilize the local financial sector.
The fall of stocks in Asia
A dramatic fall on Tuesday was experienced by stocks on stock exchanges in Asia, although indices eventually erased part of the losses over the course of the day.
Hang Seng Stock Exchange's Hang Seng index in Hong Kong wrote off nearly four percent during trading, but later erased part of the losses and reduced the decline to about 2.5 percent.
Hang Seng China Enterprises index, which tracks the movement of Chinese stocks on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange, fell 3.8 per cent, in Shanghai the markets were closed.
Shares lost more than five per cent on the Sydney Stock Exchange, but eventually cut losses to 4.3 percent.
According to the country’s index, the stock exchange fell 3.6 percent.
"The timing of the bailout in the U.S. is uncertain and will affect financial markets around the world," said Joseph Yam, the head of Hong Kong's monetary ministry.
Hong Kong, although part of China, determines monetary policy independently, without depending on China’s central bank.
Hongong has interest rates at the same level as the United States.
U.S. lawmakers, according to Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, should urgently return to negotiations and approve a proposal to support the financial system.
There is a risk that other countries will feel the impact.
Bloody bath of U.S. stocks
On Monday, the U.S. House of Representatives rejected a plan to support the financial system, for which up to 700 billion dollars (nearly 12 trillion CZK) were to be spent.
Lawmakers have thus ignored President George W. Bush's call to support the plan.
According to Bush, the plan would address the root causes of the financial crisis and help stabilize the entire economy.
U.S. stocks experienced a bloodbath on Monday, and major stock indexes recorded their biggest falls in more than 20 years.
The Dow Jones index fell nearly seven per cent, showing a slump in a similar range for the last time in 1987.
The index was already falling before voting, but as soon as it turned out that the proposal had not passed in the House, the index found itself in a free fall.
U.S. government can pump $700bnilone $70bn into banks
The U.S. leaders and the administration of President George W. Bush have agreed on a broader form of financial assistance agreement for the U.S. financial system.
There will be a vote on it early next week.
U.S. lawmakers have made a breakthrough in talks on approving a bailout plan in the form of financial assistance for the U.S. financial system of 700 billion dollars (approximately 12 trillion crowns).
However, it has not yet been won.
Congressmen must finalize some of the details of the agreement before they can publish the final form of the bill and vote on it.
The plan to support the financial system will be discussed on the floor of the House of Representatives on Monday.
On Sunday, Barney Frank, chairman of the House Banking and Financial Services Committee, told Reuters.
In the Senate, according to sources, there could be a vote on the plan, apparently no earlier than Wednesday.
According to economists, the announcement that a bailout plan will be approved should be the first significant psychological factor for the recovery of financial markets.
After that, however, there will be "sobering up" thanks to the complexity of the mechanisms to help markets achieve even in practice.
Paulson: The plan must be effective
"We have made great progress.
“We’ve resolved our differing views on what the likeness of the market stabilization package is supposed to look like,” Democrat Nancy Pelosi told Bloomberg.
According to her, the final vote could still take place on Sunday.
Legislature representatives met with U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson last night to give a definitive form to the government fund.
It should buy unsaleable mortgage assets from financial companies that drag financial companies into heavy losses and threaten the stability of the entire system.
"We are on the verge of a definitive agreement of a plan that will work and be effective in the market, too.
We still need to continue the recovery plan, but I think we're there," Paulson said.
Signal for Asian Trading
The global financial crisis is significantly impacting stock markets, which are falling sharply.
According to Nevada Democratic Senator Harry Reid, lawmakers are trying to reach a definitive deal in Congress on Sunday.
They want to appease investors from trading on the Asian financial markets, which are the first in the time zone to influence Monday’s trading.
However, it is not yet clear with certainty when the two chambers of the U.S. U.S. Congress will vote on the bill, nor whether they can't get caught up in last-minute talks on any issue.
Lawmakers hope it will be approved in the next few days.
However, the law will undergo a number of changes.
The total amount intended to assist the system is to be divided into two parts.
The initial 350-billion-dollar billion is to be available as soon as possible, as President George Bush has demanded.
But Congress can block the release of the remaining amount if it feels that the program is not fulfilling its function, in tune with an additional $100 billion and later a final 350 billion dollars.
Bush praised progress in negotiations
The president may veto this decision, but Congress can overrule his veto.
Despite these changes, however, the basic idea behind the program, which was to raise funds for the purchase of bad mortgage securities, the value of which has plummeted because hundreds of thousands of Americans are unable to repay their mortgages, has been retained.
"We've worked out everything.
The House of Representatives should be able to vote on the bill on Sunday, and the Senate on Monday," Republican Senator Judd Gregg said in a statement.
U.S. President Bush is also pleased with the progress in the negotiations.
His spokesman, Tony Fratto, said Bush was satisfied with Saturday's progress and appreciated "both-party efforts to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economy."
Restriction of golden parachutes
The chairman of the Senate's banking committee, Christopher Dodd, a senior negotiator, said lawmakers have agreed on responsibility for the money program, the protection of taxpayers from losses, the conclusion of concessions, the timing of funding the plan.
The new law is also intended to limit so-called golden parachutes for directors of companies participating in the program and establishes a committee that will have oversight of the fund.
It should be run by the Treasury.
"We've been working very hard on it and we've made great progress towards an agreement that will work and be useful for both the market and all Americans," Paulson said in a statement.
The plan also counts on help for homeowners who have repayment problems.
The government should negotiate new repayments for the mortgages it buys in order to reduce the monthly repayment cost for borrowers and allow them to keep their homes.
Marek: Psychological assistance
According to Patria Finance chief economist David Mark, the proposed plan is a good idea, but its implementation will be very difficult.
"And that's because we don't know by what mechanism and at what cost the trouble assets will be redeemed.
This will determine how the financial sector and the government will share their losses among themselves," Marek told iHNed.cz this week.
Whether the plan will be approved now or early next week is not so important, according to Mark.
"It is much more important for congressmen to agree as early as this week that they support this plan and that they will sanctify it as soon as possible.
The psychological significance of the agreement that approval will take place is the most important thing at the moment," Marek emphasized.
Knife Blade Fight: MP3 Anti-Mobile Players.
We will advise you on how to choose
While almost every phone can play MP3 files, no MP3 player can make phone calls.
It seems that it is better to buy only a phone.
However, there are enough reasons for a person to buy a separate player.
The choice completely depends on the method of use and the demands of the future owner.
It’s probably hard to get a high-end gramophone to listen to the latest Maxim Turbulenc album.
On the other hand, such musical products do not appear on LP records, and from a qualitative point of view, there is no objective reason for this.
However, if vinyls with art rock, jazz or blues are oppressed in your shelves, you will probably at least dream of such a turntable.
It is the same with music on the road, i.e. music compressed, simplified - music in MP3.
There are also price and quality categories.
For some it may be unacceptable to spend eight thousand crowns on the player, just like the Pink Floyd fan, it is probably difficult to bite headphones for twenty crowns from the market.
Two extremes, among which there is enough space for everyone.
The choice is broad.
It also determines battery life
Some high-end music players can handle much more than just playing music, but what most mobiles don't happen.
No current device is as versatile as the phone.
In addition to primary features, telephony and texting, it can play music and video, record both, it has a better, worse camera, and maybe even navigate.
And considering smartphones and communicators, you'll have a small computer in your pocket.
It's a small miracle, after all, no refrigerator knows how to grill, and no TV can photograph anything.
The versatility of the phone also brings disadvantages.
The more features you use on your mobile, the sooner the indicator on the display will send you to the charger.
And playing music is one of the biggest eaters of energy.
Few phones can play music for more than ten-twelve hours, usually it’s much less.
In this respect, music players (especially the simplest ones) are significantly more powerful.
Therefore, for cheap mobiles, keep in mind that they will not play for more than a few hours.
If you still call it here and there, get another charger to work - this also applies to more expensive devices.
You won't have this problem with MP3 players, even the worse ones will last at least 9 hours of continuous operation on a single charge.
Another problem arises when uploading music to your mobile.
Unfortunately, only a limited part of the devices has a universal USB connector, and in addition, not all of them can be directly connected to a computer without special software.
Of course, music can be uploaded directly to the memory card, but only a few card readers can handle miniature memory cards that are used in mobiles without reduction.
You don't need any special software for players with exceptions (iPod).
It is enough to fill the device with music via USB from your computer.
Most mobiles and players already create a music library based on the name of the performer, the title of the album, the genre or the user’s rating.
Some devices display only the tree structure of directories, similar to Explorer in Windows, and some know both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smartphones with an operating system).
Players are easier to operate, mobiles can do more
When choosing a specific device, be especially interested in battery life, memory capacity, quality and type of sound output and, of course, the comfort of operation.
If the music is primarily a backdrop and if you listen to it every day only briefly on the tram, you only need any mobile phone that plays MP3.
Almost any mobile phone can do that.
You don’t even have to keep an eye on what musical formats your mobile
MP3 can handle everyone, and the vast majority of the music that gets to you will be in this format.
It is one of the most widely used, and in addition, the vast majority of devices can handle music in WMA format.
There's less AAC (the format used by Apple and its iPods).
For example, better mobiles make it possible to buy music online.
It is enough to visit the respective website of the operator (or other provider) over the mobile internet and purchase a song or album.
This service alone could already be a strong argument for acquiring a music mobile.
Unfortunately, the offer of operators is lousy and is usually limited only to fast-feeding radio hits.
Prices aren't the lowest either.
However, it is generally assumed that this way of selling music (whether over GSM, internet or wireless internet) dominates the music industry in the future.
Most people always carry their phones with them.
Whenever you want to listen to something, you feel your phone in your pocket while the MP3 player is forgotten at home.
However, it also depends on whether you also always carry headphones or at least wired hands-free.
If not, you can take advantage of an integrated speaker that has almost every phone, but only a minimum of MP3 players.
Probably the biggest advantage of musical mobiles will be the price for many.
The cheapest ones usually cost under three thousand.
For three and four thousand crowns, you will get a very decent walkman.
However, you will usually be limited by the absence of output to regular headphones.
The best and, in technical terms, the most equipped phones even with a 3.5 mm socket for ordinary headphones cost up to fifteen thousand.
Among other things, the advantage of players is easy operation.
The simplest - not necessarily the cheapest (see our selection) - players can be controlled by anyone.
The minimum of functions, the similarity of control with a previously common tape recorder or, for example, a turntable is obvious.
There is no collision in the form of accidentally switched on the mobile internet and subsequently a huge account for data transmission.
What device to reach for
Even the cheapest mobiles (e.g. Vodafone 527) usually have an interchange memory card slot (type M2, microSD, etc.).
Even with the purchase of the relevant card, you can fit into two thousand crowns.
Be careful, though.
Only cards with a maximum capacity of 2 GB are supported for basic models.
Only the most expensive ones can handle 8 GB of memory.
If you already have a mobile and you still want to buy a cheap MP3 player, reach for Cowon, Creative, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton or iriver.
According to the capacity (max. 2 GB), you do not have to spend more than 1 100 crowns.
If you’re one of the more demanding listeners, you’d better reach for specialized mobiles (Sony Ericsson, some Nokia and Samsung).
These devices have separate control buttons for MP3 and the operator comfort is thus close to a separate MP3 player.
The furthest is in Sony Ericsson, whose walkman phones have the same user interface as separately sold MP3 players.
The same service and in the same price range up to four thousand crowns, but with a larger memory capacity (up to 16 GB) and battery life (normally 15 hours, a maximum of up to 35 hours), will take MP3 players away from the above-mentioned brands.
The first virtual operator will arrive in the Czech Republic this year.
Maybe
The mobile market in the Czech Republic is relatively advanced and our operators offer virtually all services like their partners in Western Europe.
Still, we're missing something here - a virtual operator that would make the classic ones cheaper.
He may not be missing for long.
We have written about the fact that Czech operators are among the most expensive in Europe, we have written on Mobil.cz many times.
After all, this was also confirmed by the CTU, which pointed out that the price of SMS is higher in our country than in the rest of the Union.
After the last steps of the European Commission in the field of regulating roaming charges, there may be a paradoxical situation - for some customers of Czech mobile operators, SMS from abroad will be cheaper than at home.
How is it possible that prices in the Czech Republic are held so high compared to Europe?
The answer is simple, there's a lack of competition that really wants to fight.
It may sound paradoxical, after all, we have three mobile networks and penetration high over 100%.
On the contrary, mobile operators like to play on the fact that the Czech market is extremely competitive.
The President of the Czech Telecommunications Office (ČTÚ), Pavel Dvořák, who has said several times in the past that there is no place for another operator in the Czech Republic.
It's probably true that the classic operator with its own network probably doesn't have many chances (after all, it would probably be difficult to find frequencies).
But there is another option - a virtual operator.
The entry of virtual operators into the mobile market brought a significant reduction in prices, to which classical operators also had to respond.
At the same time, there were similar voices in the UK before the entry of pikes such as Tesco Mobile or Virgin Mobile.
It turns out, however, that the place for such operators is and can be really successful.
We don’t have to go too far for examples.
In February of this year, we informed you about the launch of the Mobilking virtual operator in Poland.
In just half a year, this operator managed to acquire more than 100 thousand customers and become the fifth largest operator there.
At the same time, Mobilking is the financial group Penta, which owns, among other things, U:fona in the Czech Republic.
The advantage of virtual operators is the ability to concentrate on a much more specific target group.
In order to function effectively, they need far fewer customers than a classic operator.
It does not entail relatively high costs for the construction, operation and maintenance of the mobile network.
In the world, virtual operators usually profile themselves as low-cost - offering cheap calls and SMS, which is often balanced by a narrower range of services.
For example, Tesco Mobile in the U.K. offers SMS at least half cheaper than its competitors.
However, as the example of Mobilking in Poland shows, it can be a pretty good move and a bet on a fresh target group.
This operator focuses specifically on men.
"The right guys choose Mobilking - an operator they like and that they can trust," says David Seibertz CEO of the Mobile Entertainment Company, running this virtual operator.
It is difficult to say whether a similar concept as in Poland could be grasped in our country.
Miroslav Čepický, Vodafone's spokesperson, doubts this: "The Czech market is relatively small for the segmental approach on which most virtual operators are based.
Moreover, in terms of the offer of services, it is relatively saturated.”
In the Czech Republic without a chance, say mobile operators
However, it seems almost certain that if there is a virtual mobile operator in the Czech Republic, it will not be Mobilking.
"We are talking about the expansion of this successful project, but in the Czech Republic the negotiations do not look very promising.
Rather, Mobilking will be launched in other countries of our region," Jana Studničková, spokeswoman for the Penta financial group, said in a telephone interview with Mobil.cz.
Indirectly, she confirmed that existing mobile operators in the Czech Republic are definitely not inclined to this idea.
On the contrary, they try to do everything so that no potential competitor arises.
GTS Novera has made no secret of its interest in launching a virtual mobile operator for a long time, which would like to take the next step towards becoming a real-world competitor to Telefónika O2 in the field of corporate customers.
"We are very interested in being able to offer not only fixed but also mobile telecommunications services to our customers.
And the sooner, the better.
We have repeatedly discussed this possibility with all Czech mobile operators.
With some of them, we have already managed to make progress in the negotiations,” says Pavel Kaidl, the group’s spokesperson.
Maybe he's flashing for better times.
From unofficial information, it seems that we should look forward to the first virtual operator perhaps by the end of the year.
For now, however, its creation is accompanied by an impenetrable wall of silence.
"At this moment, we do not believe that there is room on the Czech market for the creation of a full-fledged virtual operator.
The Czech market is very specific compared to other markets.
So far we have not been approached by any entity with a complex and serious project in this area," Vlastimil Sršeň from the Telefónika O2 press department said in a statement to Mobil.cz.
Other operators are also referring to the specificity of the Czech market.
Martina Kemrová, spokeswoman for T-Mobile, adds in rare agreement with O2: "We have not yet seen any viable and mutually beneficial business project on which it would be possible to build further."
He adds: "It's hard to have a virtual operator in the T-Mobile network by the end of the year.
At this point, we'd have to have a concrete agreement on the table in order to think in this short term.'
Vodafone is focusing its attention on fulfilling the requirements of its own 3G license.
Miroslav Čepický says: "By dealing with potential virtual operators, we are open, especially in terms of cost-sharing and infrastructure.
This is what we consider to be the most beneficial from the point of view of our customers.
Vodafone's main strategy in this area is to find a partner to build a shared network for fast mobile internet."
Responsible authorities - with operators one hand
The progress of mobile operators can be easily understood, the input of another operator would mean an increase in competition, it would lead to a decrease in prices and thus a decline in their profits.
So why make it easier for such a competitor?
The fact that if the operators are interested in something, they can pull together, there is also a tug-of-war around the creation of U:fon.
Probably the greatest pressure towards CTU came at the time of the creation of U:fon from T-Mobile.
This is evidenced by publicly available information from the CTU website.
But now U:fon is useful as an argument for not admitting a virtual operator.
Martina Kemrová commented: "The competitive environment of the mobile market has recently been strengthened by the entry of a fourth mobile operator into the market.
With regard to the nature of the mobile market in the Czech Republic, it is clear that the entry of another player would place high demands on him and, above all, on his services."
However, due to this approach of mobile operators, the complete lack of interest of Czech regulators is striking.
It is not just the attitude of the CTU, which unfortunately in the Czech Republic - unlike the United Kingdom and other developed countries - does not have the powers to intervene in competition.
These belong exclusively to the Office for the Protection of Competition (ÚOHS).
However, he took a typical attitude towards the whole issue.
"The entry of a virtual operator into the mobile market would be possible after a contract has been concluded between a mobile operator and a virtual operator to lease the necessary infrastructure.
No such contract has yet been concluded, because the offer of potential interested parties was probably not sufficiently interesting for classical operators," Kristián Chalupa from the Department of External Relations of the Office said in response to our question.
It may not be surprising from the representatives of the operators of such a formulated statement.
It is more thank of an office to represent the interests of consumers and, among other things, to ensure that no one abuses their position on the market.
Especially when Kristián Chalupa adds: "Abroad, where virtual operators routinely operate, we can observe that their entry into the market has contributed to a reduction in prices for mobile phone services."
However, the pressure on the correct functioning of the market environment and the decline in prices does not belong to the interests of the Office.
In fact, it is shocking in the whole case that three mobile operators, which result from their reactions, are touching.
The question of whether it is really just a coincidence is offered entirely by itself.
Like exactly the same price of gasoline at petrol stations, it is certainly just a work of chance and a tough competitive struggle.
"The Office did not find signs of anti-competitive conduct in this matter and therefore has no reason to intervene in any way.
None of the operators has a dominant position and therefore does not have an adequate increased responsibility for their conduct on the market.
The necessary tools for the opening of the mobile market for virtual operators are probably lacking for the telecommunications regulator," Chalupa said in a statement.
The interpretation of the dominant position in the market is one of many contentious points.
Of course, there is an analysis of the relevant markets prepared by CTU, according to which telecommunications from the point of view of competition are the only market.
Of course, none of the Czech operators has a dominant position on such a market.
However, some legal experts offer a different perspective - the creation of a virtual operator always requires the consent of the network operator.
And the mobile operator - quite logically - has a monopoly on its network.
Therefore, in a certain segment, it necessarily has to be dominant.
However, even if we did not accept this argument, it should be at least a warning for the Office that three mobile operators proceed strikingly similarly.
At the same time, complaints from a number of interested parties (including GTS Novera) show that they encounter very similar obstacles when dealing with all operators.
In telecommunications it is a relatively usual trick that the ÚOHS puts its hands away from the whole matter.
We’ve met him in several similar cases.
Unfortunately, until the recently adopted amendment to the Telecommunications Act, the extension of CTU’s powers to competition in telecommunications has not been enforced, even though a number of experts have promised to improve the situation.
This must be known more than well, but apparently their interests are elsewhere.
It's possible when no one's watching
At the same time, the functioning of some pseudo-virtual operators, of which there are several in Czech mobile networks, remains unnoticed.
They are companies issuing their own price lists of mobile telephone services, but they are only partially public.
These are usually multi-level marketing companies.
His own mobile telephone services are offered by Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and many others.
The prices of these companies usually only get to the chosen ones.
The magic is that the company acts towards the operator as a classic corporate customer, ordering a large number of SIM cards.
As an important customer, he then receives special price (and other) offers.
The problem is that the terms and conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit a similar resale.
"If there are cases that you are talking about, it could be a violation of the contractual terms, which we would deal with and could result in the termination of cooperation with the company," Martina Kemrová replied to our question about this issue.
However, it is quite unlikely that such activity will not reach the responsible people of mobile operators.
Especially in the case of the three mentioned companies, there can be thousands of customers.
However, none of the operators oppose such a procedure.
From the activities of these companies, they have considerable profits and, unlike real virtual operators, do not endanger them with their own official marketing.
Miroslav Čepický points out possible problems: "The law allows similar speculation, but only a negligible percentage of customers use the services of these companies.
In addition, they have to accept that the SIM card on their phone is not kept in their name, so they cannot dispose of it fully as ordinary customers of mobile operators."
However, this should be at least strange.
In addition, another question opens up for UOHS.
If mobile operators close their eyes to a similar procedure, why do they block the creation of a virtual operator?
It should be added that the Office has quite strong powers to reveal a similar activity, and if it does not want it, it does not have to be satisfied with an explanation by the operators that they have no idea about such a thing and their terms and conditions prohibit it.
When won't we be most expensive
As has already happened several times in the text, the entry of virtual operators into the mobile market has brought a noticeable decrease in prices for services.
At the same time, in virtually almost all the countries where virtual operators were created, classical operators tried to defend their creation more or less.
Their arguments were practically identical - too small, possibly too saturated market, problems with the quality of services, etc.
In developed countries, however, it was at these moments that regulators intervened, defending not the interests of mobile operators, but their customers.
Whether by formal measures or informal pressure, they achieved the creation of the first virtual operator and packed with others.
Unfortunately, Czech regulators are tossing the whole case like a hot potato.
From our experience, we cannot shake the impression that confusion in competencies suits them more than in these cases.
But let's not throw flint into the rye.
If the behind-the-scenes information is correct and the virtual operator will really soon appear in our country, the Czech mobile market may move again.
If one of the operators admitted the creation of a virtual mobile operator, it will hardly be possible for the ÚOHS to remain indifferent to the refusal (discrimination) of other interested parties.
And maybe we'll stop calling more expensive than in the rest of Europe.
The new Czech anthem has four new versions.
Choose the best one
Listen to the four versions of the Czech national anthem.
On Sunday, Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek presented new recordings of the national anthem Where My Home.
The anthem was newly recorded by the orchestra of the National Theatre under the direction of Jiří BĚhlávek.
There are four versions available - with male or female solo singing, in choral presentation and instrumental.
Kateřina Kněžínková and Adam Plachetka performed singing.
The authors of the modifications of the anthem are Otakar Jeremiáš and Jaroslav Krček.
You can listen to all four versions on iHNed.cz.
BĚlohlávek considers the Czech national song to be one of the most beautiful hymens.
He has previously told Czech Radio that he deliberately selected singers with young representative voices, which are distinctive and popular.
"From the beginning, I tried to take care with both of them, and then with the choir, so that they had a clear diction, that the text would be well understood, that they would agree," he said.
The Czech national anthem will celebrate its 175th anniversary next year.
The song Where My Home was performed for the first time in 1834 on the boards of the Estates Theatre in the original Czech play Fidlovaček by Josef Kajetán Tyl, accompanied by music by František Škroup.
It gained popularity, and people spontaneously took it as their own.
After the establishment of Czechoslovakia in 1918, the first stanza of the song became a natural part of the national anthem.
One of the seven state symbols remained even after the establishment of the independent Czech Republic.
What's the "new" anthem
Prague public transport on Wednesday for the first time will also ride on a chip
Wednesday, October 1, 2008 will go down in the history of the Prague Transport Company as the date when the first passengers who have an electronic coupon in their pocket will enter the metro, trams and buses.
At the moment, he can buy about a hundred thousand people who own an Opencard smart card.
Passengers can buy a 30-day and 90-day and annual coupon (which will be valid from 1 January 2009) and at the same time have their chip card uploaded to their smart card in the information centers and workplaces of the Transport Company in metro stations Muzeum, Můstek, Nádraží Holešovice, Anděl, Roztyly, Letňany, Kobylisy, Paradise Garden, Kačerov and Luka.
Coupons can also be purchased over the Internet on the website of the Transport Company www.dpp.cz since yesterday.
More than four hundred customers took advantage of the service by the afternoon.
Those who start using the online store can definitely forget about dispensing windows in metro stations, where until now they had to stand regularly in long queues for paper tickets.
They buy the coupon over the Internet and upload it to their Opencard in one of the thirteen branches of the Municipal Library.
"In these places there is a partition with a trained operator, which will help those interested with the online purchase and also upload the purchased coupon to their card," said Martin Opatrný, a spokesman for the project.
In addition to libraries, it will be possible to upload electronic coupons to Opencard also in self-service machines, which are mounted in several metro vestibules.
What do automatons look like?
They are red, they resemble ticket markers, and passengers may have already noticed them in Roztyly, Budweiser, I. P. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská metro stations.
"There are now five of them in the city, but by the end of the year there should be eighty," Opatrný added.
Coupon will check the driver's seat vending machine
The operation of the vending machines, which will start working tomorrow, is simple.
All you have to do is insert an Opencard smart card into them, and the previously paid coupon is loaded onto it by itself.
Electronic coupons from tomorrow apply not only in the capital, but also in its vicinity.
That is, in suburban trains and bus lines with the number 300 to 499, through which mainly residents of Central Bohemian municipalities travel to the capital.
"On trains included in Prague Integrated Transport, the conductors are equipped with card readers, through which they determine the temporal and band validity of coupons," explained Filip Drápal from the Ropid organization, which compiles timetables for Prague public transport.
Suburban buses have card readers built into the driver's ticket office.
Therefore, it is necessary not only to show the smart card to the driver, but it is also necessary to attach Opencard to the designated place on the side of the cash register.
In doing so, the driver checks the validity of the recorded coupons.
"If passengers need to verify the validity of recorded coupons, there is nothing easier than asking the driver for this information," Drápal added.
Passengers who do not take suburban buses and do not remember when their coupon expires can go again to self-service machines, where coupons are uploaded.
Here, information about the validity of their documents will be displayed on the display of the device.
Sudetenland wants U.S. referendum
Sudeten Germans in 1945 demanded the incorporation of Šumava to the United States.
It flows from the letter, which is stored in the collections of the South Bohemian Research Library.
If we can't be part of Germany or Austria, we want to live at least under the patronage of the United States of America.
With such a wish, some groups of Sudeten Germans reportedly appealed to U.S. president in 1945.
It follows, among other things, a letter that was preserved in one private archive and is now stored in the collections of the South Bohemian Research Library.
The Germans from Český Krumlov ask in a letter from the American president not to allow the return of the "Shumava county" - seized by Hitler on the basis of the Munich agreement - back to Czechoslovakia.
"We understood the situation correctly, and we really want to be sure that American troops come to German territory not as oppressors and enemies, but as liberators," reads the letter, signed with the words "The people to whom Šumava's fate is at heart."
Since the connection to Czechoslovakia supposedly gives Sudeten Germans - with the knowledge of a Czech national nature - hope only for brutality, ruthlessness, misunderstanding, oppression and slavery, the authors of the American president's letter beg to take over Šumava the Protectorate for the present and eternal times.
"We cannot believe that, regardless of the opinion of a quarter of a million inhabitants, you would admit this territory to a foreign country that oppresses and disproves minorities and did nothing to contribute anything to the victory of the Allies," the letter said.
The reason why the authors can't sign is supposedly a concern about the vindictiveness of "Czech oppressors."
If the president of the U.S. reportedly doubts about the thinking of the Šumava Germans, it was enough for him to hold a referendum on whether its inhabitants want to become part of the U.S. or live under the patronage of the United States.
Although the text of the letter may sound a little naïve with today's knowledge of post-war history, according to some witnesses, it appropriately illustrates the expectations of Sudeten Germans at that time.
Even though the Czech Germans had to at least suspect that it would be their expulsion from the republic, few of them even acknowledged such an option.
Some expected the United States and the United Kingdom to negotiate a kind of autonomy for the Sudetens.
Others believed that they would simply remain in Czechoslovakia.
This is also confirmed by the famous Austrian journalist Gustav Chalupa, born in České Budějovice.
During the war he was like a Budweiser German deployed to the navy on the Baltic.
At the end of the war he was captured by the British and placed in a prison camp in Holstein, dozens of other Czech Germans went to him to teach them Czech.
"They believed that when they came home, Czech would come in handy," Chalupa said.
According to him, none of the German prisoners living before the war in Czechoslovakia anticipated that he would be displaced from the republic on the basis of Beneš’s decrees.
You don't think - you'll pay!
Emotive campaign attacks drivers
The most expensive event focused on road safety in Czech history.
Even so, it could be characterized by an emotional media campaign, which the Ministry of Transport intends to launch on Wednesday.
The office of Minister Aleš Řebíček promises to deter the driver from dangerous behavior behind the wheel.
The campaign for 150 million will appear on television, radio, cinema, on the Internet, but also on leaflets.
It should deter the driver from driving and alcohol at the wheel.
And also to remind them, before they go out, to be handcuffed.
He wants to reach into his conscience especially to young drivers, motorcyclists, pedestrians and, last but not least, professional drivers.
And what will appear in spots?
Mainly human stories that attack emotions.
And that's evidenced by the "bloody" slogan of the whole campaign:
You don't think - you'll pay!
The word "don't mean" is written in capital letters on an espézetka.
And in a pool of blood is the word "you'll pay."
This is supposed to symbolize that a person can pay with one's own life in a moment of inattention.
Audiences will "frighten" spots until 2010.
The ministry was inspired by similar campaigns in Anglo-Saxon countries, where images with drastic images of accident victims can normally be seen.
It promises that this will reduce the accidency and the number of deaths on Czech roads.
Spoty was prepared by Euro RSCG.
Sugar and whiplash for the driver
"The driver's sugar and whip system applies.
The sugar for them is that if, for example, they go for red, it saves time.
A whip is a threat of a fine or an accident.
Drivers must be reminded of the risk,” says Martin Kořán, a traffic psychologist at Na Homolce Hospital.
And that's why there's a need for information campaigns in the press and, above all, negative advertising, the psychologist said.
According to him, boards that alert drivers to danger are already less effective.
"If a static board is installed warning of danger, it will probably soon merge with the surroundings and the drivers will not attend to it," Kořán pointed out.
Olmert would return almost all occupied territories in exchange for peace
Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Olmert has voiced support for the return of virtually all territories occupied in 1967, including Pre-Jordany, East Jerusalem and the Golan Heights, in exchange for peace with Palestinians and Syria.
He did so in an interview published Monday by Jediot Achronot.
"We must come to an agreement with the Palestinians, which means that it will be necessary to return almost all (occupied) territories, if not at all," Olmert told the newspaper.
"We will retain a certain percentage of these territories, but we will have to give the Palestinians a similar percentage (of the territory of Israel), because there will be no peace without it," he added.
He acknowledged that East Jerusalem would include a possible return.
"He who wants security in Jerusalem, and in particular, does not want tractors or bulldozers to run over his feet, as happened to one of my best friends, will have to give up parts of Jerusalem," Olmert noted, alluding to two July attacks in a city in which several people were killed and dozens injured.
According to the prime minister, it will be necessary to find "a special solution for the Temple Mount, sacred and historical places" that lie within the Old Town.
“He who wants to maintain Israeli sovereignty over the whole city will have to integrate 270 000 Arabs.
That won't work," he stressed further.
Leaving from the Golan Heights
Olmert has also spoken out for Israel's departure from the Golan Heights, which he said is an essential condition for concluding a peace deal with Syria.
However, this would also require some efforts from Damascus.
"I'm not proposing to make peace with Syria alone on the basis of Leaving from Golan," he said.
"Syrians know very well what they will have to give up in order to acquire the Golans.
They will have to give up their current relationship with Iran; they will have to give up their relationship with (Shiite) Hezbollah; they will have to give up the lasting support provided by the terrorism (Shiite movement) of Hamas, (terrorist networks) of both Al-Qaida and jihad (holy war) in Iraq.”
Questions after the accident: Are stewardes on the bus illegal?
The law doesn't know them, you can't walk on a long-distance bus
In connection with the tragic accident of a Student Agency bus near Padua, the question arose as to whether stewardes and passengers can move around the bus while driving.
"If we want to strictly and formally comply with the law, the activity of the steward is in violation of the law on road traffic," says Karel Hanzelka, a spokesman for the Ministry of Transport.
The accident, in which a twenty-two-year-old stewardess died last week, was apparently caused by the driver of the truck, who crossed the road.
Unfortunately, the girl was sitting next to the driver, in the least safe place in the car, although she still has two other seats reserved.
However, the bus can crash or brake sharply, even when the stevard delivers coffee or newspapers.
It plays a role whether the bus, according to the technical card, is equipped with places to stand.
If it is not homologated on the bus, passengers must sit.
Student Agency long-distance buses do not have parking spaces; which was also confirmed by Aktuálně.cz director Radim Jančura.
"We discussed this with the Ministry and did not allow the movement of passengers at first.
Afterwards, they dismissed it and we came to the conclusion that passengers are allowed to go to the toilet while driving if they have their seat," he said.
He doesn't know Steward's law
"There is no unambiguous legal interpretation of the steward's role - no legislation solves this role.
However, no legal regulation even prohibits the carrier from providing services.
The Road Transport Act knows only the term conductor of the vehicle; it does not define its rights and obligations," Hanzelka's spokesperson adds less clearly.
On the contrary, Josef Pokorný, the director of the Department of Road Vehicle Traffic of the Ministry of Transport, says that the law treats the issue adequately.
"It depends on whether the bus is allowed standing places in homologation.
That's the end of it," he says.
Buses are divided into urban, where there are places for parking and limited maximum speed, intercities with places for parking with a higher speed limit and for long-distance parking spaces with the highest speed limit and seats equipped with seat belts.
It's like on a plane, the expert says
BESIP transport safety expert Jaroslav Hořín does not see a fundamental problem in the movement of stewards on board the bus.
"There is no danger when driving smoothly, the situation can be compared to servicing in airplanes.
It's not even certain when turbulence will come and the stewardess can hit her head.'
"However, if the bus brakes sharply, of course, physical laws apply.
Just like in a city bus or tram.
The safety of stewards must be dealt with by carriers by internal regulations," he says, and also means, from the point of view of the law, the issue is unresolved.
Jančura is willing to cancel the services
The Student Agency offers steward services as the only bus carrier.
It employs two hundred of them.
Even after an accident, the director does not consider their profession to be risky.
"But as soon as I get the impression that the steward vocation is risky, I'll abolish it.
Coffee and newspapers can be given to passengers at boarding.
It would also save costs," he says.
As an interest in safety justified the absence of stewardesses in their cars, the director of Asiana Alexei Litvin, which operates a fraction of the lines compared to SA.
"We don't have stewardesses, we've been against that from the beginning.
Sooner or later it will end with some fatal accident," he told Týden.cz less than a year ago.
There has been an immense tragedy
A young stewardess from Pilsen, who worked for the company for two years, was killed in an accident.
The Slovenian truck had probably departed without the use of a signpost from the shut-down lane and blocked the bus's path.
Its driver tried to avoid a violent manoeuvre, but hit the right front.
Jančura refuses that two places reserved for stewardes's rest would be sold out.
"These places cannot be reserved for long-distance connections, they are blocked."
The company intends to sue truck drivers.
With the compensation of the girl's family, the agency will not wait for the ortel of the court, but he expects financial compensation from the culprit.
Adjusting the law?
I'm not in sight
Specifying the movement of stewards aboard the bus could bring an amendment to the law, on which a thirty-member expert group of the Ministry of Transport has been working for over a year.
"But there was no concrete conclusion.
Buses don't even solve much, rather the point system, the maximum speed on the highway and the use of a mobile phone while driving," says expert Hořín.
"The Ministry of Transport does not consider the activities of stewards to be something that would fundamentally endanger the safety of passengers," Hanzelka concludes, adding that inspection of the safety of buses is within the competence of the police and occupational safety authorities.
Düsseldorf's hope is Frankfurt
There are no big words in WestLB’s letter to the bank’s employees.
By combining units of this Düsseldorf money institution with the Frankfurt Deka-Bank, a "central institution of the financial group of savings banks for trade in the capital markets," we will read in a letter that came to the public on Monday.
