Now, after the elections to the regional assembly in Bavoria, BayernLB's merger with Landesbank Baden-Württemberg could be the first to begin.
That's not exactly good news for Helaba.
If the pressure for further mergers were to increase, it gradually lost possible partners for such a connection.
In the south, she would play only a second violin, even though the financial crisis has so far faced significantly better than banking houses in Munich and Stuttgart.
At the beginning of the year, the Association of Savings and Gior Banks Hessen-Thüringen, which has an eighty-five percent stake in Helaba, was banned.
Nord LB would be considered at most.
However, geostrategic considerations of this kind are only one side of the problem.
On the other hand, it is a question of how much the business models of individual institutions fit together.
In June, Merl declared that the decisive question of which business model should ensure the future competitiveness of provincial banks, the German Association of Savings Banks and Giorist Banks has not yet answered.
Everything could also end with the distribution of WestLB, which, of course, no one wants in this institution.
That's what's letter from the local board of directors also talks about trying for a "comprehensive solution" for the entire banking house.
Whether and how it will be possible to connect it with the interconnection of individual folders with Deka-Bank will tell time.
