Sleepless nights for creditors Hypo Real Estate
Last week, Federal Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück stressed that Germany's banking system was relatively stable.
It's good that this politician used the word "relatively" and the fact that Germany's banking sector will not be spared for crisis development.
For it happened over the past weekend.
The Dax-tracked financial house specializing in property finance Hypo Real Estate (HRE) will receive a loan of several billiona from a consortium of German banks to protect against turbulence in the markets.
A person familiar with the situation told Reuters on Monday that the loan aid would go up to 35 billion euros.
Interest will have to be paid by Hypo Real Estate (HRE) only from the amount actually spent.
The money comes from both private and public banks.
In the background are the losses of Depfa's subsidiary.
According to a newspaper report, this financier refinanced extremely short-term for long-term projects for which he lent money, which is no longer possible now due to disbelief in the markets.
In the first half of 2008, the bank's reserve shriveled by 95 % to 2.37 million euros and the share of equity decreased slightly from 1.6 to 1.45 percent.
Current revenues and net returns on financial trades have fallen to zero compared to last year, revenues from the increased value of receivables and from certain securities, as well as from the abolition of reserves for credit trading have fallen by more than 70 percent to 5 million euros.
Only thanks to the higher proceeds of compensation for subsequent clearing costs in the restructuring of the former Depfa group and the compensation of interest according to the Tax Code and for the intermediation of trade for group companies, the sixteen percent decline on the revenue side was still moderate.
After its privatisation in 1990, an earlier state financier burned himself on mortgages for tradesmen, which were then separated from the portfolio of services and further maintained by Aareal Bank.
Because there was too little money in classical state funding, Depfa became more strongly involved in infrastructure projects and in the financing of joint projects of private investors and the state (Private-Public-Partnership, PPP) and diversified internationally.
From the value of its subsidiary, Hypo Real Estate now has to write off something, and these depreciations will have a "major material effect" on calculating the profits and losses of the entire group, as some have been heard vaguely.
The fact is, there won't be a dividend.
It has already been shortened by two-thirds in 2007.
Hypo Real Estate has been a major financier of state and infrastructure projects since Depf took over.
Stronger than other institutes depends on refinancing in the interbank market because it has no customer deposits.
Two weeks ago, Lehman Brothers virtually dripped the source of interbank lending because banks are guarding liquidity.
Hypo Real Estate needs short-term refinancing funds per year for part of Depfa's lending portfolio - about 50 billion euros.
According to the Hypo Real Estate statement, the group's financial need is covered by this loan for the foreseeable future and the firm is protected "from the influence of international money markets at the moment to a large extent incapable functions".
Other sources were told that this money would ensure refinancing by the end of 2009.
HRE Chairman Georg Funke, on the other hand, stated that Hypo Real Estate Group would not have to take advantage of money markets anytime soon.
The Federal Bank and the Federal Institute for Financial Services Supervision (BaFin) assume that the competitiveness of the Hypo Real Estate Group on the market is now ensured.
That's what the bank's shareholders and creditors are hoping for.
But given the dynamics and intangibilities of the crisis, there is probably no certainty.
It is not even entirely excluded that the loan does not need to be exhausted completely.
However, the opposite cannot be ruled out.
After the bank appeared to be facing insolvency late on Sunday afternoon, it is unable, albeit tentative, to reassure passions.
The stock's exchange rate fell 75 per cent to 3.30 euros.
On the other hand, bonding courses were well maintained.
While preferential debts have practically not moved, they are losing rates of secondary bonds more significantly.
This applies in particular to the bonds of the former subsidiary Württemberg Hypo and logically bonds with a maturity period after 2009.
Hypo-Real-Estate initially looked like an alternative investment opportunity for speculators, but their stability needs to be understood, depending on the stability of the international financial system, after a significant intensification of the banking crisis and the crisis of the institute itself, which brought it into the company by taking over Depfa.
As long as issuance banks, governments and lesser-affected commercial banks are willing to support the system, it is possible to count on the payment of securities within their maturity period.
However, there may also be what almost no one dares to hope for at the moment - a financial crisis will be overcome in the next 15 months.
However, in view of the reports coming at the same time as this partial nationalization, the probability bordering on certainty has now become a mere legitimate hope.
This, especially for long-term creditors, is likely to prepare a number of waking nights when they will decide whether to give up their obligations.
Short-term investors can use the marginal line at least to create a transfer of loss from the previous financial years to the exchange rate revenues of later years for the purpose of the future payment of capital income tax.
Sylvana and the mystery of the bento box
When sixteen-year-old Sylvana Hiltrop is supposed to introduce herself on her first day at high school in Tachibana, the girls start giggling: the German is completely "kawaii"!
What does that mean?
Sylvana already knows this word from mangoes. On his new blog about an exchange stay, he talks about Japan.
"The first day in Japan.
Sir, I was nervous.
I've been out of my way to school.
I live on the outskirts of Tokyo towards Kawasaki and take a 40-minute train to high school in Tachibana.
I had to find the trip myself, fortunately I didn't get lost.
It seems that the beginning of teaching at Tachibana is not given as strictly as at home in Lower Saxony.
Students will start meeting after eight o'clock, and twenty minutes before 9 a.m
But even before classes began, I was supposed to introduce myself.
I'm not the kind of person who likes to speak in front of people, let alone 40 students in front of a class.
Since I didn't have a school uniform yet, I wore the old skirt of my hoster's mum, a blouse, blue socks and black leather shoes.
So I stood in front of the bench and tried to pronounce everything correctly: 'Sajónara Wo še Sylvana... - my name is Sylvana, I come from Germany and it's my birthday in a week.
At that moment, I was very glad that I started learning the language two years ago.
All 31 girls in class were whispering, giggling and waving, and I kept hearing the word 'kawaii'.
I've known that from my mangoes.
It belongs to the basic vocabulary of Japanese girls necessary for survival and means 'cute, sweet'.
Then I quickly sailed into my seat.
The girl next to me smiled quite broadly, she considered it an honor that a foreign student was supposed to sit next to her.
The rest of the hour was fairly calm, but when the gong sounded, they literally buried me.
They spoke over each other and giggled and asked me about the possible and the impossible - and yet it's really difficult to understand Japanese English.
During the break, everyone unwrapped their benta.
My host mother also made me such a box for lunch - and that's something other than boring German snacks.
I had rice with herbs, small sausages, cucumber and algae salad, fruit and a kind of fish in tofu batter.
I don't usually know exactly what I'm eating here in Japan.
These things look odd and taste quite new.
What's certain is that everything's really good.
Well, except for the salt-loaded plums, br!
Benta in Japanese schools isn't just lunch.
Sometimes it happens that a girl gives a baby hand-prepared bento, which in this case is especially artistically adapted, and hopes to get to know the boy closer during a snap during the break.
That's what I read about in my manga comics.
I wonder if I'll see it here, too.
'Why Japan?' my friends asked me when I told them I wanted to go to school in Tokyo for a year.
Then I always said:
'Because I'm fascinated.'
After all, I've loved reading mangoes for years.
And to better understand these comics and their culture, I started to learn a little Japanese one day.
It was obvious to me that I would have to come here someday.
Because I also have a weakness for tradition, customs and customs.
And they're highly appreciated here.
On the one hand, Japan is a country of the latest technologies and trends, on the other, it is strict, disciplined and traditional.
And because everything is so well orchestrated, I've never felt lost here before, even though Tokyo is the largest city I've ever seen.'
Rats have feelings, too
When a rat or cockroach runs away from danger, does that mean they're also feeling afraid?
Neurologist Joseph LeDoux is convinced that even many animals have feelings.
But how would it be possible to verify whether they feel the same thing when they behave like us?
Posts from well-known scientists have been published in the online journal "Edge" for several years.
SPIEGEL ONLINE in its series presents selected answers to the question "What do you think is true without being able to prove it?"
I believe that animals have feelings and other states of consciousness, without I or anyone else being able to prove it, when we can’t even prove that other people have consciousness.
But in this case, we can at least hope, because all human brains have the same basic configuration.
However, once we begin to focus on a different kind and open up questions of perception or universal consciousness, we enter impassable terrain, because the hardware does not match.
When a rat gets into danger, it will do the same thing as many other animals: it will pretend to be dead, flee or attack.
Humans behave similarly, of which some scientists conclude on a comparable subjective experience.
I don’t believe in that, though.
Two aspects of the brain structure make it difficult to transfer our subjective experience to experiencing animals.
This is because circuits associated with human consciousness almost always include the lateral prefrontal cortex (due to its share of short-term memory and performance or control functions).
This extensive region is significantly more developed in humans than in other primates, and it seems to be completely lacking in other creatures.
At least in those aspects of our consciousness that depend on the prefrontal cortex, among them the knowledge of who we are, and the ability to plan and make decisions, there is a reason to believe that a person differs even from other primates.
Another dramatic peculiarity could hide the linguistic nature of a person: because such a large part of his perception is associated with language, it is often said that consciousness depends on it.
In this case, we could exclude the animals.
But even if consciousness didn't matter about the language, it would certainly influence the language, so that animal consciousness, no matter how it's constructed, would probably differ from most states of our consciousness.
That's why it's difficult to see what consciousness looks like in other animals.
When, as something internal and subjective, it cannot even be measured, because of special hardware, to analyze on the basis of our own experience, it puts us before its examination before major problems.
Most of my outlets relate to the content of conscious experience, but in addition, there is another aspect of consciousness that should be scientifically more easily accessible.
It would be possible to examine the processes in the consciousness of animals regardless of their content, and this is exactly what happens in short-term memory studies in primates, especially on the example of visualization.
But Christof Koch and Francis Crick focus on the neural correlates of consciousness rather than on causal mechanisms: both can be identical, but they may not.
Interestingly, this approach also emphasizes the importance of the non-frontal cortex for the implementation of visualization.
What does this mean for feelings?
I conclude that an emotional system, such as a system of fear, is activated in the brain conscious of one's own activity.
What we refer to as "fear" is therefore the mental state in which we find ourselves when the activity of a defense system localized in the brain (or its consequences, such as bodily reactions) uses operating memory.
Seen from this angle, feelings are closely intertwined with those areas of the cortex that characterize primates, and especially people.
In connection with linguistic nature, there is a subtle graduation of perception, because through words and grammar we can distinguish and categorize states in order to attribute them side-by-side to others.
Antonio Damasio, on the other hand, believes that feelings arise from more primitive activities in cortex areas and the brain stem devoted to bodily sensations, similar to Jaak Pankseep, which, however, focuses more on the cerebral stem.
Since this network has not changed much in the course of human evolution, it could easily participate in feelings perceived by multiple animals.
In theory, in theory, I cannot contradict it, but I consider it unprovable.
Pankseep argues that both organisms probably feel the same way about what appears to us in rats and humans: But how is it possible to verify Pankseep, whether rats and humans feel the same thing when they behave similarly?
Cockroach flees from danger - does he also feel fear?
From my point of view, the mere similarity of behavior cannot prove the conformity of experience.
It's true that neural comparisons move us further: rats and humans have similar brain stems, while cockroaches, and brais.
But is the brain stem responsible for feelings?
And even if it was possible to prove it in man: how would we want to prove it with rats?
I believe that rats and other mammals, maybe even cockroaches (who know?), have feelings.
But I really don't know how I should prove it.
And because their feelings are probably fundamentally different from ours (because human consciousness rests on special circuits and language), I examine emotional behavior rather than the emotional perception of rats.
Anyway, in this case, we can expect progress on a neural level if we measure the same in rats and in humans.
As with rats, I don't study language and consciousness, I don't examine feelings, I don't know if they exist.
It's possible to find it short-sighted, but I'd rather move forward rather than repeatedly throw myself against the wall.
I'm a practical emotionalist.
UEFA President Platini: Deadline from Grace for Poland and Ukraine
Acting Prime Minister Ivan Vasjunyk said the preparations will soon be directed directly by the president and the prime minister.
"The government sees UEFA's decision in Bordeaux as evidence of confidence in Ukraine and its ability together with Poland to organize the ME," Vasjunyk said.
“It’s not an amnesty for our country.
