Flight prices and hotels are falling
In the first half of 2008, flights and accommodation in Europe were cheaper than in the previous year.
In Germany, according to a recent study, flights were even cheaper by up to 50 percent.
Flights and accommodation in hotels have become cheaper again - this is the result of a study by travel and financial services provider American Express.
The company analyzed its data on reservations made from the first half of 2008 and after the increase in prices in the first quarter found their significant decrease in the second quarter.
After prices on the hotel market grew for several years, accommodation in many places has already become cheaper.
From January to March, prices in 41 of the 48 European cities surveyed still increased by an average of 6.9% compared to 2007.
From April to June, however, prices were already 3.6 percent lower than in the previous year.
Most noticeable was this trend in Paris and Rome, where passengers had to pay 30 to 37 percent less than in the previous year.
As a cause of this trend, American Express considers a weak exchange rate of the dollar, which caused "the declining appetite of U.S. tourists to travel."
The decline in hotel prices has also affected financial centres more severely than industrial centres, such as Hannover, Cologne or Stavanger.
In this Norwegian city, hotel prices rose by 26.3 percent even recorded a European record.
Ticket prices also showed an upward trend in the first quarter:
Due to kerosine prices, passengers in the Economy class had to pay an average of 7.1 percent more than in 2007, fuel-intensive long-haul flights increased by up to 29.2 percent.
From April to June, ticket prices fell by an average of 12.5 percent compared to the previous quarter, but changes were much less pronounced in Business and First-Class classes.
In Germany, prices fell even more sharply than in other European countries:
They rose 11.9 percent from January to March, falling 23.6 percent in the second quarter.
On German domestic flights, according to the cited study, prices fell even by half.
One of the reasons for this price fall is slowing economic growth, the firm explains.
Demand in many segments of the air transport market is uncertain.
On the other hand, due to the Open Skies agreement, which liberalized air traffic between the U.S. and Europe, there is increased competitive pressure.
A sign of the tense situation is the cancellation of unbearable routes by air carriers, as well as the high number of airlines that declared bankruptcy in the first half of 2008 - in Europe alone there were 24 airlines.
