Dark matter influences space probe flight
For years, scientists have been unable to cope with one mysterious phenomenon: dark matter influences the speed of space probes during the so-called swing-by maneuver.
Experts fear that Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity might not be quite correct.
For nearly 18 years, the "Ulysses" space probe has flown through space.
Due to the fuel lines, which will soon freeze, she is now at risk of deathing in winter.
Among scientists for years, one mysterious phenomenon causes confusion - the anomaly of the gravitational sling.
Gravitation slit is an aerial maneuver of space probes in which they gain additional momentum in the gravitational field of a cosmic body, such as Earth.
Thanks to this, probes can save a lot of fuel.
The Cassini-Huygens Euro-American research probe used this physical trick as well as the Ulysses solar probe.
But what scientists have stated in this case for years in amazement: the flying bodies do not behave one hundred percent in the way one would expect according to the good old law of gravity.
On the contrary, irregularities occur at speed.
Probes unexpectedly accelerate or slow down.
So far, physicists have not been able to explain this effect.
He was baptized with an anomaly of a gravitational sling.
Several experts are concerned that perhaps even Albert Einstein’s universal theory of relativity, which describes the attractiveness of matter by curving space, might not be entirely correct.
American physicist Stephen Adler of the Institute for Advanced Study in Princeton continues to be convinced that Einstein was not wrong, and tries to explain the anomaly of the gravitational slinger using so-called dark matter, the presence of which was discovered by astronomers in the universe.
"Responsible for this anomaly could be dark matter, which could also be very close to Earth," he writes.
Cosmic explorers have only known about the existence of dark matter for about ten years.
It is clearly based on a gravitational force whose effect on other cosmic bodies can be observed.
On the contrary, she herself is completely invisible, which means that she does not broadcast or reflect any electromagnetic radiation, just visible light.
According to the calculations of scientists, a good fifth of the total mass of the universe is from this dark matter.
