Abuse by stepfather 'led to suicide'
Several-year abuse by the stepfather led the woman to kill herself has been heard in court.
After pleading guilty to eight cases of sexual harassment, seventy-seven-year-old Michael Trerise of Stithians of Cornwall was sentenced to six years.
The Crown Court in Truro heard his stepdaughter, Stephanie Randle, was regularly tied when she was between five and seven years old.
"I feel responsible for her death," Trerise wrote in the confession.
It was also said in court that Ms. Randle killed herself at her Mutley home in Plymouth on April 1, 2007 at the age of 34.
When she lived in Coventry as a child, Trerise tied her and blindfolded her and subsequently sexually abused her while her mother worked in a pub.
Trerise divorced his wife in the 80's and returned to Cornwall.
His stepdaughter went to Plymouth.
However, it was heard before the court that Ms. Randle, who spent a substantial part of her time as a volunteer for the National Trust organization, was haunted by the spectre of the abusing that had been perpetrated twice a week.
Prosecutor Ramsey Quaife said in court in 26rd that Miss Randle's friends, who was also very dedicated to surfing and cycling, said she was "emotionally dead" and that she "renounced her life."
In April 2007, her real father, Terrance Randle, found her dead in her Plymouth apartment.
She's suffocated with a plastic bag.
Randle faced his daughter's stepfather, who wrote the confession, which ended with: "I tied her and blindfolded her.
“I didn’t abuse her.
“I feel responsible for her death.”
Judge Christopher Elwen told Treris, "You're an extremely strange fetishist, and it must have been gruesome for your victim when she's only been seven years old.
"This unpleasant story ends when the victim took his own life.
What's even more terrible is that if she hadn't done so, she probably wouldn't have gone to court at all.'
