"Of course she'd probably be a promising, successful and happy young woman."
An official who lost data will be charged
The BBC has learned that a government official who left top-secrecy documents on a train in June will be charged under the State Secrets Act.
The man in question left two top-secret documents on a train on Waterloo while he was temporarily assigned to the Ministry of Defence.
The documents were handed over to the BBC before being handed over to police.
It is the least serious offense against that law and there is no indication of criminal intention.
BBC Defence Affairs correspondent Frank Gardner said such a move would be surprising to many in Whitehall.
"They probably thought the matter was being handled internally between the ministry and the Cabinet Office," he said.
An employee who may not be appointed is charged under Section 8.1 of the law in question, a clause that deals with the protection of information.
The crown prosecutor has advised the Metropolitan Police to indict an official on the basis of the very sensitive nature of his work in the Joint Intelligence Committee.
The documents he had forgotten contained classified analyses of Al Qaeda and the capabilities of Iraqi security forces.
He was informed of the decision on Monday morning and was moved from home to an unknown location.
Co-operatives face B&B's debt
Britain's credit unions may have to deal with more than 80 million pounds in debt after the rescue of Bradford & Bingley.
The government has provided 2.6 million clients at Bradford & Bingley with 14 billion pounds to protect deposits.
The interest of this loan will be repaid by all firms that take client deposits, including 59 credit unions.
Adrian Coles, director-general of the Association of Co-operatives (BSA), said it was "humiliating" that credit unions and their members had to pay.
The fourteen-billion-dollar debt will eventually be repaid by saving Bradford & Bingley's mortgage.
However, the interest accumulated in the meantime will be repaid each year by all 700 financial firms that take client deposits.
The Money Services Compensation Plan for the BBC has confirmed that all 59 credit unions are counted in this issue.
It's humbling that these institutions, which have behaved prudently... now have to repay some of the accounts of those institutions that weren't nearly as judicious
Adrian Coles, BSA
He said every firm is supposed to pay proportionally to the deposits it held, and that midfies are supposed to pay about 18% of the price.
The Treasury's Treasury estimates that interest on debt will rise to 450 million pounds in 2009.
This means banks will have to find 81 million pounds to repay first-year interest.
Adrian Coles, director-general of the Co-operative credit unions trade association, has told the BBC that he believes this is not fair.
"It is humiliating that those institutions that behaved judiciously during the expansion of the housing market now have to repay some accounts of those institutions that were not nearly as judicious."
According to the BSA, no banks have fallen since 1945, when they appeared.
"We will have to examine all aspects of the matter within the next few weeks to see what are the options to protect credit unions and their members from an account that could be substantial," Cole added.
Interest due in 2009 covers seven months from the end of September to the end of March.
There will be an interest payment for the whole year in 2010, and that could be almost twice as much.
Due to the fact that credit unions are mutual organizations without shareholders, this account will eventually be paid by their members through higher fees or lower interest rates in their accounts.
Milk will increase by five pennies per litre
People in Jersey might have to pay more for milk.
The Jersey dairy has announced that it will increase the wholesale price of its milk by five pence per litre from Monday.
It is the second price increase this year, after a two-pence price increase in February, and comes after a research carried out by a consulting company.
Retailers have to decide for themselves if they want to reflect the increase in customer prices.
Prior to the February increase, Jersey's wholesale milk price last changed in 2002.
Bail for Bangladesh ex-prime minister rejected
A Bangladeshi court has dismissed former Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina Vajida's request for release on bail, which means that if she returns to her homeland, she can be arrested.
Her lawyers said the court had refused to release her on bail in connection with accusations that she received more than fifty million taka (729 700 dollars) from a certain businessmaker when she was prime minister.
The case is one of several allegations of corruption brought against it.
Khalida Ziya, a long-time political rival of Sheikh Hasina, was released on bail earlier this month.
Sheikh Hasina is due to return to Bangladesh within the next few weeks, but her party (Awami League) has demanded that the local army-held government first give her complete freedom.
Correspondents say that while she has been dismissed on a word of honour so that she can undergo a medical procedure abroad, it is not clear, according to the conditions of her release, whether she will remain free when she returns to Bangladesh.
Her lawyers say she will now appeal the order of the High Court to the Supreme Court.
"The High Court dismissed Sheikh Hasina's request for bail, saying he did not need bail when he is already undergoing an examination released on an affidavitary abroad," her defense attorney Rafique-ul Huq said.
"We'll appeal the High Court order."
Her lawyers said she needed bail to be able to return from abroad to the country in preparation for the December elections.
A senior representative of the People's Ligue said Sheikh Hasina is due to return home after October 17.
She was detained last year after an interim government took power by an army, but was released from prison in June in order to go abroad for medical intervention.
Both Sheikh Hasina and Khalida Ziya both deny all charges against each other.
