New Investigation of the U.S. Advocates
The U.S. chief prosecutor has appointed a special accuser to investigate the release of nine U.S. attorneys in 2006.
Tah came in a situation where a report on an affair drawn up by the Justice Department's chief inspector criticised the activities of government officials.
The report criticised White House officials, lawmaker and former Justice Secretary Albert Gonzales.
The investigation was prompted by fears that lawyers had been removed for political reasons.
Special Prosecutor Nora Dannehy will now investigate whether officials interrogated during an investigation have lied to investigators to obstruct justice.
Inspector-General Glenn Fine said in the report: "Serious allegations, including potential criminal conduct, have not been fully investigated or explained."
Appointing a special prosecutor with the power to force witnesses to testify was one of his key recommendations.
The dispute began when the U.S. Department of Justice deprived nine U.S. attorneys in December 266 of their functions.
At the time, the ministry said the attorneys had been dismissed because of weak performances.
However, it later became clear that the dismissed lawyers were no worse than many of their colleagues, leading many observers to conclude that redundancies were motivated politically.
Some of the attorneys were involved in prosecuting high-ranking Republican deputies and senators, while others have refused to investigate allegations of voter deception against Democrats.
U.S. attorneys are investigating and running lawsuits in court cases on behalf of the government.
They may be dismissed at any time, but may not be removed for political reasons.
