Other officials, including former senior Bush adviser Karl Rove, have been criticized for not cooperating in the investigation.
Meat Loaf's command will be honored
Rock star Meat Loaf receives one of the Q Awards for his epic anthem Bat Out Of Hell.
The singer, who will attend a ceremony next weekend in London for his special gold award, said he was "honored" by his recognition.
The song, which lasts nine minutes and fifty seconds, was Meat Loaf's 1977 title track.
This year's nominations are led by Coldplay, which has been shortlisted in four categories.
Paul Rees, chief editor of Magazine Q, said: "There are some songs standing above such things, like time and genre, and Bat Out Of Hell is certainly one of them."
The song, written by Jim Steinman, needed large vocals provided by Meat Loaf.
It began with an instrumental part that lasted almost two minutes.
The song was released in Britain in 1979 as a single and reached the 15th.
But Meat Loaf's album Bat Out Of Hell has held the chart for about nine years and has sold more than forty million copies around the world.
Meat Loaf - the real name Marvin Lee Aday - has been suffering from health problems in recent years, having also been operated on in 2003 because of an irregular heart rhythm after collapsing on stage.
In 2007, acute laryngitis forced him to cancel concerts.
It later turned out that he had a cyst on his vocal cords.
Obscure streets, often forgotten and possibly early for sale
The city of New York has thousands of roads, streets, highways and other roads, some famous, others only utility.
But even many taxi drivers-veterans and long-time locals have difficulty finding Extra Place, mostly because they've never heard of it.
This tiny aisle is in the East Village and leads north from First Street - without making it to Second Street - between Bowery and the Second Avenue.
At a width of about ten and forty meters in length, it resembles a dusty passage rather than a move.
There's no signs with street names.
It is practically invisible in a regular atlas.
However, there is an aisle of admirers.
For example, Avalon Bay, a developer of recently built luxury buildings on First Street.
It wants to pave Extra Place and create a cleaner passage to shops and boutiques that are supposed to open in new buildings.
Other admirers are long-time neighbors, who say they have been enchanted by the shortened aisle simply because it was one of the last remnants from before the block was refined.
However, the Municipal Office for the Protection and Development of Development, which has a street in its power, has not the slightest interest in sticking to it.
At a meeting of the municipal community's land committee, representatives of the municipal authority and Avalon Bay outlined a plan according to which the developer should buy the street.
However, according to Suzan Stetzer, District Manager of the Municipal Community 3, the committee favors such an option, when the street will remain in the hands of the city, and has asked for a new plan to be submitted next month.
"The city already owns very few plots of land, and we'd like Extra Place to remain in its possession," she said.
"Public and administration proposals could be made, which Avalon could do, but we also want to guarantee public accessibility."
Avalon Bay announced a few months ago that it wants to pave the small street and install seats, several of which would be used by the cafe, whose interest in renting the premises the developer expects.
The idea that Avalon Bay could buy the street was first published at a meeting this month.
Michele de Milly, spokeswoman for Avalon Bay, said that although the company wants to use the street, it doesn't necessarily want to own it.
"Avalon Bay's main intention is to repair this desolate aisle and make it a beautiful place for the public," she said, adding that the sale was proposed by the city.
Seth Donlin, a spokesman for the Office for the Preservation of Deployments, said the city really wanted to sell the street, noting that the task of his office was to develop the property rather than manage it.
Compared to its more colorful neighbor, Bowery, Extra Place has left a shallow mark in history.
Some people think that the street got its name in 1802, when Philip Minthorn's large farm was divided among several of his children, and no one was entitled to that narrow streak of land.
To Extra Place led the back door CBGB, a punk-rock monument that closed in 2006, and the aisle is perhaps best known as the background for a photograph on the cover of the Ramones "Rocket to Russia" album.
"The place was amazing," says Danny Fields, Ramones manager, who took a picture in November 1976.
"It's been full of garbage, torn clothes and pieces of barrels, posters, leaves and ropes."
Some locals, including bloggers who write about ennobling the East Village neighborhood, oppose the idea of selling Extra Place, arguing that that piece of public property, albeit modest, should remain public.
Others consider Extra Place a rarity - an almost unchanged piece of the city that should be left as it is.
Jeremiah Moss, who writes a blog named Jeremiah's disappearing New York (subtitled "The Book of Laments: A Bitterly Nostagical View of the City in the Process of Demication"), posted part of a story about Extra Place from the section Talking About the City, which appeared in The New Yorker newspaper in 1952.
Brendan Gill wrote: "Extra Place is a narrow blind aisle, dark even during the day and bounded by the rusty iron gates of warehouses and hammered windows where a week-old newspaper runs through the ditches."
With a few exceptions, the aisle looked exactly the same one recent evening.
The rat passed through the blind end of the street near the door that once led to CBGB, in the place of which today stands an expensive clothing store.
There was graffiti on the walls, and next to a fresh batch of discarded newspaper lay an empty beer bottle.
A few passers-by said they rarely think about Extra Place, but suggested that he could use a boost.
Fields, by contrast, said he thought some people underestimated the appeal of the aisle in its current state.
"The best years of my life," he said, "were in places dark, wet and vile."
Debate on bilingualism: immersion in English
In this edition of Focus on Education, Bruce Fuller and Lance T. Izumi will discuss candidates' position on bilingual education.
Lance T. Izumi, an academic from California and director of education at the Pacific Research Institute for Public Policy, co-writes the book "Worse Than You Think: Why the Middle Class Needes Choosing Schools."
Efficientely addressing Hispanic voters is a tricky matter.
Barack Obama's educational agenda only proves that.
Obama's campaign notes that "Afro-American and Hispanic students are substantially less likely to graduate than white pupils," which is true.
In order to struggle with such performance differences, Obama's educational agenda promotes, among other things, "transitional bilingual education" for those who are yet to learn English.
However, the question for Barack Obama remains whether his commitment to bilingual education, which puts an emphasis on using languages other than English in the classroom, is stronger than his interest in reducing performance differences.
Take, for example, Sixth Street Prep, a charter elementary school in Los Angeles East.
Pupils of this school are, in the vast majority of cases, Hispanics from low-income families.
More than a third of the students, many of whom have only recently arrived, are still learning English.
Even so, one hundred percent of fourth-grade pupils reached the highest level of state mathematics tests in 2008.
Nearly an equally amazing ninety-three percent of fourth-grade pupils made it to the highest level in state tests from the English language and literature.
This incredible achievement has been achieved by a different method than that preferred by Obama.
Sixth Street emphasizes control and practice, constant assessment of abilities and access without excuses.
Furthermore, Obama should notice, according to Linda Mikels, director of Sixth Street, the pedagogical approach of the school for those who are yet to learn English is a "complete immersion."
Immersion in English emphasizes the almost privileged use of English in teaching.
Miss Mikels, who is against bilingual education, told me: "We've had tremendous success with one completely new pupil from Mexico - after twelve months you'd walk into the classroom and not know what it was."
"It's working," she said, "it's working for us."
Would Obama take a school like Sixth Street Prep as a role model for what other schools with large proportions of Hispanic and English-leading pupils should look like?
The results achieved by the school should take for granted from the answer to this question, but the educational agenda of its own party (the National Education Association is a long-standing advocate of bilingual education) and its own consistent support for bilingual education makes it unclear to predict Obama’s response.
While Obama agrees that immigrants should learn English, he recently trivialized when he declared that people should stop worrying about "only English" laws.
Instead, "you should make sure that your children speak Spanish," he said.
If Obama really wishes to erase performance differences, he should think carefully about educational patterns that work rather than mock them and trivialize them.
Oil is falling sharply due to renewed economic concerns
Oil prices fell sharply on Monday because of fears that even the U.S. plan to save financial markets might not revive the economy.
Oil futures prices fell as high as 7 per cent to 99.80 dollars per barrel in morning trading on the New York Commodity Exchange.
Since last Monday, prices have fallen by more than twenty dollars per barrel.
In the past two weeks, commodity markets have been shaken by financial riots on Wall Street, as well as a pair of strong hurricans in the Gulf of Mexico.
After reaching a peak of 145.29 dollars per barrel in July earlier this month, prices fell to almost 90 dollars per barrel earlier this month, after which they jumped again on a wave of huge uncertainty in the financial markets.
Anxiety gripped investors again on Monday, even after the congressional bureau announced it had reached an agreement over the weekend regarding the biggest rescue plan in history.
This report has been overshadowed by recent fears that the financial crisis is far from over.
In the latest episode of advancing decomposition, the government announced that Citigrop Group would buy Wachovia Corporation's banking section.
In the meantime, the Belgian, Dutch and Luxembourg governments have partially nationalized the European financial conglomerate Fortis.
Barclays Capital's analysts said the frenzied weekend talks, which led to a plan to save the economy, "prove incapable of revivinginging the mood in the market."
As the economic situation deteriorates, demand for commodities, including oil, is expected to decline.
"The prospects for markets for global stocks, interest rates and exchange rates have become very uneasy," analysts from Deutsche Bank wrote in a report to investors.
"We believe that commodities will not be able to escape contagion.
From a commodity perspective, our biggest concern is the extent to which the U.S. virus spreads around the world, especially to China.”
Analysts at the bank limited their expectations for next year as oil consumption declined due to slower growth in the economy, cutting their gasoline price forecasts for 2009 by about 20%.
A wild ride in the second quarter broke the team's record
After spending more than anyone in the Jets jersey over the past eight seasons, defensive player Shaun Ellis thought he'd seen everything before.
Then on Sunday he watched the development of the second quarter, in which the Jets turned a pointless game into a splendid ride.
"I've never seen anything like it," Ellis said.
The second quarter began after the Jets and the Arizona Cardinals matched the bleak clouds above each other with their troubled play.
Then quarterback Brett Favre sent Laveranues Coles for a twelve-yard touchdown sprint, perfectly foreshadowing an afternoon in which their gradually unfolding collaboration moved a mile ahead.
Favre dispatched Coles in that quarter for the next two touchdowns, including the one in which the cornerback fell, and the next 10 seconds before the half of the game.
"It wasn't anything extraordinary," Favre said of all his passes to Coles.
"He's a hell of a player.
“We’ve done great communication, no matter what people think.”
While Favre and the Coles tossed, the defense attacked Cardinals quarterback Kurt Warner.
Cornerback Darelle Revis caught a stray throw and put him back 32 yards for a touchdown.
Safety Eric Smith picked out another pass and intercepted it.
Linebacker David Bowens forced Warner to fumble.
The last loss of the ball, the fumble that Bowens enforced, was perhaps the best ever.
The Cardinals got to the ball 10 seconds before the end, and after the Jets got the ball back, Jay Feely kicked a twenty-yard goal from the field, taking a 34-0 lead.
That's the most Jets have ever gained in one-quarter.
Jets coach Eric Mangini, when he was invited to describe the madness, said, "The second quarter, or the third quarter?"
When he was told that both - the Jets allowed 21 points unanswered in the third quarter - Mangini said: "I can tell you that I enjoyed the second quarter a lot more than the third.
There were a lot of points in a short period of time.”
Rested Gebreselassie improved his world record
Haile Gebreselassie of Ethiopia once again showed why many consider him the best long-distance runner ever when he broke his own world record on Sunday in the Berlin marathon and became the first person to run a 26.2-mile-long race under two hours and four minutes.
By winning on a pleasantly flat track in a time of 2:03:59, he overwhelmed by 27 seconds his previous time of 2:04:26, which he ran in Berlin last year.
Gebreselassie, who is 35, has set the 26th world record of his career, which is truly incredibly wide - breaking the world record or at least being a world champion at all distances from a metric mile to a marathon.
Last month, Gebreselassie skipped the Olympic marathon in Beijing because he feared - in hindsight, perhaps too alarmed - that polluted air irritates his asthma problems.
Instead, he vowed to run in Berlin.
In near-perfect conditions - sunny and 48 degrees at the start, 55 degrees at the finish - Gebreselassie won on Sunday with a lightning-fast average of 4 minutes and 43 seconds per mile.
What track runners like Gebreselassie and Paul Tergat of Kenya focused on the longer tracks, the marathoning record in the last decade dropped by more than two minutes, since Ronaldo da Costa of Brazil won in Berlin in a time of 2:06:05 in 1998.
After Sunday's victory, Gebreselassie told reporters that he had cramps in his calf two weeks ago and skipped a week of training.
Clearly, the break aroused him rather than delayed him.
"I'm so happy," Gebreselassie told reporters.
“Everything was perfect.
I started again a week ago and I was in a bit of a doubt today, but everything was fine.”
He was also criticized - mainly in Kenya, Ethiopia's East African rival - for overly planning his races, generally avoiding the world's marathon peaks, relying on conductors as if it were a race on the track, and putting world records above a sense of competition.
But this criticism can hardly reduce Gebreselassie's huge running qualities.
Maintained at a pace by four Kenyans, Gebreselassie reached the halves in 1:02:04.
After the conductors fell away, Gebreselassie was constantly chased by two elite Kenyan runners James Kwambai and Charles Kamathi.
A little earlier than three miles before Gebreselassie's goal, he took the last and escaped Kwambai, who then finished second in a time of 2:05:36 and improved his personal best by almost five minutes.
Kamathi finished third in 2:07:48.
In the women's race completed an excellent year with the seventh fastest time in history 2:19:19 Irina Mikitěnková, a native of Kazakhstan, who is now a citizen of Germany.
A record time of 2:15:25 is held by Paula Radcliffe of Great Britain.
Thirty-six-year-old Mikitěnková surprisingly won last spring in the London Marathon in 2:24:14.
She skipped the Beijing Games because of something that was described as a back injury, but she recovered and won in Berlin, and in the third marathon she had ever run, she improved her personal best by nearly five minutes.
She ran the race smartly and thoughtfully: she left four Ethiopian and Kenyan runners to a big lead and then dragged them after about 18 1⁄2 miles.
Askale Tafa Magarsa of Ethiopia finished second in 2:21:31, and Helene Kiprop of Kenya finished third in a time of 2:25:01.
Pride and Obstacles to the Lives of Negroes in a Separate City
Hiding among the larger Orlando's sprawling theme parks, a few miles away from everything shiny, noisy and huge, lies a quiet city in which the pride and difficulty of African-American life comes alive.
Eatonville, the first completely black city to be incorporated into the state and where Zora Neale Hurston spent her childhood, is no longer as simple as she described it in 1935: "a city of five lakes, three croquette playgrounds, three hundred brown skins, three hundred good swimmers, plenty of quality, two schools and no prison."
Now it's a place of pilgrimage.
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee have come to the annual Edonville Zora! festival to compose a tribute to Hurston, the Harlem Renaissance's most famous female writer.
The city she described remains aside, even as it has made it a stop for tourists by including him in a Florida tourist guide created by the Federal Writers project from the time of the economic crisis.
It's still as independent, dignified and private as it was in the 1930s when Hurston wrote that rural black people in Florida often resisted and didn't share their true feelings with a white man who "knows so little about us that he doesn't even know what he's missing."
Even today, in a year when a black presidential nominee, Senator Barack Obama, calls for an open discussion about races, many residents here remain cautious about the views of strangers.
"We're very careful about how our story is told," says 59-year-old Hortense Jones, who has lived here since birth and is a member of the city's oldest parish.
"It has to be right."
Eatonville has long been characterized as a paradox of triumph and exertion.
It's both a historical model of black rise and a community of nearly 2400 people, where the poverty rate is double above the national average.
It is the center of literature, but also an oak shaded by an example of southern rural black culture - sometimes despised, sometimes vaunted - which arose from American slavery.
It is not very surprising that these residents here are both proud and protective.
And worrying about Eatonville's idea really started with Zora, which is the only way Hurstons are called here.
She introduced her hometown to the world through heartfelt books called "Mules and Men" (1935) and "Their Eyes Were Watching God (1937).
Five paragraphs of Florida's tourist guide turned the city a short distance into Route 17, which leads through Florida's often-received center, into a stage of black history and human drama.
With a big drum in life and in literature, Hurston introduced readers to the shop of the first Edonville mayor, Joe Clark, and then turned to more personal places.
"To the left next to the road," she wrote, "is a white brown-decorated modern national school with well-maintained yards and playgrounds, which is always taken care of by Howard Miller, even if he can't read or write much."
She also mentioned the new husband of widow Dash and wrote that Lee Glenn "sells drinks of all kinds and anything that fits into an hourly hotel."
So, in just a few hundred words, Hurston connected Eatonville with self-government, but also with illiteracy, re-closing of marriages and sex.
This obviously wasn't a typical Fodor's publishing house.
In fact, it wasn't a portrait that everyone would appreciate.
"Zora said it the way it was," says ninety-year-old Ella Dinkins, one of Johnson's Girls choir, who made Hurston immortal by quoting men who sang vulgarous songs about their beauty.
"Some people didn't like it," she added.
Hurstone is still remembered as a chipper eccentricist who often came back after her family moved to Jacksonville, Florida.
Seventy-seven-year-old Augustus Franklin recalls that most of the time when she rushed into the city, she arrived unannounced in a huge Chevrolet, smoking and wearing pants in a city that is still proud of dignified dressing today.
Most of the residents were fascinated, Franklin said, but many sneered.
"The people of Zor have always been happy to see it," Franklin said.
But, he added, swinging onto a chair on the back terrace overlooking Lake Sabelia, where Hurstone was most likely baptized, "never stayed too long."
When Hurston died in 1960, she was poor and her books were eliminated from the press.
Eatonville, along with the rest of the world, seemed to have forgotten her.
Although she was once a literary star, Langston Hughes and the only black woman at Barnard College in the 1920s, she was buried in an unmarked grave in Fort Pierce, Florida, where she had previously lived.
There were no major mourning ceremonies or major public readings in Eatonville.
"I don't think they understood either its importance to the world or its legacy at all," said Valerie Boyd, author of Hurston's "Wrapped in Rainbows," published in 2003.
The change came in the 80's of the 20th century.
Orange County County officials wanted to stretch the city's five-lane highway instead of Kennedy Boulevard, a local two-lane main road.
The spreading of Orlando has already pushed interstate highway #4 through the western outskirts of the city.
The proposal came at a time when Eatonville was still coming out of a difficult historical period.
Forced integration, along with other things, has ended the relatively idyllic isolation of this community.
In the 1950s, the struggle to mix races brought hatred up to the threshold of the community.
"At that time, a bunch of white boys, they came and threw oranges and stuff at people sitting on the side," Franklin said.
"Actually, we had a lady who was killed.
They threw watermelon out of the car.”
In a 1955 letter to The Orlando Sentinel, Hurston challenged the supreme court's demand for forced integration, and of his decision in Brown's case against the Chamber for Education said it was "offensive for my race rather than glorifying."
Residents now claim that the desegregation of schools, even though it was positive in some areas, has diminished cohesion in Eatonville and undermined the courage of local youth.
"Black kids were used to hugs - I remember - you hugged your teacher in the morning, hugged your teacher in the evening," said N. Y. Nathiri, Ella Dinkins' daughter and executive director of the non-profit group Preserve the Eatonville Community (Saving the Eatonville Community).
It lasted as long as the teachers and pupils came from the same place, she added.
"You don't hug your white teacher because your white teacher - I want to say there's a cultural barrier," Ms. Nathiri said.
However, civil rights have helped create space for many other Zory Neale Hurstons - black writers, actors and artists who, like her, have risen above prejudice with reckless self-confidence and quotes such as "How can one deny the pleasure of my presence!
I won't understand."
In 1975, Alice Walker traveled to Hurston's unmarked grave and began fighting to revive her reputation.
Five years later, a vaunted biography of Hurston by Robert E. Hemenway, who reclassified her into an American canon, came to the shelves in bookstores.
The project with the highway arrived just as Eatonville's most famous daughter found the ramp lights again.
Hurston's former neighbors have now considered her a savior.
In order to show what can happen to its highway, the community began planning the Hurston Festival in 1988.
Thousands of fans arrived at the inaugural event two years later, and every January, many return to the celebration.
After several years, the state backed away from the highway proposal.
"Those five streams of highway resurrected, you'd say, you'd say it directly detonated, real civic pride," Ms Nathiri said.
Boyd said it more simply: "Zora saved Eatonville."
Victory over a highway project has helped change the city's image.
Out-of-the-city as Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who began going to church here in 1993, began to appear in greater numbers, and residents began rethinking the past.
Eatonville joined the national historical register in 1998.
The new one-storey library (named after Hurston, of course) was opened in 2006 on re-asphalted and beautified Kennedy Boulevard.
Today, Eatonville remains a Florida anomaly: just six miles from downtown Orlando can sometimes act like a sunken aisle in the summer rain, as small as when it was founded with only 27 black families in the 1980s.
(Today it is 90 percent black.)
Strangers who come looking for the story and significance of Eatonvill are still often treated with caution.
Permission for most conversations is necessary to have in advance and certain things - such as murals in the oldest Eatonville church, which have been painted by whites and which depict blacks in the fields - are not allowed to be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, such as Jones, a bold, confident teacher who likes deep red, still worry that their isolated community will not be properly understood.
And even as Hurston's festival went bigger, domestic pride has also increased.
Especially young people tend to see Eatonville in the way Hurston has seen her whole race: beautiful, with problems and so on, but not better, not worse and equally proud, creative, hard-working, foolish and diverse like other American races and ethnicities.
The voice of Franklin's nephew, eighteen-year-old Edwin Harvey, hears sincere love with the village when he plans to return to Eatonville after college to work in a local government or with the police, which he says would take some help.
And even the younger, such as the eleven-year-old twins from Alondra Winter Park and Alexia Kenonova, it seems, have learned to describe Eatonville correctly.
"Most of the people, when they're just passing through here, think, ♪ Oh, this town's nothing against the others,'" Alondra Kenon said last Sunday after the church.
"But if you really stop and look into history for a moment, it's a very nice city."
Here in New York, we're all fluent financial
At first glance, nothing has changed here.
If Manhattan tourists had stayed away from Newstades and huge TV screens in Times Square, they wouldn't have realized they were standing in the middle of a storm.
They would never have guessed that in a glass beehive of offices over their heads every day bankers and investment fund managers who monopolize New York have the worst "bad day in the office" since the 1929 crisis.
The messengers are still rumbling with metal carts along the sidewalks, transferring muffin pans to breakfast meetings.
Hispanics keep shifting between the towers and carrying, like human mules, fifty ironed shirts hanging from the scales on their backs.
Office rats are still waiting in line for their morning baguel by the street menus, and it's still not possible to get a yellow cab between four and seven in the afternoon on a weekday.
It didn't seem like a crisis at all, until, with all the talk of pessimism, I decided to open a savings account on Thursday afternoon.
Even though I timed this task to coincide with a late-afternoon siesta at my local branch of JP Morgan Chase, about twenty people were already waiting in the queue.
Not in front of the cash registers to pick up or deposit cash, but in front of the counter where older clients order chequebooks, tourists ask on their way to the Macy's convenience store, and new customers set up bills.
Most of the people in front of me, as I learned from Starr, my "licensed personal banker," came for the same reason as I did.
However, compared to me, they have already collected their savings from smaller banks in the places from which they arrived, such as New Jersey, Pennsylvania or Staten Island.
They collected money from so-called thrifts (a bit similar to building societies), cash associations, and from those types of savings banks whose branches operate in only one state, and which claim to look after their customers as a family.
In the past year, 15 such banks have quietly gone bankrupt.
A few days ago, Washington Mutual, a $327-billion-dollar asset bank, went into bankruptcy, and when New Yorkers were returning home for the weekend on Friday night, another bank, Wachovia, with assets worth £440bn, was trying to catch a bailout buyer.
Employees of offices and shops who stood in front of me and feared for another collapsion moved their cash to a bank that is perceived as a safe haven and which makes most of the bailout on Wall Street.
Ordinary New Yorkers have fluent in the past week.
Some woman at my local Chinese supermarket was talking on the phone and speaking to Lehman Borthers, that bankrupt bank, and her skinny chief executive Dick Fuld with such familiality as if she were gossiping about a lazy colleague.
And Lyn, my tenement's roughly fifty-year-old housekeeper, changed the topic of our ordinary conversation - Brad, Angelina or my grocery delivery.
After my visit at the bank, she told me, "I've got my savings in money market funds now.
“I’ve moved it, I won’t take any chances.”
Muslim graves desecrated after Austrian right-wing break
Heinz-Christian Strache, on the left, from Austria's right-winning Freedom Party and Filip Dewinter of Belgium's right-wing Vlaams Belang party, is taking part in a demonstration presented in Antwerp on Thursday, January 17, 2008 by "Cities Against Islamization."
They carry signs similar to traffic signs that show mosques crossed by a red line.
Police accuse right-wing extremists of desecrating a Muslim cemetery in Austria at the same weekend in which extreme right-wing parties made huge gains in the general election.
More than 90 graves in a graveyard in the town of Traun near Linz were severely damaged sometime between Friday evenings and this morning, and the police are convinced that it was an organized event.
Culprits spray-painted Jewish symbols such as David's star on some graves, but detectives are convinced that it may have been an attempt to mask the motives of extremists led by hatred of Muslim immigrants.
It is not clear whether this incident was in any way linked to the elections.
A spokesperson for the Islamic community said they were "deeply shocked" by news of desecration, which comes as Ramadan's Musmic Lent moon draws to an end.
Austria, meanwhile, is stepping into a round of searching for one's own soul after it swung to the right in yesterday's parliamentary elections.
The two far-right parties, which received more than 30 percent of the vote combined, the Freedom Party and the Union for the Future of Austria, campaigned vehemently anti-immigration, and some of their slogans were condemned by critics as xenophobic.
Heinz-Christian Strache, the leader of the Freedom Party, which won over 18 percent of the vote, protested against headscarves and burqas in the campaign, and even expressed his opposition to the dishes in which the relationship to Islam is perceived.
At his last meeting with voters in Vienna, he spoke of a "European fraternity" designed to prevent the rise of Islam.
Both sides are trying to ban the construction of mosques and minarets, arguing that these are political symbols of the ongoing Islamization of Austria and Europe.
Polls and analyses carried out immediately after the elections, which put the extreme right as the strongest political bloc in the country, suggest that this change was caused primarily by young voters worried about their future in the European Union.
Gary Glitter is not allowed on a trip abroad
Gary Glitter, a glam-rock star who was convicted of sexually abusing children, was banned by the court from travelling to France and Spain.
Officials imposed this six-month ban on Glitter after informing monitoring authorities that he planned to travel through France to Spain.
Kenta's police were approved last Thursday after handing over evidence of Glitter's past sentences for child sexual abuse to officials.
Glitter, real name Paul Gadd, did not take part in the hearing at Ashford's lowest criminal court in Kent, but was represented by Corker Binning lawyers.
A court spokesman said: "On Thursday, September 25 at Ashford's lowest-ranking criminal court, Kentan police asked for regulations that would have prevented Mr Paul Gadd from crossing the border into France and Spain until March 25.
Paul Gadd didn't attend personally.'
The 64-year-old Glitter informed authorities that he wished to travel abroad, as required by his record in the register of sexual assailants.
He has to notify the authorities if he wants to travel abroad for more than three days.
They also have to hand over the scheduled departure date to the police a week in advance.
If he were to omit the obligation to inform the authorities, he could be sentenced to five years.
Kent police have announced that they will not be discussing individual cases of Glitter's possible trips around the county.
A spokeswoman for the Kentan police said: "The Kentan police will do everything necessary to protect children in this country or abroad.
From our point of view, it's easier to track someone who stays in Britain because we have large-scale systems that handle potential hazards more effectively.
Protecting Kent residents is a top priority for the Kent police, and we are working carefully to monitor the behaviour of recorded sex attackers in order to reduce any danger to the public to a minimum.”
Glitter returned to Britain last month after spending 27 months in a Vietnamese prison for abusing two girls aged 10 and 11.
When he was released, he flew to Bangkok, where he was scheduled to embark on a flight to London.
But he said he wasn't feeling well, and refused to fly to London.
Thai authorities didn't let him pass passport control and eventually flew to Hong Kong.
Chinese authorities also refused to allow him to enter and he flew back to Bangkok, where Thai police and immigration officials insisted on his return to Britain.
On August 22, he finally arrived at Heathrow Terminal 3 and remained there, while his attorney David Corker went to Uxbridg's lowest criminal court in west London, and at the same time Scotland Yard successfully demanded an order ordered him to register as a sex attacker.
Glitter has previously been imprisoned in the United Kingdom for four months for possessing child pornography.
An Interior Ministry source said Jacqui Smith, the interior minister, was calm and glad Glitter was prevented from travelling to France and Spain.
"To capture the real character, I photograph the master off the scene." Günther Bauer - photographer
No heels, no ruffles or costumes.
Only the essences of the great flamenco masters, detached from the focus on interpretation - I will just achieve the relaxed expression of a man or woman.
On the one hand, an artist.
On the other - man.
Inseparable, apart from privacy, in those moments away from the audience - at that time you can see an intimate gesture, the depth of a glimpse, or the passionate gesticulation of hands, which are carefree, because they are not about to appear on the scene.
The German Günther Bauer alternates in "Flamenco-people" the theatricality of performances with singers, dancers and guitarists with the lesser-known face of the masters.
With this intention, the photographer compiles a unique iconcography of flamenco from different backgrounds.
The exhibition, which takes place as part of Flamenca's Mes de la Fotografía Flamenca program, "reflectes the ever-increasing international importance of flamenco," the author says, explaining that "Flamenco-people" "may be any kind of people, but when you look at the photos, you'll find they're not typical pictures of artists on the scene.
These people, portrayed "on the street," are artists able to bring passion, sadness, joy to the scene... and completely captivate the audience.
There is no need to take pictures on stage to capture the personality of these artists.
They carry their personality and character inside.
I was interested in creating a relaxed atmosphere that will allow me to capture the personality of flamenco giants.
Enchanted by "Cante de las Minas," Bauer began a long journey in 1997 to portray flamenco singers, guitarists and dancers on a board camera.
They are portraits that, thanks to the choice of camera and material, resemble classical photography.
The application of different stylistic means is contrary to the classical basis of the film and lends it to the contemporary spirit, which is an idea that, according to Bauer himself, mirrors flamenco: "Music with very traditional roots, which, however, continues to surprise and evolve."
What draws the viewers is not the action of the dance itself, singing or play, but the personality and character of the artist.
The search for the moment when we meet with the most important one, with that spirit that appears in a way of perceiving, being and experiencing, it has been and is a constant of the work presented during these days in the El Fotómata gallery.
This selection of nearly seventy photographed artists is shaped by well-known flamenco characters such as Diego El Cigala, Chano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales and Vicente Amigo.
The project has become a kind of anthology of flamenco and will soon get a booking edition, which will contain exhibited portraits.
Two years ago, Bauera's passion for portraiture led to the idea of repeating "Portraits of a Sports Veteran" ("SportARTproject").
Although their concept is different from "Flamenco-people", the photographer admits that "photographing people, getting to know them and having the opportunity for a moment, whether longer or just brief, to enter their lives - that's my passion.
I think that in photography the portrait is the most difficult genre.
A person meets or encounters people, personalities he sometimes doesn’t even know, or gives him only a few minutes to shoot.
In spite of similar circumstances as a photographer, I always try to realize my ideas.”
A collection of portraits of contemporary flamenco entitled "Flamenco-people" is on display at the El Fotómata Gallery in de la Mata Street 20, parallel to Alameda de Hércules, and can be visited until October 16.
Vargas Llosa has acknowledged that the novel he writes is "presumption"
It takes place in an environment he doesn’t yet know and tells about events unknown to him, yet Mario Vargas Llosa intends to successfully realize his new project – he’s just working on a novel whose main character is Roger Casemant, an Irish nationalist who has lived in Congo for twenty years and traveled in the Amazon.
An eternal favorite of the Nobel Prize in Literature confessed yesterday at the Hay Festival Segovia that a project that just employs him "is bold" and that "everything suggests I shouldn't write" this novel.
"If I had sense, I wouldn't do it, but I'm going to do it, and I'm going to finish it someday," he concluded his post at a festival of exchanging ideas, ideas and words, which within four days saw an aqueduct around a hundred writers, historians and journalists and thousands of other people.
The author of the novel "City and Dogs" was one of the main characters of the third year's festivities of the word and fulfilled expectations.
Vargas Llosa, who won the Hay Festival award for most popular writer - the old edition of Dickens' "Our Mutual Friend" - filled Juan Bravo theatre to the final place.
With Félix Rome, who introduced him, he had an audience handed in front of him, to whom an hour of time had passed like water - a Peruvian with amazing precision and a considerable amount of humour reminisced in him, how from the original "unbelievable adventure" from the "pleasure of a passionate reader" and from "the power to live more than what I lived" was a literary mission
The Hispano-American novelist admitted that "there is no way" to find out whether and to what extent books can change their lives, "but I refuse to believe that literature is a mere entertainment, a fleeting experience" - Vargas Llosa emphasized that, according to him, "it affects people" and "forces them to try out of line," and urged writers not to "succumb to frivolity"
Because of climatology, he's losing a festival in Pozoblanc - there's no spear
The performance with the spears, which concludes the festival in Pozoblanc, was canceled yesterday morning after the performer's announcement was confirmed that the surface of the square was not in good condition.
That's why the company, along with Fermín Bohórquez, Pablo Hermosa de Mendoza and Leonard Hernández, decided to cancel the bullfighting.
Unfavorable meteorological conditions have plagued this celebration for a long time, two out of three celebrations could no longer take place.
On Saturday, the rain prevented the course of second planned bullfights.
José Luis Moreno, Curro Díaz and Juan Bautista were supposed to be built by bulls in an arena on Castilblanc.
That's not just how it was celebrated on Fridays.
The winner was Daniel Luque, who cut off three ears and the reward for a dynamic and aesthetically high-quality performance was carried out on his shoulders.
On the same day in the afternoon, Alejandro Talavante exchanged two spear wounds for greetings and one ear, which were brought to him by uncovered seats in the arena rather than of Toreador art.
Even worse was El Juli, who walked through the bullry arena Los Llanos ungreasy.
In the second round of toil without a single respite, he earned sharp criticism from most of the audience.
They escaped control in Álava in a van with two thousand pounds of hash
In ten minutes five.
Ertzaintza road inspection in the Armiñón (Álava) district.
The members stop two people riding in the van and they miss them.
The load, more than two tons of hashish, is not to be stopped.
According to a report by the Department of the Interior of the Basque government, members persecuted and detained one of those people riding in a van, a man aged 38, who was identified as A. A. J.
The detainee was charged with drug smuggling and falsifying state documents.
The incident occurred when several members of the Transport Unit were conducting a roadside inspection on the N-1 in places where it passes through the border of the Armiññón municipal district.
They were about to stop the van for inspection, but the driver disobeyed the instructions, accelerated sharply and drove away.
The members immediately began to prosecute the car until the vehicle stopped at the side of the road a few kilometers away, at which point the two passengers jumped up and fled into the forested vegetation.
Ertzaintza members eventually chased one of them and seized 2 178 kilograms of hashish, which the suspects were transporting, as it turned out during a tour of Iveco Daily.
The rear part of the vehicle was loaded with bundles of hashish, which were subsequently transported to other police departments for detailed expertise and drug weighing.
In addition, the police found that the license plate is "wrinkled" and does not agree with the data provided in the documents of the delivery, so the detainee was also accused of falsifying state documents.
Ertzaintza continues his search for the second suspect, who, as has already been mentioned, managed to flee in order to finally determine the origin of the seized drug.
Detainee A. A. J., 28 years old, was transferred to the police department to deal with appropriate formalities before proceeding to another investigation.
Actress Heather Locklear detained for driving under the influence of medication
Actress Heather Locklear, the popular Amanda of the Melrose Place series, was detained in Santa Barbara (California) this weekend for driving under the influence of medication.
The witness saw her perform unusual maneuvers as she tried to pull out of the parking lot in Montecito, a California road patrol spokesman told People magazine.
At around 16.30 p.m. Locklear "treaded sharply on the gas, driving forward and backwards in a noisy roar, trying to get out of the parking space - when reversing, she ran over her sunglasses several times."
The witness, who apparently didn't recognize the actress at first, saw Locklear stopping on a nearby highway and getting out of the vehicle.
The person also alerted the emergency services because they "were worried about Lockleare's life."
When a police patrol arrived at the scene, they found the actress inside the vehicle, which partially blocked the road.
"She looked disoriented," and that's why members took her to a specialized drug and alcoholic center to undergo a test.
According to a police spokeswoman, the actress cooperated and excessive amounts of alcohol were immediately excluded, yet "we believe Locklear was under the influence of some drugs, which was obvious to officers at first sight."
Locklear was detained on suspicion of driving under the influence of unspecified substances and was taken into custody around 19.00, although she had already been released hours later.
Two months ago, Locklear returned from a treatment clinic where she was recovering from anxiety conditions and depression.
Llamazares urges Andalusian association IU to exercise its powers in the Silva case
IU's chief of staff and Member for the United Left, Gaspar Llamazares, said the final decision in the case of Seville's former youth and sports councillor, Francisco Manuel Silva, who resigned from office last week after beinginning for action in favor of his family, would depend on the party's regional association.
"I'm too federalist for it, it's up to the Andalusian organization to exercise its powers," Llamazares hinted at the negotiations, to which the party will have to proceed in relation to a former member of the Seville City Council.
However, the IU chief insisted that, in the event of an investigation into the illegal conduct committed by Silva, "transparency" is absolutely necessary: "The IU has always fought to ensure that such matters are as transparent as possible."
Likewise, he stressed that the party has a "strict code of ethics for all public functions."
"May blood" ("Sangre de mayo"), Garci's epic bet
José Luis Garcī in "Sangre de mayo", inspired by Episodes from Benito Pérez Galdó's national past, revives the famous uprising of the Spaniards against the French - this is the most ambitious film in his filmography with a budget of around 15 million euros.
In this way, the filmmaker wanted to "draw the eloquency of the characters and the analysis of the company", which he always admired in the work of the famous novelist, he explained today, along with Quimo Gutiérrez and Paula Echevarría, during the presentation of the film in front of an extensive team of performers.
Mafflower blood, with a measure of over two-and-a-half hours, will be introduced to cinemas this Friday, and a more than four-hour series for Telemadrid television is planned in the future.
As a great admirer of Galdós’ literary work, José Luis Garci claims to have fulfilled a “children’s dream” by making this movie, for which Esperanza Aguirre, who three years again commissioned him three years againg Autonomic Province of Madrid to make a film on the two hundredth anniversary of Madrid, he accepted the offer, saying that the plot would be based on Episodes of the national past.
The £15 million budget, to which the province of Madrida has completely contributed, represents a "unique opportunity" for Garcio to create an environment with backdrops and costumes corresponding to the image of "thriving Spain, which was mistaken in the leader for whom it went to fight - the struggle for independence led to the decline and backwardness of the country during the reign of Ferdinand VII," he said.
However, the director regrets that he cannot count on the financial support that has been given to the films The Sad Wing (Ala triste) or Goya's Ghosts - almost double of Flower Blood - so he could not film some of the scenes from the script signed at the same time by Horacio Valcárcel, with whom he works almost throughout his film career.
The director of "Start Again" (Volver and Empezzar) works the way he is usually used to - on the stage together with the actors, with whom he usually tries before filming and with whom he does not choose casting, because he finds it "humiliating" and also believes that "part of the director's profession is, among other things, to go to theaters and cinemas and so learn what's going on in the craft".
Many of the actors he has chosen for Flower Blood have been appearing in front of his camera for several times, such as Miguel Rellán, Carlos Larrañaga and Fernando Guillén Cuervo.
Garci, with four Oskar nominations, one of whom he received, is of the opinion that none of the three candidates representing Spain this year before the Hollywood Academy, the Flower Blood itself, The Seven Tables of the French Biliare, and the last selected Blind Sunshite, represents a young generation of the Spanish film scene, but claims that "it is yet to appear."
There are no awards for this film Garci.
“At first, you think you’re working for what happens next, but then you realize you’re only working for earnings, so you’re going to make one more movie,” Garci said.
Isabel Bayón revives the spiritual legacy of Seville dancer Tórtola Valencia
Seville flamenco dancer pays tribute to another Seville dancer.
Carmen Tórtola Valencia, from the father of the Catalan (Florenç Tórtola Ferrer) and Andalusianka's mother (Georgina Valencia Valenzuela), was born in the Triana district, and when she was three years old, they moved with her family to London.
She danced modern dance and focused on oriental dances.
She lived a life full of passion, pioneering female emancipation such as Isadora Duncan, Virginia Woolf or Sarah Bernhardt.
She was a Buddhist and a vegetarian and fought for the abolition of a corset that made it impossible for women to move freely.
The life of this woman, influenced by the dance and aesthetic revolution of interwar Europe, enchanted Isabel Bayón.
"I wanted to work with Pepa Gamboa, and she designed this character for me - it seemed unbelievable."
According to the dancer, "we're not interested in narrating her life, it's about grasping her soul.
The names of her dance performances reminded me of flamenco rhythms.
So "butterfly dance" evoked my guajira or "snake dance" some thong.
Her dance was sensual and they told me that my dance of this aesthetic also corresponds," she argues.
The performance envisages the involvement of two exceptional personalities: with Miguel Podé, which Isabele will sing in one performance just like in "Open Doors" (La puerta abierta), and with dance teacher Matilde Coral, who made an exception and accepted cooperation with her former pupil.
"Matilde represents the older Tórtola, a ripe figure.
I'm happy she's accepted an offer to participate in my project, and it's a great honour for me.'
In the performance he also counts on the music and guitars of Jesús Torres and Paco Arriaga, the singing of Miguel Ortega, el Pulga and Moi de Morón; the drums of El PÁjar, the viola of Rafa El Viola, and the collaboration of the actor Juanfra JuÁrez, "who has the role of an eternal guide of this kind of artists, who are always in love".
The work also takes into account the life of this woman, which took place for his time in such distant places as India, the United States or Russia, and also throughout Europe, because she usually resided in London.
"I wanted to remind her because I think she was one of those women who have a huge influence at some time, but today no one knows her and even less appreciates her work," Bayón says.
The performance begins today at 20.30 at the Lope de Vegy Theatre.
"Diary of the Newly Married" also closes the triennial
The introduction of the first illustrated edition of "Diary of the Fresh Married" in Saint Stephen's Church (New York), where Juan Ramón Jiménez married Zenobia in March 1916, is part of a program coordinated by the Cultural Council of the Autonomous Andalusian Government to celebrate "Trienale of Zenobia and Juan Ramón" (2006-2008), organized to celebrate the 50th anniversary
"Triennial of Zenobie and Juan Ramón" is coming to an end with a new edition of this work, an international congress to be held in New York, the repair of the Nobel Museum House, and the traveling exhibition "Juan Ramón Jiménez: The One Ray of the Sun", which opened in May of this year, and, after circumventing various Andalusian provinces, it should, according to the schedule.
The Autonomous Andalusian Government's Cultural Council has also commissioned eight hundred copies of "Diary of the Freshly Married" edited by Pedr Taberner and illustrated by Jacob Pérez-Encis to continue spreading awareness of the personality and work of one of Andalusia's most worldly poets.
America doesn't like itself so much without Paul Newman
"Sometimes God creates perfect people.
"Paul Newman was one of them."
These are the words of actress Sally Field, who collaborated with him on "Without Bad Intention" and, under the weight that Newman had left, reveals his dismal vision of abandoned American cinema and all of America.
Look how it took them time to get an Oscar.
And he's been retired since 2007.
And it was known that he was seriously ill.
Still, he was a man for everyone.
"Sometimes what a person feels can't be expressed in any way, and I've lost a great friend right now," Robert Redford said in tears, "my life and my country were better because of it."
No one was afraid that it would be ridiculous or exaggerated.
The father of film and homeland has died in a particularly dark time of fear of terrorists and financial markets, at a time when American self-confidence is at the bottom, desperately seeking reasons for pride.
When the times are rough and people without virtues and only a handful of those worth your admiration - there's no need to be Luis Cernuda to love Paul Newman.
For all, he embodied an almost platonic ideal of beauty and truth.
He was an official opponent of Richard Nixon and a fighter for both losing and winning cases, ranging from an anti-drug addiction foundation based in honor of his first-born son, who died of overdoses, to his own brand of homemade food and organic food.
A complete treasure for a country that cost a lot of effort to free yourself from suicidal eating culture.
The tumultuous wave of admiration has flooded Hollywood regardless of nationality or nationality.
The last James Bond, Englishman Daniel Craig, stated that not one man died, but a whole era.
Another Englishman, Oskar's holder for directing Sam Mendes, said working with Newman was without exaggeration the pinnacle of his career.
An evil boy from Australia, Russell Crowe, dared to label Newman as his greatest source of inspiration.
"The fox has set a pretty high bar for all of us," observes George Clooney, perhaps one of the few, who nowadays has the courage to try to live like Paul Newman lived - he prides himself on honesty and leads a serious private life away from the cameras.
It's not always easy in today's media jungle, sometimes even possible.
But luckily, that's how people are, and Paul Newman is their role model.
He divorced once, and has since been married for fifty years with Joanne Woodward, to whom he was faithful, even though he certainly had more than enough opportunities.
"Why would I go out for a hamburger when I've got a fillet at home?" he liked to joke.
Lest pride accompanies him in heaven that there is harmony on earth between his daughters and admirers - it is not very common in America.
“Of all the great roles he embodied, he was most proud of the role of a devoted husband, a loving father, a willing grandfather, and a self-sacrificing philanthropist.”
Imperfect races of Olympic Madrid
Yes, many of us are eager for the Olympics to take place in Madrid, and after a failed year 2012 it will be maybe 2016.
But I ask: are we ready?
If we're going on to last night's race, I'm saying: no!
He was referred to as a perfect race, and in my opinion he was the worst I've ever been on.
Such a bunch of nonsense that right away, if it came down to an assessment and I was an examiner, it would be clear insufficient.
But let's look at it from the beginning, nice one by one.
Nonsense begins with picking up the racing number, because of which one has to leave early.
That doesn't seem bad to me, but when you're going there, let them give him everything (including a chip that's supposed to avoid overcrowding the track).
Oh, no.
Here you had to pick up the number first (the first nonsense, go there only because of it) and (after standing the appropriate queue) - what a surprise! - had only a T-shirt size S (the second nonsense, because I need size XL).
Where are we living it?
Because of the crisis, they decided to buy only small T-shirts, because it is less fabric and they are cheaper?
Well, those are, of course, little things compared to what happened on the day of the race.
In addition to having to go for the chip very soon and the queue lasted more than 15 minutes, he still had to surrender his backpack there.
For over 35 minutes, the waiting time lasted, something incomprehensible at a race where 11,000 people are expected.
So, as expected, the race started and we didn't put down our backpacks yet, and when it did, it was much worse with the pick-up.
In addition, the wait was extended by 40 minutes by rain, which led to a general revolt and an attack on the cabin.
I believe that 500 of us will have bronchitism tomorrow and some, like me, will not see the right time in evaluating the results because of the chip they gave me and which, as I suppose, did not correctly record the start time.
Of course, if someone asked me for a perfect race, I wouldn't think of one.
And so I think we'll just have to dream about a well-prepared Olympic city.
Microsoft is looking for a receptionist (virtual)
At a conference on new MIT technologies, Microsoft demonstrated further developments in a new image recognition system and human interaction on artificial intelligence - not very intelligent, but able to perform basic tasks such as summoning taxis (or, in the case of Microsoft, a messer who would move all over the company's premises) and perceive what he hears in the interview.
Thanks to a camera and system that recognizes shapes and faces, this "receptor" can find out how many people are in the room, who they are - even remember what they're wearing - and listen and understand the keywords of their conversations.
It can also sort those present according to conversation groups.
In the demonstration video, the receptionist records a third person and understands that he does not belong to the group that talks to her, and that he is not an employee of the company.
He asks her if she's going to see someone and tells her she'll take care of her in a moment.
All this without the blue screen of death.
Stunning.
Of course, this is the music of the future.
Now the company has more important things to do, for example, a new version of the Windows operating system.
There's been a lot of talk this week about changing Microsoft's strategy.
Windows 7 will be lighter and won't have as many associated programs.
Steve Ballmer found that if computers had only the most necessary things, without the burden of commercial uselessness, they would be five times more powerful - man, Steve, they'd be cheaper.
Discovering like thunder when we consider Microsoft adds new features and more "extras" to Windows since the version.
Changing direction would be a meritorious act.
Microsoft is right, the operating system is slowly losing its importance, and so is the software.
When Google Chrome appeared on the network, I became one of those inclined towards the theory that it was Google's first step towards competing with Windows.
Many have rightly pointed out that the browser is not an operating system.
The operating system will always be necessary, because it is a necessary layer to communicate between hardware and software.
It has the same function as your wife or your husband when you have to say something to your mother-in-law.
That's the truth.
It will never disappear, but it is necessary to think of the "death" of the operating system not as an absolute truth, but a relative truth.
What Ballmer said this week confirms the idea.
People are paying less and less attention to the operating system and, on the contrary, to the Internet browser.
The computer turns on to launch Firefox or Explorer - or Chrome - and we spend more time on the Internet every day than around the windows.
What is the purpose of half of the applications that accompany the system?
There's less more.
And Windows 7 has to be less, much less than Vista.
Less heavy, less loaded, less complex.
Microsoft is now interested in offering its services online and finally becoming an alternative to Google.
They're desperately needing it, and here's a small example.
I took this photo in one rest room for Microsoft employees in Redmond at the beginning of the month.
There was a poster on the beverage vending machine asking them to use their search tools: "Your search. Our future."
In 2006, an independent study showed that 66% of Microsoft's employees used Google to search the Internet.
Last week, by the way, confirmed the delay of the new version of Windows Mobile 7.
It won't come at the beginning of 2009, but at the end, let's count on 2010.
At Microsoft, they will definitely try to launch Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7 together.
The phone is becoming more and more computers.
Since Android and iPhone are already on the market, they will have to pay dearly for the delay.
Le Fabuleux Marcel
Pablo Picasso wore it on the Côte d'øield, Leonardo di Caprio in one glow in Edward Zwick's "Bloody Diamond," as well as Al Pacino in Francis Ford Coppola's "Godfather," plus it's part of the uniform of a muscular gay community.
And if we want to enter the list of his life roles, we can immerse ourselves in the image of a typical muscular man with a pointed mustache that lifts dumbbells in a nomadic circus.
Basically, it is nothing more than a typical cotton men's bottom tank top, snug and sleeveless.
English spokespersons call him 'tank', but le Fabuleux Marcel from Brussels stands out above everyone else.
First of all, because of its crippling name - albeit popularly called 'Marcel' - which is related to the sport because, as the legend tells it, it was named after boxer Marcel Cerdan, the lover of singer Edith Piaf.
'Marcel' stands out above other high-quality cotton, unisex cut, distinguishing label and (almost unnoticeable) Belgian flag, which models have the most emblematic tank tops as detail at the neck.
And beware, because the Belgian company does not specialize only in tank tops.
It also produces college sweatshirts, retro boxers, cashmere sweaters, cotton pants, nautical T-shirts, children's clothes, mini shorts...
Sophisticated pieces for comfortable underwear.
Although it's a shame that they're just underwear.
They're too beautiful.
“I want to be a young mother. Whoever will be a father doesn’t worry me.”
The great appearance with her sister did not prevent her from developing her own personality.
Mónica Cruz, though tiny figures, is a woman full of life.
They now take their new tenure in fashion very seriously.
For Mónica, this means so much more than just that some pretty, famous girls lend their name.
In Paris, she participated in a graduation event organized by Mango at its new headquarters, as well as the announcement of most important fashion prizes, which the Spanish company sponsors.
She's cordial and timid, and she'll get you up close.
Tell us what you're doing in Paris.
I came to present a new collection, which Sister Penélope and I designed for Mango for this autumn-winter season, and at the same time I participated in the announcement of the second annual "Mango Fashion Awards" and dinner at the Opera in honor of Valentino.
It has been an exciting and exciting day.
What is the basis of your new collection for Mango, for which you have been working for the fourth season?
I and my sister love movies from the 50’s and 60’s and we drew a lot of stimuli from them.
I also love wandering around the city and watching women walk dressed, how they combine and layer their clothes, I do this very often.
You're a dancer, an actress, a designer, a very versatile woman, aren't you?
I think so, but after all, everything is interconnected and has many connections.
For example, since I was a kid, I still wanted to go to my grandma's, go through my aunt's closets and look at her clothes.
It was a sensation, almost better than going on a pilgrimage.
When I became a dancer, I always tried to take care of my clothes.
You know that we dancers pride ourselves on how groomed we are, so that we feel good about our lessons and rehearsals when looking in the mirror.
And as an actress, when they give you a costume, that's when you're really into the character.
So everything is connected, but with great respect for designers and professionalism.
You're 30 recently, you're affected by a crisis?
No, no.
I'm feeling better than ever before, I'll enjoy my 30s.
I find that a woman is already finished, has experience and enjoys life with fewer mistakes.
Biological clock doesn't scare me.
I've been doing what I want since I was a kid, and that gives me great inner happiness.
So you're thinking about being a mother, aren't you?
Of course, I know I'm going to be a mother because my body demands it, and besides that, I want to be a young mum, I don't want to miss it.
When it comes to fathers, I don't worry about that.
But one thing I know for sure, I will give my child a feeling of happiness and I will raise him with great love.
When it comes to a movie, you've got a few premieres of the films you made last year.
That's right, and I'm looking forward to them coming to cinemas.
I come from the theatrical world, so I'm very impatient and restless, I want everything right now and that's obviously not the case.
I'm looking forward to the premiere of "La última hora" and "En busca de la tumba de Cristo".
Let's see how you're going to like...
A few days ago, you've had your premiere on the catwalk alongside Naomi Campbell, right?
Well, don't say that.
It's not like I'm going to stand on the pier as a model.
I just got a call from Naomi Campbell, whom I've known for a long time, and she invited me to London to take part in a benefit parade for her "Women for a Better Life" organization, and the clothes we've been showcasing - I've also chosen two models from our Mango collection - has been auctioned off and the proceeds have been handed over to this NGO.
People don't know Naomi has such a good heart.
There's a lot of things talking about her, and because I know her, I can tell you that many of them aren't true.
In addition, I'll stick to the good he's involved.
She's asked me to perform for charitables, but when she asks me to do a handstand, I'll do it, too.
In favor of third-world women, I'll do whatever it takes.
Drivers repeatedly caught with alcohol in their blood will have to install "alcolock"
The Ministry of the Interior demands that a driver with three administrative penalties for driving under the influence of alcohol has to install a system in his car that prevents him from starting the car while drunk or if he does not breathe.
The proposal became known as early as June this year and is now included in the annual report of the Supreme Public Prosecutor’s Office, according to which it is a pilot project that will last one year.
His goal is to return the driver behind the wheel, which is why drivers will also have to take a re-education course.
In addition to the National Transport Safety Authority, the General Directorate of Transport (DGT) and the Foundation of the Institute of Technology for Car Safety (FITSA) will participate in this experience.
According to statistics, between 30 and 75 percent of those charged more than once while driving while intoxicate will get behind the wheel again.
Alcolock should prevent this, as it is a system connected to a vehicle that measures the level of alcohol in exhaled air and prevents the start of the car if the driver does not breathe earlier or if a higher than the permitted concentration of alcohol is recorded.
Its price exceeds 1 000 euros and it would have to be paid by the culprit himself.
A study carried out to this effect in the U.S. in 2007 showed that vehicles equipped with this device reduced violations by 65% during the first year since its introduction.
In Spain, 115 people could save their lives each year.
The measure is confined to administrative sanctions for two reasons: in order to address this behaviour before it results in a crime.
And in order to facilitate its introduction, because the legislation can be implemented in the usual way, but the necessary regulation of criminal provisions requires a change in the law.
The equipment is not yet available on the Spanish market in any sales version, although it has already been rehearsally installed in some industrial and public service vehicles.
Just tomorrow, the Congress of MPs will vote on a PSOE (Spanish Workers' Socialist Party) proposal, in which it is recommended to install this system in school buses, as is the case in France.
In Mexico and without "peeptoes"
