Stars under the stellar skies of Paris.
Oh là, c'est la vie.
1001 - anti-stress number
Not 1000, but 1000 plus one way to forget the messy and frenzied daily life.
And to make matters worse, all are summarized in one book, which on September 30 will bring relaxation to the bookstore.
The alarm clock rings every morning at 7 p.m. as punishment, but it's even worse to imagine yourself engulfed in the hasty rhythm brought by contemporary life.
The office is called tension, and the computer has back pain.
Stress is a 21st-century disease.
However, there is neither one nor two methods to combat daily exhaustion and the problems of detaching yourself from the world of work.
No, that number isn't any bigger and no less than 1001.
In addition to yogic positions, classical massage and "spa" therapy on the pages of her book, Susannah Marriott has collected a whole "menu" of options, adjusted according to each individual's tastes and temporal options.
The anti-stress book is titled "1001 method of relaxation" and thanks to Pearson Alhambra and DK will bring peace to the bookshop.
If someone considers that it is not possible to achieve peace in areas such as work or personal relationships, the "handbook" denies this view.
In addition, it offers other procedures how to be in contact with nature or 100% natural recipes that contribute to relaxation.
If you don’t have adrenaline in your veins, which restores balance in your body, and stress has become a problem, it’s time to sit on the couch, shut down and focus on reading.
Speaking from Sleep
Some kids talk at night.
I don't mean those who wake up and tell us something that happened the day before, but children who talk while they're sleeping.
When this happens for the first time, you might be startled, but it's actually meaningless.
It is a harmless sleep disorder called somnilokvia.
It is quite common, especially for children and adolescents.
I'm surely one of you've been told that he was talking in his sleep at night.
This phenomenon can occur very sporadically (for example, once in a lifetime in high fevers), or with greater regularity (every day, once a week, once every few months...), the next day the child does not remember anything.
There's not just one reason to explain her rise.
It is often associated with other sleep disorders, such as nightmares, woes, enuresis nocturna (night wetting) or sleepwalking, but this may not be the case.
As previously mentioned, it can often be accompanied by very high fevers, stressful or inflamed situations.
In any case, this is a little explored phenomenon.
The intensity with which it appears is also different.
The child can speak in a calm voice, say individual words, whisper, or quite the contrary (cries, crying, long speeches...).
In cases where it manifests itself with great strength, it would be advisable to observe and consult with a specialist to find out if he has any problem, which provokes a lot of anxiety and manifests itself in this way.
We have said that this is a harmless disorder, so it is not treated only if it is associated with another sleep disorder, which can be treated (for example, if it is associated with apnea syndrome or with permanent stress).
However, we can do this by keeping in mind some very simple preventive measures, such as:
Try not to upset the child too much before going to bed.
Therefore, search for quiet games and avoid scary or somewhat violent television programs or series.
For very nervous children and those who will be able to relax, it is possible to use relaxation techniques that will help them to get a certain inner peace.
This will allow the child to sleep more peacefully.
When somnilokvia appears, try to keep calm.
Remind yourself that it is harmless to the child and that from what happened, the next day he will no longer remember anything.
Useful advice
Don't be him.
It's useless because he's not conscious, and you're interrupting his sleep.
Even though he speaks, he sleeps, and therefore rests.
Remember that if it seems to you several times in a row that they are very troubled, it would be advisable to turn for advice or find out what provokes such anxiety in a child.
If he understands what he is saying (which is not so usual), avoid the temptation to interrogate him and draw information from it.
It won't do anything, because when he's asleep, he's not conscious to organize his thoughts.
Moreover, you will hear what he is saying, but you do not know what he is dreaming about, and therefore why he says it, so be very careful how you interpret the information and what conclusions you come to.
Never tell a child that you can ask him about his secrets at night.
Not even for fun.
This provokes a lot of anxiety.
Don’t worry, it’s nothing wrong, it’s just an episode that lasts only a few minutes.
Illhaeusern... finally
Illhaeusern finally found his way to victory again after a long skinny period.
Without playing a particularly big match, the yolk-bearers created more chances and managed to change one of them victoriously.
The game did not have too fast a pace during this encounter, in which the attackers had a hard time asserting themselves.
After the captain's first attempt, Kauffmann, whose a shot from a distance only by a hair missed the junction, Illhaeusern began to have a slightly upper hand, and first Fonderflick from a distance, then Hirne through the air, Romain for a long leg or Diebold gradually came to irritate Dannemarie's defenses.
The opponent, on the other hand, built a defensive roll halfway through the pitch, leaving only the agile Riahi on the tip of attack.
The beginning of the second half proved decisive, first thanks to Dannemarie's two chances with the names Riahi and Temporale, then after opening the score for Illhaeusern, taken care of by Marais, well positioned to process the ball deflected after a previous Diebold attempt (53').
Illhaeusern's goalkeeper, however, didn't enjoy the joy for long, after about twelve minutes he was forced to return to the dressing room and leave his teammates weakened.
Dannemarie, however, could not get out of it, and the 11th from Illhaeusern, who had just withdrawn a little to "thicken" the defense, did not cause any more trouble, except for the very final seconds of the match, when the ball flew very close to the beam after great confusion.
Sarkozy goes towards angry fishermen
Before flying to the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy takes a detour through Brittany.
In order to face a demonstration by fishermen's strength, who on Monday severely disrupted the accessibility of several Breton and Normando ports, the French president decided to make his way Tuesday morning to Guilvinec port in Finistère to meet with their representatives there.
The latter have vowed to be adopted "in a constructive vein," and hoping to "come up with concrete proposals."
The presidential visit comes at the same time as the general assembly of the "crisis committee" created by Breton fishermen in order to obtain measures against a sharp increase in diesel prices, which has reached a peak exceeding an average of 1.14 euros per litre.
According to Elysee Palace spokesman David Martinon, the president "will talk to them about solutions that can be imagined in an effort to help their businesses regain competitiveness and better ensure the conditions of employment and disbursement of seafarers."
Nicolas Sarkozy is expected by fishermen with intransigence.
On Tuesday morning, they carried out an "snake" operation (an operation of slow-moving vehicles) in the Morbihan and Calvados departments, causing nearly 10km of constipation.
The recreational harbour in Quiberon was also blocked, but the connections in the direction of Belle-île were not interrupted.
A filter dam has been opened on the Quiberon Peninsula.
Sailors demonstrated their determination as early as Monday, when, just at dawn in front deposits in Brest and Lorient, they were burning tires and pallets, while demonstrations took place in Concarneau and Saint-Brieuc.
The movement spread to other ports on the Atlantic coast and the la Manche canal, such as Sables d'Olonne in Vendée, where traulers remained in the port, or to Le Havre, where about twenty vessels blocked the operation of merchant ships.
Fishermen from Turballe and Croisic in the Loire-Atlantique area halted a blockade of an important Total refinery in Donges during the evening.
Boulogne-sur-Mer decided to wait until Wednesday's meeting at the ministry.
In Guilvinec, a "crisis committee" is scheduled on Tuesday to decide on how to continue its journey, and on its participation in an upcoming meeting at the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
However, some Breton fishermen do not want to be represented by the leaders of the state's fisheries union, which did not support their movement.
"The strike will continue" because "fishermen won't abandon anything" and "don't want to come to Paris," Liliane Cariou, chairwoman of the Lorient Fisheries Department, said.
Occupational fishermen who do not pay diesel tax are currently paying about 50 cents per litre, but considers that the threshold of profitability of the ship is reached only when diesel is below 0.30 euros per litre.
Paul Newman spectacular
An unforgettable "Butch Cassidy and Kid" representative died as a result of cancer at the age of 83 at his home in Connecticut.
Hillary and Bill Clinton, Nicolas Sarkozy, George Clooney, Julia Roberts, Robert Redford and Sam Mendes have bowed to film legend and politically engaged actor Paul Newman, who died at the age of 83.
Embraer maintains its delivery requirements for 2008
Brazil's plane maker is relying on the delivery of 215 aircraft this year, despite the threat of a recession hovering over the world's economy.
The world's fourth aerospace engineer, Embraer, maintains its delivery assumptions for 2008 with 215 supplied instruments, confirmed its president, Fredrico Fleury Curado.
In the first half, the manufacturer delivered 97 aircraft.
The Brazilian designer expects a lot from the Chinese market, especially considering the range of his aircraft from 30 to 120 seats.
To date, Embraer has supplied Chinese clients with 41 devices within eight years.
AIG is said to be considering assigning 15% of its assets
American International Group, which U.S. monetary authorities have been put over water, is considering transferring more than 15% of its assets in order to repay $85 billion given to it as an advance, writes Sunday's Financial Times edition.
AIG is ready to examine the sale of most of its activities outside of life insurance policies internationally and pension activities in the United States, specifies a journal that cites sources close to ongoing negotiations.
According to the FT, the AIG Board of Directors met on Sunday during the evening to examine the eventually.
It was not possible to obtain any confirmatory statement from AIG.
The assets that AIG intends to get rid of include it's subsidiary of International Lease Financial Corp., its 59% participation in Transatlantic Holdings insurers, as well as its property portfolio and private equity investment, the FT adds.
No final decision on these referrals has yet been taken, the FT continues.
AIG needs to quickly raise cash in order to repay the 85-billion-dollar loan provided to it by the Federal Reserve Bank, at the risk that U.S. authorities will seize 80% of its capital, diluting at the same time to the appropriate ratio of current shareholders.
Paris. Analysis of the Historical Session of the Morning Meeting
The New York Stock Exchange posted a historic decline on Monday after the House of Representatives rejected Paulson's plan to save banks: Dow Jones lost 6.98%, an unprecedented loss approaching 800 points, and the Nasdaq 9.14%.
The Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJIA) fell 777.68 points to 10 365.45 points.
The biggest slump so far in its history (684.81 points) was recorded on September 17, 2001, the day of the resumption of trading after the 9/11 terrorist attacks.
The Nasdaq index, with its prevailing technological component, lost 199.61 points, according to definitive data after the trading deadline, and held steady at 1 983.73, and the expanded Standard & Poor's 500 index fell 8.81% (i.e. 106.85 points) to 1 106.55 points.
dejected since the start of the session, Wall Street has literally plummeted after an unexpected rejection of a plan to bail out the banks by the House of Representatives, a plan that was supposed to make it possible to find a bit of stability in the markets again.
228 votes against and 205 for representatives refused to provide the secretary of the Treasury Henry Paulson with the funds he asked for.
Nearly two Republicans out of three rejected his plan.
"It's a bad signal to a posted market that sees that Congress is incapable of adopting a certain plan," Lindsey Piegza of FTN Financial said in a statement.
"It shows how much the market relied on this plan," she added, expressing concerns that the project adopted in a few days would not arrive "too late."
"If this project is indeed doomed, the relay will immediately be handed over to the Fed and other central banks to spark a crisis, which would strengthen the lending conditions," said Brian Bethune, lead economist at Global Insight.
Piegza pointed out that “every new day brings about the downfall of a financial institution in the United States.”
After Friday's crash, Washington Mutual was orchestrated by authorities to buy Wachovia's bank's bank's banking activities by its rival, Citigroup.
In Europe, the crisis has suddenly regained momentum and has demanded urgent intervention in favour of banks in the United Kingdom, Benelux, Iceland and Germany, suggesting that markets are struggling “not only with the U.S. crisis, but with the global crisis.”
The bond market has risen strongly.
Yield from a cashier's order for 10 years fell to 3.632%, compared to 3.827% on Friday night, yield for 30 years to 4.161% versus 4.357% the previous day.
Obama won on points in first televised duel
Friday's first debate between the two candidates in the White House was followed by a second debate, whose task was to find out which of the pair John McCain and Barack Obama won.
Republicans and Democrat campaign headquarters were not surprised when they both declared their victory in a televised duel watched by a third of U.S. families.
Political analyst David Gergen believes John McCain's performance, however polite, wasn't "brilliant" enough to allow him to stop, five weeks before the presidential election, a drop in the polls.
Gallupa's institute, whose opinion poll released Saturday attributes Obama's five-point lead (49% against 44% for McCain), said the Democrat is the one who won the duel.
Among the undecided voters that matter right now, Obama is a winner for 39% of respondents, while, according to CBS polls, 25 per cent attribute victory to McCain.
Obama should have the opportunity to confirm his leadership on Thursday using the supposed good performance of his co-candidate Joseph Biden, during the first and at the same time only public debate with his rival Sarah Palin.
"It would be a mistake to underestimate her.
She's gifted," Bill Clinton pointed out over the weekend.
Seventy-year-old McCain has consistently highlighted his undeniable experience in the field of foreign policy, while highlighting his opponent's "naïvence" on issues that formed the core of the first encounter.
"Senator Obama doesn't seem to have understood that..." was the repeated allegation.
"I don't think Senator Obama has sufficient experience and knowledge to become president," he said.
There have been Republican TV spots in such an offensive spirit since yesterday.
Obama, by contrast, emphasized that it represents the future.
He didn't lose his head and turned out to be concise and precise.
He highlighted his ability to take the right decisions and restore the good reputation of the United States in the world.
"McCain still didn't understand, while Obama did," the Democratic spot replied over the weekend, reproaching the Republican for not taking into account enough the impact of the crisis on an "ordinary American."
"During the ninety-minute debate, John McCain had a lot to say about me, but nothing about you," Obama said in front of 20,000 people gathered at an election meeting in North Carolina for heavy rain.
Nor did he unspeak of words such as "middlace" or "workers." The financial decomposition unambiguously favors Obama, whom Americans consider more competent to save an economy on the edge of the abyss.
"Next week, there could be a decisive reversal," influential Democratic Senator Charles Schumer estimated yesterday.
Democratic representative Rahm Emanuel, a former member of the Clinton administration, likened Obama's "economic foresight" to the one Bill Clinton had during the 1998 financial crisis.
For Democrat nominees, the current disaster is "the final verdict of an eight-year bad economic policy, as run by George W. Bush, and how Senator McCain supported it."
Reafferred by the Illinois senator that despite Wall Street's unexpected jumps, he should be able to cut taxes on 95% of Americans, and also cautioned that implementing the country's eventual energy independence by enforcing renewable energy sources is its number one priority, along with the introduction of a health insurance system.
McCain, in turn, plans to build a park with 45 nuclear power plants by 2030.
The two candidates cautiously avoided issues related to the 700-billion-dollar (470-billion-euro financial assistance plan) adopted by Congress last night and which is heavily unpopular.
"Have you noticed that there have been elections in Minsk?"
Young people and opponents of the regime demonstrated on Sunday evening in the main square of the Belarusian capital to reject the results of parliamentary elections in advance.
Several hundred Belarusians gathered on Sunday evening immediately after polling stations were closed in Minsk's main square to protest against the irregularity of elections to parliament.
Although no results have yet been announced, many young people have come to demonstrate against President Lukashenko's regime: "dictator, resignation."
Some admit that they didn't even go to the polls: "What good would it be if they did whatever they wanted with my voice anyway," says one young man.
They do not believe that this regime, which has acted friendly to foreign observers in order to resume negotiations with the European Union, would be prepared to take a turnaround towards democracy.
Sanctions, which include a ban on visas for 40 Belarusian bosses and freezing some assets, could be lifted if the election is seen by observers as clean.
According to the opposition, this was not the case.
"We were kept away from the electoral commissions and our observers were not allowed to participate in the vote count," complained Yuri Potemkin, a spokesman for the Union of Democratic Forces, a diverse group of twelve parties.
“It’s a cosmetic change,” emphasizes Alexander Milinkievich, a former opposition candidate in the 2006 presidential election and the leader of the pro-European movement, “because the important thing is who counts the votes, and in our country it is executive power.
If some opposition MPs get into parliament, it will be because they will be appointed and not elected.
However, their accession to parliament can mean space on television, meetings in the regions and draft laws.”
Therefore, the opposition feels obliged to participate in the political functioning, because "a dialogue with the European Union needs to be initiated.
If our economy, deeply indebted to Russia, is affected by the crisis and we do not reform ourselves, we are in danger of becoming the Protectorate of Russia.
But we want to be independent.”
The opposition is concerned that the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe, which monitored the conduct of the elections, does not declare the elections valid.
He points out that turnout seems to be too high and that the general introduction of early voting (26% of voters in Minsk) has opened the way for all sorts of manipulations.
"How about you, have you noticed that any elections have been held?" asks social-democratic leader Alexander Kozulin, one of the last two political prisoners released in August this year.
"There wasn't even a leg at the polling stations.
And there will be a maximum of five opposition MPs in parliament, those most suited to the regime."
Night's Silence has once again taken place in the October square.
On television, President Lukashenko and members of the Central Election Commission congratulate themselves on their success.
Conversation - Blood, which in turn is certain
Héma-Québec celebrated the tenth anniversary of its founding yesterday, and its president, Dr Francine Décary, rightly rejoiced because Héma-Québec became one of the most reliable and effective suppliers of blood in the world.
Above all, he regained the confidence of the public, which was severely shaken after the scandal of contaminated blood in the 80's and 90's.
As is widely known, the state blood reserve administered by the Department of Transfusion of the Canadian Red Cross was contaminated by immune deficiency virus (HIV) and hepatitis C virus during the 1980s.
Due to this drama and a wave of public displaugement, in February 1994, the Canadian government began a public inquiry presided over by Judge Horace Krever, which was intended to explain the circumstances that led to such a tragedy and to formulate recommendations not to repeat such a disaster.
While Judge Krever hesitated to submit the report, some provinces set into action a committee aimed at planning a new blood management system in Canada.
In Québek, Gélinea's committee defended this role.
After Gélineau's and Krever's report, two suppliers were set up in Canada: Héma-Québec, which was supposed to manage Québec, and the Canadian Blood Society to supply all provinces and both territories.
"When Héma-Québec began its activities in 1998, barely 38% of people still had confidence in the Quebec blood system," Dr. Décary recalled.
“The situation was daunting.
We have regaineded public confidence by continuing efforts to increase the safety of blood products and continuous work in this area in the coming years," he says, proudly adding that at the beginning of 2008 Héma-Québeku was trusted by almost 94% of the Quebec population.
Dr. Décary, however, does not lose sight that "confidence is something very fragile."
Since its inception on September 28, 1998, Héma-Québec has applied the principle of prudence.
“At that time in Europe, we have witnessed the occurrence of many cases of the human form of mad cow disease, a new variant of a disease called Creutzfeldt-Jakob’s disease,” says the woman, who has led the company since its inception in 1998.
"We had no idea if this disease could spread through blood.
Therefore, we relied on one of the basic recommendations of the Krever and GÉlineau report, according to which, if we do not know and when the spread of a serious disease is at stake, in this case Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, which is characterized by brain degeneration inevitably leading to death, it is necessary to take excluding measures.
Therefore, in April 1999, we dismissed blood from people who had been staying in the UK for more than a month since 1980.
A few years ago, it was safely proven that this disease is transmitted by blood.
This episode, therefore, showed us that sticking to the principle of prudence made sense.”
In 1999, a much more accurate technique appeared on the market to detect HIV: tests using nucleic acid (NAT), which allow to detect directly the presence of the virus itself in the donor blood, and not the presence of antibodies on which tests were used before.
NAT tests have made it possible to shorten the deaf period, which occurs between the moment of infection of the patient and the moment when the first viruses can be detected by our techniques in the blood.
We were able to shorten this period to less than a week.
We've substantially reduced the risk that some donors could be infected without knowing about it and without being able to figure it out.
These improvements made it possible to significantly reduce the risk of obtaining AIDS through blood transfusion.
The risk of getting blood contaminated with HIN is today exposed to one person in 12 million.
“In other words, this means that 12 million blood products will need to be distributed before someone becomes infected with HIV.
Due to Héma-Québec distributing 400 000 to 500 000 blood products annually, it will take 24 years for the first case of transmission of HIV through a blood product.
I believe that today we can get or prescribe the blood transfusion necessary for recovery,” says Héma-Québeku, head of management.
"The challenge for the next decade is to preserve the safety of our products.
We never know what disease-causing element may appear in the system.
Just last year, a chikungunya virus appeared on Réunion, which, like the Western Nile virus, is transmitted to the blood by stabbing insects.
It is necessary to be constantly cautious," Dr. Décary continues, stressing that, in addition to safe supply, Héma-Québec also has to count on sufficient supply.
"Needs are growing because the population is getting older.
As a result of more aggressive cancer treatment, patients often need a new dose of red blood cells or platelets.
Population aging also has implications for a network of blood donors, which on average consists only of 3% of Québeans permissible for donation.”
Any healthy person at least 18 years of age, corresponding to admissibility criteria, but can donate blood only once every 56 days, that is, six times a year.
14% of people offering blood are not admitted on average because, for example, they are carriers of AIDS or malaria, and whether they have visited a country where mud chills rages are eliminated for a year.
"With these 3%, we are able to maintain a condition that meets the needs of hospitals.
Deficiencies are very rare.
However, members of the network of donors need to be supplemented in proportion to how they get older and can no longer donate blood.
But it is difficult to persuade people to give blood, especially to keep and encourage them to become regular donors.
“Life is so unbridled today that we rarely find an hour so that we can donate blood.”
With an annual budget of 297 million, Héma-Québec delivers not only blood products, but also human tissue, such as bone transplants, heart valves, eyeballs and skin, which is almost exclusively used for major burns.
These tissues are analyzed, processed and freezed before they are deposited with Héma-Québek, which also manages the only public bank of blood from the umbilical cord in Québek.
The umbilical cord, which was previously considered biomedical waste, abounds in stem-rich blood, which can be used to treat serious diseases such as leukemia.
"A few weeks ago, we celebrated the deposit of our thousand-dollar blood sample from the umbilical cord into the bank, and we expect to take over a thousand of them a year.
At the beginning of next year, we should start offering them for transplants," explains Dr. Décaryová, who describes the blood collection program from the umbilical cord as very popular.
"Some mothers are disappointed that they don't have access to it," because Héma-Québec doesn't have enough resources to offer this comprehensive and expensive program to all hospitals in Quebec.
Soccer - Impact in semi-finals
Impact Montréal qualified for the semi-final round of the USL's semi-final round at Saputo Stadium yesterday during the second and crucial quarter-final match played on the total number of wickets, thanks to a 3:1 (4:3 victory after both games) over the Sounders of Seattle.
Midfielder Antonio Ribeiro scored the decisive goal in the 90th minute of the game.
After a two-sided goalless first-half, midfielder David Testo and Rocco Plancentino scored the remaining two goals of the Montreal formation.
Roger Levesque was the only one who was able to cope with Matt Jordan in favor of the Sounders.
Recall that on Friday during the first of two quarter-final matches, Impact suffered a 2:1 defeat at Starfire Complex in Tukwile, a Seattle suburb.
The impact will measure its strength in the semi-finals with the Whitecaps of Vancouver.
On Saturday night in Minnnesota, Thunder equalized a 3:1 deficit in his home game and eventually won 4:3, but the Whitecaps advanced to the semi-final round with a total of 5:4 goals.
Impact is expected to clash with Whitecaps on Friday and Sunday, but details of the series of matches will not be confirmed until today.
In short - Kovalev's winning goal
Canadien's first offensive trio are ready to start the season.
A unit made up of Alex Kovalev, Tomas Plekanec and Andrej Kosticin last night at Centre Bell encouraged an offence to win Canadien 3:2 on separate raids.
Kosticin, the biggest star of the encounter, scored a goal, Plekanec accumulated two assists and Kovalev reaped one pass in that fourth-round win from six preparatory matches.
Kovalev secured the victory by scoring the only goal during separate raids.
"I'm not ready yet," the Russian pointed out.
"I need one more game to feel good."
"In the first period, he tried to handle too much," Guy Carbonneau observed.
"But I think he appreciated the end of the game.
He got some applause.”
Charest bets north
The Prime Minister has not ruled out calling an early election by the end of the year
Immediately after the end of the Liberal Party (Parti libéral), Jean Charest moved to Jean-Talon in Québek, where the new health minister Yves Bolduc (left) is running in today's supplementary elections.
"Relegating the limits of our last great northern border": such is the long-term ambition that Jean Charest presented yesterday in a visually engaging way at the conclusion of the Congress of the Liberal Party of Quebec in Lévis, without, however, giving details.
During his speech, pictures were projected behind his back in a kind of picture grinder.
On three giant canvases ran a loop with remarkable images of hydroelectric dams, down, Québek from the point of view of "Google Maps", maps, etc.
Generous means, as in the pre-election period.
At a press conference, the prime minister refused to rule out that it would provoke early elections in the autumn, although he pointed out that it has a very busy schedule.
"Plan North" forms the fourth dimension of the "vision" that the prime minister intends for Québec, a vision of a "new economic area", which includes an agreement with France on labor, a treaty between Canada and Europe, and agreements with Ontario.
Despite the fact that the opening of the North - northern territory on the 49th parallel - the prime minister often mentioned in his statements recently, it has never been the subject of a presentation until now.
However, the prime minister limited himself to explaining why the North represents "a jewel with enormous potential," but did not give a deadline, the amount of investment, nor a clear priority in future projects.
At the most, he mentioned several approaches to points that could be further developed: in particular, hydroelectric power plants, the forest, but in particular the mining sector, encouraged by the growth of rapidly developing countries and the restoration of public infrastructures.
The north, according to Charest's list, contains "nickel, zinc, copper, platinum, gold, silver, iron, titanium, and even diamonds."
This "territory twice the size of France is with us", "is in Québek.
And he's not just with us, but he's in us," he pointed out, as if he wanted to show that he's appealing to a certain national founding myth.
Québec was built to a large extent in relation to the North, the prime minister highlighted: the hunt for gold in Abitibi, Quebec engineering, James bay.
"It's ours, it's our future," he added after pausing theatrically to view the huge map behind his back.
The north, 70% of Québek's territory, includes a part of Labrador that Québec has always demanded, the prime minister specified during a press conference.
"This is a traditional stance that has been repeated by all governments so far.
“There is one frontier line on which there has not been a convention for a long time, and if there is a suitable opportunity, in the name of the government I will take that position again.”
Otherwise, in the context of climate change, it is also necessary to rethink our vision of the North," he said, referring to the north-west crossing, which will open year-round as a result of glacier melting.
In this new geopolitical context, the Prime Minister believes that seizing territory is becoming an obligation.
And what about the inhabitants of the territory, the indigenous peoples and the Inuit?
The prime minister said that the Quebec government would be very attentive to them and that the project would be beneficial to them, especially the young.
Two former ministers from two different parties, Pierre Corbeil and Michel Létourneau (former Pequist from Ungavy), have already started negotiations with about thirty communities.
The work was initiated by an inter-ministry committee chaired by Benoît Pelletier in which Julie Boulet (Transportation and Natural Resources) has an important position.
The prime minister does not rule out a major meeting, "not necessarily a summit," but the moment when "a common vision will be set."
Moreover, the development of the North should take place in accordance with the principles of sustainable development.
The North plan will be "together designed, structured and environmentally friendly," he promised.
Jean Charest described this "vision" as a seamless follow-up to the vision of liberal prime ministers who preceded it.
"Like the Liberals who preceded us, we have changed our mindset to turn challenges into new opportunities for growth and development."
This, after all, was one of the ever-shaken themes of the weekend, which was launched on Friday with a big cocktail highlighting the 50th anniversary of Jean Lesage's arrival at the side.
One of the adopted resolutions proposes rebaptizing Beauharnois's headquarters at Adélard-Godbout Dam.
"From Godbout, who founded Hydro-Québec, through Lesage, who carried out the Quiet Revolution and the nationalization of hydropower, to Bourassa, who created James Bay, our party was able to shape the future," declared Jean Charest.
At a press conference, he appealed to John Diefenbaker's "Roads the North," the prime minister from 1957 to 1963.
Speaking the turn of Eastmain1a-Ruper's dam hydroelectric power plant, Charest emphasized that its launch is planned for 2012.
"I'm just saying that between us, but I'm planning on being there," he confided to his member base, prompting considerable applause.
The same prime minister, who said in April that there would be no elections in 2008, and undoubtedly not in 2009, did not rule out the possibility of calling an election in the autumn of this year, which is allowed by law.
However, he emphasized that his program is packed, especially by the federal council's mission in China and the Québec-New York summit.
The weekend congress provided the Liberal leader with a platform for some nationalist resolutions, in particular certain "efforts to amend the constitution" that have passed very smoothly: PLQ (Liberal Party of Québek) calls especially for the recognition of the "specificity of Québek" in the Constitution.
He also wishes Québec to appoint part of the members of Canada's Radio and Television Broadcasting Council, who would have veto power in the event of decisions taken in Québek.
On the part of the opposition, Plan North, in its main features, was accepted skeptically.
Pauline Marois, PQ (Parti Québecois), has once again pulled the Northern Québek Development Policy launched by Landry's government in 2001.
According to her, when the government of the Liberals came to power in 2003, she renounced the implementation of this policy.
If she had adhered to it, "we'd have gone on today," Marois remarked alongside her Jean-Talon candidate (where the election is taking place today) Françoise Mercure.
ADQ said it would support the adoption of the North plan in the government.
"However, we wish it wasn't mere theatre," explained Sébastien Proulx, a leader representing Maria Dumonta, who is returning from the holidays today.
Letters - Disgraced Country
It was Vic Toews, the ex-minister of "justice" (o irony) in Harper's cabinet, who took the liberty of asking about Louise Arbour in the summer of last year that he represented "shame" for Canada.
In this way, humiliating a woman who has wagered an unceasing fight for justice and who deserves all our admiration says a lot about the prime minister's surroundings and about Stephen Harper himself.
We have before us a government that is the only one of all participating Western countries that has not transported back to the homeland of its nation closed in the Guantánamomo concentration camp.
The government, which does not take into account at all, that at the time when he was supposed to perform the acts he was accused of, Omar Khadr was a young adolescent.
The only government that, even though Canada has signed the Geneva Convention, denies its word and leaves to rot in the inhumane conditions of a child soldier.
The only one who relies on a parody of justice to judge the defendant's guilt.
Recall that UNICEF, Amnesty International and especially the Association of Canadian Advocates are advocating the repatriation of Omar Khadr in order to await a fair trial in Canada.
The same government closes its eyes to torture: it doesn't care that this young man is psychologically destroyed, and undoubtedly permanently, after so many years of torment.
We don't realize it, but we're just witnessing the creeping erosion of our rights, our freedom and our "democracy," the words to which Harper is swaying so much and which sounds so void from his mouth.
The time has come when there is no morality anymore: it is possible to torture a Canadian citizen, mishandled him, imprisoned him, according to the arbitrariness of the head of the still minority government (as will be the case after October 14...).
Many voters believe that they will find salvation in a party that proclaims Virtuity, the Law and the Order: they do not see that this government renounced what constituted the reputation and seriousness of our country.
Shame, Mr. Toews, in this renouncement, and not elsewhere.
Opinions - Orwellian Discurse of Barrick Gold
In the September 17 issue, Le Devoir published a letter from Patrick J. Garver, executive vice president of the Canadian mining company Barrick Gold.
With Orwellian logic, he confirms that the world's largest gold producer has launched the prosecution of three authors and a poor little publishing house for insult to honor in order to provoke a "transparent public debate" and calls for six million.
If it is true that judges are independent and impartial, they do not judge truthfulness, especially in the case of prosecution for insult to honor, but by law.
Unsurprisingly, Garver completely ignores this fundamental distinction.
Or is it the best way for Barrick Gold to have a public debate to braise anyone who dares to defend the opposite position?
Whatever the outcome of the trial, the authors and publisher of Noir Canada will be forced to spend respectable sums so that they are ready to defend themselves and fight in a very unbalanced fight with an army of attorneys backed by the mining company’s almost bottomless pockets.
Moreover, the tactics in these unbalanced struggles often lie in ruining the defendants in order to enforce conciliatory proceedings, which clog the mouth of the opposing party and, moreover, scatter anyone who dares to devote themselves to the matter.
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public debate, it should have presented its view of the matter to public opinion.
Contrary to what is happening in several countries where Barrick Gold operates, in Canada, debate normally consists in the exchange of ideas, and not baton wounds.
Mining companies, therefore, were not enough but to open their books, their documentation, and establish a real debate through newspapers, media, publications and the financing of independent scientific teams that could bring light to the matter.
Barrick Gold, however, doesn't seem to have confidence in public opinion and smudges rejects petitions and letters in support of the authors of Noir Canada.
Perhaps Garver could explain how he can demand a "public debate," and at the same time despise the views of the same public?
It seems that this word in the vice president's world refers only to bold-paid lawyers, and not all Canadians; that's a rather narrowed conception of this term.
In a democracy, they have the right to speak, too.
Barrick Gold doesn't understand - or doesn't want to understand - that the public, which supports authors and publishers of Noir Canada, is doing so primarily because it rejects the company's brutal ways.
Barrick is prepared to hear and assess the matter, but on the basis of a civilized exchange of arguments, not in the context of what many perceive as a prosecution in which the defendant’s mouth is to be clogged (as part of his prosecution of “public debate”, Barrick has, by the way, just filed an official appeal to the authors of Noir Canada to officially notify them that they should stop using this term...).
Apart from Garver's surreal speech, the prosecution initiated by Barrick Gold is a direct attack on the university's freedom of inquiry and the search for the truth that are essential to every democratic society.
Overall, it denies the right to cite credible sources and to debate the facts and interpretations, which constitute the very basis of intellectual activity.
If it will be impossible in a country like Canada to study and discuss topics that do not appeal to the rich companies of this world, without exposing ourselves to repeated prosecutions, who else will be able to do it?
If Barrick Gold really wanted a public and transparent debate, it could create it if it followed the scientific norms used by Noir Canada authors.
He's entitled to an answer.
The university environment to which we belong has always managed debates and disagreements and resolve conflicts.
Barrick Gold's exaggerated lawsuit makes it clear that, despite what it claims, it doesn't have the slightest desire to have a transparent debate.
It's a loss for the university world, as it is for public debate in Canada, and it's time for governments to introduce measures that would limit such a type of unauthorised prosecution.
Operation to save Wall Street - Democrats and Republicans understand each other
The Bush administration and congressional leaders, both Republicans and Democrats, finally reached an agreement yesterday on the biggest bailout economic plan since the great crisis of the 1930s.
A new version of the plan to use 700 billion U.S. dollars to hamper a financial crisis that has been around Wall Street for months has yet to be approved by both chambers of Congress.
In particular, it assumes: better control of elected representatives over its application, new mechanisms ensuring the repayment of stored public money, new measures to capture the amount of houses seized due to non-repayment, and also the right to monitor the amount of wages paid to executives of rescued companies.
U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson, Speaker of the House of Representatives Nancy Pelosi and Republican official at the negotiating table, Senator Judd Gregg, announced the good news yesterday a few minutes after midnight, after the two-day negotiating marathon ended.
“We’ve made great progress, and we’ve come to an agreement that’s going to be effective in the markets,” Henry Paulson said.
"Everything has been done in such a way that the real economy and ordinary Americans are safe from the crisis just occurring on Wall Street," the Democrat explained.
"That's an essential point: if we don't do it, the trauma, the chaos and the disruption of Americans' daily lives will be huge," Republican Judd Gregg said.
Over the last few hours, the negotiations have run out to the maximum, out of the fear of the effect of further failures on the mood of investors.
The designated time limit was the opening of markets this morning.
Both U.S. presidential candidates were invited to the negotiations later, and both expressed themselves more for the deal, but did not omit their dissatisfaction with the need to resort to such an extreme solution.
Democrat Senator Barack Obama credited supplementing the agreement with some of the measures, in particular those aimed at allowing owners to preserve their residence, and measures on the wages of the heads of state-saved firms.
"When we ask taxpayers to take special precautions because of the irresponsibilities of some people, there's nothing to congratulate themselves on.
However, these measures are necessary," he explained on the waves of television.
His Republican rival John McCain, who was interviewed by another station whether he would vote for the proposed plan, replied: "I'd like to see the details, but I hope so."
"The gross features I've read [shows] that this is something we're all going to have to digest and through which it will be possible to move forward.
Choosing inaction is simply unacceptable," he said.
The goal of the 700-billion-dollar plan is to build away with taxpayers' money dubious receivables and risky and unsaleable assets of U.S. financial institutions.
The plan comes after 14 months of crisis unleashed by the bursting bubble in the property market, which first led to the collapse of high-risk subprimes, then transferred to other, more widespread forms of loans (PCAAs) and resulted in the freezing of cash and the disintegration of banks and financial institutions.
Last week's decline in last week's first draft deal is due to Republican MPs.
Since Democrats agreed that for some of the "toxic" assets of companies having difficulties, the government will only vouch, but will not buy them, there is a belief that the impassable situation is ignited.
In an effort to converge opposing views in Congress, a measure has been annexed setting out that the government will be able to introduce new fees that would help it get its deposit back if, as a result of the program, it would have to take losses for five years.
The new plan foresees a deposit of the first amount of £250bn, to which an additional ££100 billion can be added, if the president deems it necessary.
A deposit of an additional 350 billion should be approved by Congress in a new vote.
In exchange for its assistance, Washington will acquire shares in the rescued companies, which, in the event of a profit, it will be able to pay dividends and which it will be able to resell when it is all over.
The federal government will have the option of delayinging property foreclosures associated with loans covered by the plan, as well as set limits on the amount of valuations and compensation paid to executives of affected firms.
The architects of the plan would likely subject it to a vote in the House of Representatives as early as today and in the Senate on Wednesday.
Despite all the efforts made, it is not yet certain whether their plan will have the green light.
Yesterday, voices continued to rise against this project in both the Republican and the Democratic camp.
"It's perfectly obvious that Washington doesn't listen to you," Republican Scott Garrett, a member of the House Financial Commission, said in a statement.
"Yes, we have a serious financial problem.
Yes, there are solutions.
However, it is not an option to make money on taxpayers.”
Democratic representative Brad Sherman, moderator of a group of congressmen nicknamed "committee of skeptics", presents the whole thing differently, but there is the same conclusion.
"Four hundred esteemed professors of economics, among them the three winners of the Nobel Prize, wrote to us: we are asking Congress not to rush too much and carefully examine which path to take," he declared yesterday.
At the same time, it was reaffirmed in Europe that this crisis was a global crisis.
Following an emergency meeting, three Benelux countries announced partial nationalization of the Fortis banking group for US$11.2 billion (US$16.3 billion).
Interventions between the governments of Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg became necessary after French bank BNP Paribas announced it was withdrawing from the purchase of its competitor, which has been on the verge of collapse since it lost 2 billion euros (2.9 billion USD) as a result of a crisis of risky loans to buy real estate in the United States.
Fortis employs 85 000 people, among Europe's twenty largest banks and among the top ten insurers.
"We're not going to lose this money," Dutch Finance Minister Wouter Bos said yesterday.
"In return, we get the right to vote in the bank and influence.
And it is the savers and households that can be appreciated in these uncertain times.”
On the other side of La Manche's canal so far, it seems, the British government has begun nationalising another bank affected by the financial crisis.
According to the English press, the government was supposed to take over all of Bradford and Bringley's existing loans, of which more than $41bn (US$75.5 billion) consisted of loans to buy real estate.
According to yesterday's announcement, the branches and accounts of the clients of the British bank will be purchased by the Spanish bank Santander.
Bradford and Bringley will be the second bank after Northern Rock, which the British government must nationalize since the start of the financial crisis.
Paul Newman (1925-2008) - The death of a socially engaged icon
His beauty with azure's eyes long represented his trump, according to other weaknesses.
Has this American actor escaped with a gnaming smile from narcissism?
Yes and no.
The shyness, sensitivity, and unfailing talent that were inherent in him also distanced him from the force category that breeds true stars.
What a long time runner!
Paul Newman died Friday at the age of 83 at his ranch in Connecticut as a result of lung cancer and remains one of the last icons of the fat years of American cinema.
Less magnificent than Brando, to whom he was similar and for a long time his rival on the screen, Newman was a more cautious actor, certainly a more fragile, but also an apostle of freedom and the disintegration of dramatic conventions.
Mixed blood often begets beautiful babies.
He was born in Ohio in 1925, his father was a German Jew and mother of Hungarian origin.
From these European roots, one of the famous faces of the American dream was born.
No exaggerated ego, which is the last sign of quality.
Because in it, the whole of this land in love with self-made man, a warrior in the war in the Pacific, wounded in a battle who in the early 1950s studied acting in Yale, then attended New York's Actor's Studio and celebrated triumphs in theatre, on television, and soon in film.
The same man who fought for civil rights, he was also a car racer, who ranked second in the 24-hour Le Mans race in 1979, and also a sexual symbol, despite the fact that from 1958 he formed a model couple with actress Joanne Woodward.
With it, too, the dismay of this hidden, discreet, socially engaged stars, allergic to the fuss of their own show-business, which will forever remain mysterious to some extent.
Decent man, in the noblest sense.
Paul Newman's departure this weekend caused dizziness for film lovers, while his big roles compete with each other on a black canvas of remembrances.
More than half a century of ever-changing American seventh art defilates after him.
A charming cowboy from Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid (1969), a young man who rejects his sensual wife (Elizabeth Taylor) in The Cat on the Hot Roof, a 1958 adaptation of Tennessee Williams.
From the less ancient past (1990) we recall his sensitive Mr. Bridge, disturbed at an advanced age by marriage that didn't meet all expectations.
So many other Paul Newmans went down in history.
Boxer Rocky Graziano, an abused, violent, memorable delinquent from Somebody Up There Likes Me by Robert Wis, a role designed for James Dean, taken after his death, which made Newman a true acquaintance in 1956.
Sympathetic gangster in absolutely great The Sting, Oscar for Best Picture in 1973.
A bitter and vengeful billiard player in the amazing The Hustler Robert Rossen (1961), will play the same character in Martin Scorsese's Color of Money, which will bring him the only Oscar from seven nominations in 1987.
This face wandering through the ages, it was him.
And him again.
There are various epochs competing in his professional career.
The youth know him mainly thanks to the iconical Slap Shot George Roy Hill (1977) from the role of Reggie Dunlop, an unconventional hockey coach.
Fans reunited with him in 1994 in The Hudsucker Proxy of the Coen brothers.
He concluded his career with the role of the godfather of the Irish mafia in Road to Perdition by Sama Mendes in 2003.
In contrast to a number of film legends, which relatively soon abandoned craft, Newman remained in the business for a long time, albeit denouncing Hollywood with its gilts, flamboyances and concessions.
French filmmaker Bertrand Tavernier considered him, not without humor, "a vivid challenge of underplaying," judging that in his performances he put too much on the sawmill.
For the beautiful Paul Newman, to whom the roles came as on a tray, the profession wasn't always so tender.
However, he played his first lovers less often than outsiders: neurotics, alcoholics, crooks, and desperators whose declinement and riots were reflected in his perfect features.
As director Newman has revealed little-known pages of his shy sensitivity.
His 1968 film Rachel, Rachel (according to Canadian Margaret Laurence's novel), starring his wife Joanne Woodward, gives a sensitive female portrait full of melancholy.
His son Scott, who he lost because of an overdose, was donated in 1984 by the film Harry and Son, a work full of shame and sadness.
In a surging tribute to Newman yesterday, his great friend and former co-fighter Robert Redford (starred together in The Sting and in Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid) sadly declared: "His presence has made my life and this country better."
Generosity, love of life, such are the main qualities of the deceased actor.
Apart from his legendary career, a great civil rights campaigner, ecology, an independent film, an opponent of Hollywood's decline are also leaving.
We used to see his portrait on vials of wine vinegar, sauces, biscuits and other foods, which might have seemed rather tacky to us, even though it was a gag at first.
However, the profits from the sale went to the account of his Foundation for a Difficult Childhood
Newman, rather than being lulled by fame, was involved in society until the very last moment.
He left a letter to his five daughters containing these sentences: "Always and to the end, your father was grateful to his happy planet.
It was an honor to be here.”
And it was an honor for us to be able to follow the life path of such a clairvoyant and generous American artist.
And besides, after all, a model citizen.
What was produced in Zuschlag & spol. invoice factory?
Soon, on October 8, the trial conducted against János Zuschlag and his associates will begin.
In addition to other offences, including the forgery of deeds, the politician and his companions are charged with long-term and systematic criminal fraud, as a result of which extremely significant damages have been caused.
According to the prosecutor's office, they used the money raised in public tenders for their own purposes and often documented the fulfillment of tasks with fictitious invoices.
Money from the state and self-government - a total of approximately 65 million forints - went partly to the coffers of the Fiatal Baloldal organization, partly to the pockets of individuals.
The method, which was also reported by the Investigating Office, consisted in that the money raised in public tenders was collected on the basis of invoices for which there was no actual consideration, and they never lived up to the accepted obligations.
According to the indictment, much of the invoices were fake.
The forms for these invoices came from the Yoga-Fváros market, the round stamps were made in Kiskunhalasi.
The names on which companies the production of invoices should take place were decided, among other things, on a circular route along the northern shore of Balaton, to which he sent Istvána Lados János Zuschlag.
According to his testimony, the former was supposed to look out for guesthouses and restaurants.
In them, he asked for bills for his consumption, according to which invoices submitted in public tenders were then produced, among others, by the Ministry of Youth and Sport, the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs, respectively Self-Governance of the Capital.
Invoices were mostly issued by István Lados with his companion András Őri, who, according to our information, most often wrote reports needed to obtain subsidies.
During his "study journey," Lados picked out a beer hall.
According to two invoices issued by Zuschlag and its associates in 2003, this hospitality catering facility provided for the ninety-member group from Biztos Pont.
So many people, however, according to the company’s manager, would not suddenly fit into this facility.
Likewise, an invoice issued in the summer of 2002 by the Jobb Jövőért, later Együtt and Harmadik Éveredért was teeming with formal and content errors.
The guesthouse in Badacsonytomaj had only three rooms, more than ten people could not sleep in it one evening, yet one hundred and thirty guests were listed on the invoice.
However, however, the fillers of forms with a rich imagination exaggerated this even when they put on documents a company also dedicated to the hospitality industry - Pigal s. r. o., in whose accounting, according to the information provided to the public prosecutor's office, documents sounding in the name Jobb Jövőért and Hoppá did not even figure.
Győző Farkas told hvg.hu that when he was summoned to the prosecutor's office and invoiced, he knew immediately that it was fake documents.
"The name of our business and our tax number were in the wrong spot."
In addition to the formalities, he did not agree that according to the invoice, they accommodated a hundred people, because they have only eleven rooms that do not have such a large capacity.
In addition, catering has also been indicated on the invoice, while their restaurant has not been functioning for a long time.
Zealous invoice manufacturers did not even convince themselves that the popular Budapest travel agency organizes only foreign trips, and on an invoice with cash payments, which also did not coincide with the company's billing practice, they said foolishly as a service of mediating a trip around Hungary.
In addition, as has emerged in the course of the investigation, VAT was also stated in the document, although the organisation of trips to countries outside the European Union has been exempted from VAT.
On the four documents issued by the Jobb Jövőért Foundation by Hotel Révfülöp s. r. o., among other things, the amount of VAT did not agree. The company’s activities are not covered by the 25 percent, but 15 percent VAT, in addition, the hotel’s employees issued only printed invoices, but the invoice produced was written by hand.
In addition, "publishers" spoiled the invoice, for example, by stating with one company how it became clear thanks to an investigation by the prosecutor's office, as an activity carried out by road transport of goods, which was not carried out by a company originally based on investment advice.
The fact that the invoices issued by the Együtt and Harmadik Évezredért and the XXI associations. Századi Kulturális Egyesület and Biztos Pont were also faked due to formal deviations - non-corresponding invoice format and false stamp.
As in the case of Ravenna k. s. or the Kunfehértō Sports Camp, whose representatives denied that they issued the invoices.
For example, counterfeiters did not notice that HIB s. r. o. from Nyíregyházy was in liquidation at the time (2001), when a group from the Fiatal Városért association was eating, which is why, according to our information, the company’s liquidation commissioner also denied that he had issued the paper.
Invoices were exposed consciously
The second group of invoicers consists of those who knew to issue a fictitious invoice.
Esély Budapestért has issued bills for about four million forints for the Harmadik Évezredért Foundation at the request of Fiatal Baloldal - Ifjú Szocialisták.
According to the indictment, one of Ludovice Györfi’s accused was forced to issue a fictitious account.
Biogate s. r. o also cooperated with Zuschlag and his associates, currently in liquidation, which in 2003 supplied the Jobb Jövőért Foundation and Atlasz and Vörösfeny�ő associations with invoices worth about five million forints, behind which there was no actual economic activity.
Not even the movement of money, because the created invoices were later canceled, which, of course, organizations had already forgotten to state in their documents.
Another group of invoicing companies includes those that had ties to the defendants.
Thus, for example, Iván Kubatov was an authorized representative of Delta Trió s. r. o., a company engaged in the trading of fuels and motor vehicles, based in Budapest, on which, however, the company cannot be found.
In October, November and December 2005, the company issued several invoices for the sale of computer resources worth approximately 18 million forints.
According to the data found by the investigation, these invoices were purchased by Kiskunhalan company Cash Investment s. r. o., which had been liquidated since 14 September 2005.
The managing director of this company was a man from Romania, to whom Iván Kubatov was supposed to hand over documents relating to the company in 2002-2005.
As we know, Kubatov admitted during the investigation that on behalf of the company he issued invoices to Atlasz, Hoppá and the Együtt and Harmadik Évezredért associations and himself accepted their consideration.
At the request of Ivan Kubatov, Fa-Bani Jos Faipari s. r. o. and Jos-Investment k. s. were founded by Romanian bricklayers
In fact, neither of the companies worked, according to the defendant, because they did not get a job.
Nevertheless, his businesses benefited him because, at the request of a co-accused lawyer Zsolt Szente, who also prepared their founding charters, he issued invoices on behalf of the companies to Atlasz and Y-Generáció, linked to Zuschlag.
On behalf of Investment, Delta Trió, Fa-Bani Jos, Jos Investment and Linaxtrade, Kubatov gave organizations a total of 15 invoices worth 14.2 million forints.
The prosecutor's office concluded that Kubatov came to 10 percent of the amounts listed on invoices and VAT.
Szi-Beri k. t was also used to issue fictitious invoices, this time for a tender for Biztos Pont
In January 2006, Y-Generáció and Szövetség and Modern Magyarországért submitted proposals for the Mihály Táncsics Foundation (TMA), a publicly beneficial organization of the Socialist Party.
They received after 500 thousand forints, but did not spend the sums on the targets specified in the proposals.
In order for this not to become clear, TMA submitted five fictitious invoices from Works Team s. r. o.
Moreover, these invoices were found during a house search at Árpád Kemecsei’s office at the Ministry of Education.
During the trial, some of the defendants are likely to describe the course of production of invoices, as they have already done at the prosecution.
It can be more interesting if he decides to reveal everything himself the main defendant János Zuschlag.
His statement so far is not very detailed, and although he has already promised several times to interpret everything extensively, he has not yet done so.
The question is whether it is in his interest - in exchange for the payment of a large part of the damage, fifty million forints, perhaps the Socialists made an agreement with him and Zusi will take it upon himself.
After all, he may have been offended so far, because his requests to ease the conditions of his detention, namely his placement under house arrest, have remained unheard.
While perpetrators of economic offences, who have done much greater damage that have not yet been paid, are free to walk, or at least wait for the end of their proceedings in their homes, he does not have this option.
Psychology of trumping: why do we spend more money?
During auctions, customers are willing to spend more money, overestimate goods.
The neurological and economic foundations of this well-known and seemingly irrational behavior have been uncovered by researchers in Neurology and Economics at the University of New York.
According to their study, published in the journal Science, people don't want to fall behind in the eBay auction competition, so they prefer to pay even too high a price.
Using an experimental auction, the research attempted to map the scheme of this "game" in the nervous system.
One group of volunteers were betting, the other group participated in a played auction, using a method of functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) to examine the active areas of the brain of experimental persons.
In both games, the participants could win money, but when playing at the auction, the important element of which is to trumpet the other side, a whole located under the cerebral cortex, called striatum, began to work intensely during the loss.
In addition to many other functions, this brain region plays a role in the "self-rewarding" mechanism of the nervous system.
According to the researchers, it is therefore possible to explain the acceleration of the price by trying to avoid a sharp reaction of the striato.
To support their theory, members of the research team of economists carried out a financial cross-cutting survey.
Under different conditions, three groups licited against one partner.
The control group was simply communicated what the real value of the product is, and they made bets.
In addition, members of the next group were promised that if they came out of the game victoriously, they would still receive 15 dollars for the reward.
On the contrary, the third group received 15 dollars already before the auction, but in the event of a loss, its members also lost this money.
Therefore, the difference between the two groups consisted in the fact that one was highlighted by winning and the other by losing.
However, members of a group playing "against loss" were willing to go higher than they did in the control and "winning" group, which confirms the neurologist's hypothesis.
"These results cannot be inferred from any pre-existing economic theory.
Until now, they had explained phenomena observable at auctions of the joys of victory and the avoidance of risk.
It wasn't until neurological imaging methods helped us resolve the contradiction between these two theories so that we have now come up with a third explanation," said Andrew Schotter, a professor at the Faculty of Economics at the University of New York.
Hepatitis: when to see a doctor?
Approximately half a billion people around the world suffer from chronic hepatitis B or C infection, in Hungary this chronic liver disease is listed among the main causes of death.
The number of domestic cases of hepatitis infection is estimated at one hundred thousand, but only seven thousand of them are treated. The reason for this is that patients often do not know that they are infected.
The name hepatitis is a comprehensive naming of infectious liver damage caused by various viruses.
There are many similarities in their symptoms, symptomatic is inflammation of the liver and jaundice, but the agents of the disease and the diseases caused by them differ from each other.
Hepatitis viruses, depending on the agents of the disease, spread in two main ways: hepatitis A and E to the digestive tract (for example, infected foods, water), hepatitis B, C, D and other viruses with blood and body fluids (sperm, vaginal secretion, saliva).
In any case, travelers should protect themselves by vaccinating against virus A and B, there is no vaccine against type C virus.
Symptoms of hepatitis A appear two to three weeks after ingestion of infected food, usually accompanied by nausea, weakness, abdominal problems, urgency for vomiting.
After that, jaundice is formed on the skin and whites of the eyes, lightening the stools and darkening the urine. Treatment can take two to three weeks.
Every year around the world, a million people die from a more serious type of this disease, liver cancer and liver cirrhosis caused by hepatitis B.
The main cause is that, according to estimates, only one in tenth infected person is medically treated.
Also, hepatitis C can have very serious consequences: if it is not treated, it can lead to liver cirrhosis or liver cancer.
Most of the infected do not even know about their disease, the cause of the disease destroys the liver cells insidiously, and so the first symptoms occur in many cases only in 20-30 years.
Therefore, it is very important early prevention, the implementation of regular depistation.
In three groups, this disease occurs a lot more often than on average: among people who underwent blood transfusion before 1991, among drug users and among people with tattoos.
Currently, the estimated global number of individuals infected with chronic hepatitis C virus is 200 million.
Since the disease can prove already a routine laboratory examination, a person should contact his/her general practitioner if he/she notices any symptom of unknown origin.
With early detection, the disease is treatable, the virus can be removed from the body, preventing contagiousness and preventing the development of cancer.
On September 3rd, World Hepatitis Day was first organized worldwide in 2004.
Its aim is to draw attention to the chronic inflammation of the liver caused by these viruses, to the causes of its development and to the importance of early recognition of the disease.
Those who believe in god don’t suffer that much?
According to the results of recent research, the belief in god works as a certain analgesic that may have made death easier for Catholic martyrs.
Scientists from Oxford University conducted an interesting experiment, examining how a person's belief in a god influences the perception of pain, Britain's Telegraph reported.
As part of a rather bizarre survey, they subjected 12 Catholic believers and the same number of atheists to electric shocks, while all observed a painting depicting the Virgin Mary.
According to the results, Catholic believers were able to rule out much of the
Thanks to a technique examining the human brain in detail, it has also become clear that Catholics have been able to activate the part of their brain, which is responsible for regulating pain.
The research is the first in a series of research, in which scientists, philosophers and theologians from different parts of the university also participate in among academics.
Exactly the test proceeded by attaching a sparkling tool to the left hand of the test subject, which emitted electric shocks.
The researchers asked the participants of the experiment to closely observe two paintings: Sassoferrat's Virgin Mary dating from the 17th century, and a Lady with the prismatic of Leonardo da Vinci, painted in the 15th century.
The authors of the research hoped that the view of the Virgin Mary would provoke a certain state of piety among believers, while they chose da Vinci's image because it is not much different from the first and gives a reassuring impression.
The experimental subjects spent half an hour in an MRI scanner and during this time received 20 electroshocks in four successions, while separately watching the image with a religious or general theme.
The Catholics said they felt "safe" at the sight of the Virgin Mary, as if they were "taken care of" and "calmed down, they were filled with peace."
Watching an image with a religious theme, they felt 12 percent less pain, according to their statement, than when they had Leonardo's work in front of them.
Psychologist Miguel Farias, who also participated in the research, believes that a similar effect can be achieved with any highly impressive image even in atheists, but it is only necessary to find the right photograph.
"We would have to find an image that they will perceive very positively, from the position of mother or father," the psychologist said.
Anglican Bishop Tom Wright welcomed the research results.
"Practicing the faith should change, and in many cases it will also change our personality," Wright added.
After such great results, it is only a question of what effect it would have on the regulation of the pain of believers if they had been showned by a Madonna by Leonard or a painter of a similar caliber.
Would a look at a high-quast Virgin Mary achieving even better results?
You're what you're going to share
On their 10th birthday, Google can declare that people can find it in their pockets at least as well as in the sky above their heads; they know where they have been on vacation, what professional examinations go and what they are interested in.
According to Eric Schmidt, one of the company's bosses, that's still not enough.
However, when it comes to sceptics making a parallel with Orwellian's dark vision, the very essence of Web 2.0 is not clear.
Eric Schmidt recently told the Financial Times that Google’s main goal is to collect even more personal information about users, which is necessary for Google’s founders to be able to stick to their original intention: “Let’s organize all the information in the world!”
According to a Google official, even after ten years of world-class work, the company is only at the beginning of being able to work with all the information.
When asked what will happen in five years, Schmidt replied that Google would be able to answer questions such as "what should I do tomorrow?" or "what kind of work would be useful for me?" - however, software will not be able to answer such seemingly simple questions until they know enough information about users.
Not all Internet users know very well that during the Internet search, the search words are accurately captured, as well as what IP address was searched for and to which pages the interested parties moved after the search.
Thanks to Personalized Search launched two years ago, for example, users can keep a history of their surfing, look back at what they were looking for and what they clicked on, thanks to which, in the long run, findings adapted much more directly to them are generated - the same logic will be based on the Recommendations service, through which the system will offer products and services based on previously mentioned and retained preferences.
What if my data is used against me, what if someone looks at what I'm dealing with? - they ask users who are scared when looking at Google's data monopoly.
In most cases, the extent of the potential power represented by the company is more threatening, the counter-reaction does not provoke knowledge of guilt in Internet users, fear of punishment, the desire for freedom or even rebellion against power.
However, it is a confirmed fact that all search terms are not only captured, but in justified cases they can also be issued to a third party, in such legal cases the personality rights of Internet users do not protect anything.
The most well-known recent example was probably the coordinated action against Brazilian paedophiles, during which Google provided the Brazilian government with information that allowed the identification of paedophiles from Orkut community sites, established through him.
However, Internet users who are worried about personal rights could still be relieved in May, when the firm, hand-in-hand with the electronic rights organization EFF, successfully proved its truth in a dispute with the FBI: Google must not be forced to provide any information secretly requested by the FBI in the future.
(At the same time, the federal government successfully won nearly fifty thousand web addresses and thousands of search terms, which it obtained, citing reinforcement years ago by the Adopted Child Protection Act, but still failed to get lists of search items of individual users, respectively, complete lists of websites and keywords involving a period of two months.)
Google's opinion is quite contradictory: it is whipped up by those who are worried about providing their personal information, because they feel that their intimate sphere and private life will be subjected to harm by starting to use Google's services, and acting out against it with a revolt that belongs to multinational firms with a conservative image.
And so far, the U.S. geek culture and its wild offshoot, grouping enthusiastic computer fans, activists and users, appearing in many countries, sees in it a new generation of large-scale corporations, "corporation that's creeping out now," unlike his rival Microsoft, considered because of Windows to be the conquerors of the world, however they both fight for the same goals.
This may be due to the fact that Google, unlike Microsoft, doesn't require identification code from its users and doesn't want money for its apps; an open, free and constantly fresh source of information and a package of programs acts as a much more accessible, youthful, "color" than the practice of an official software giant.
However, this may also be due to the fact that geeks accurately understand the very problematic relationship between the Internet and personality rights, which has brought with it just one of the most popular keywords of recent years: web 2.0.
Web 2.0 is not about anything other than cooperation, that we deliberately leave traces behind and use the channels of communication at our disposal, and although their scope is different for everyone, the essence remains the same: we show up.
The average young person can have a profile on several community pages from Facebook to Xanga, which they can easily connect with other services, such as Twitter or the last.fm app, recording the music listened to them, all of which can be connected to navigation systems such as FriendFeed or Blip.fm, in addition to writing a blog, on which will automatically appear its latest photogalleries, which it saves
At the same time, we can read about a lot of exemplary cases, from which it shines: in the morning, when looking at a flood of photos of mutual friends, it easily finds out with whom her scary boyfriend spent the evening, or the company can fire his worker only because he recounts his sincere opinion of the top management on his blog: one thing is certain, the concept of private life has been completely re-evaluated today.
One who lives an online life consists of what it shows of itself, so we could define the basic thesis behind the behavior of today's youth.
And anyone who wants to hide something, or at least avoid the Google monopoly, has the option (starting with Scrooglem, which uses Google's search system, however, does not intentionally leave traces).
For example, a large number of people do not deliberately register on community pages so that they cannot be found. Google's online applications from text editor to photoeditor and news portal can be exchanged for alternative versions that aren't connected to giant server parks and that don't build the highest-qualified profile.
Public opinion does not secretly await when Google will show teeth.
With a current value of 156 billion dollars, the company is worth more than IBM (140 billion dollars), Intel (124 billion dollars) or Coca Cola (110 billion dollars), AdWords/AdSense advertising space threatens the investment of advertising agencies, and the services implemented at an ever-accelerating pace seriously make the lives of not only Microsoft, but also many other software designers.
Critical observers, however, also find counterpoints causing a disturbing impression: for example, the top management completely rejected the protests of the employees after raising twice the fee for a company nursery, according to the company’s arbitrary daily meals, were too expensive, according to the company’s management, but all this is lost in addition to the fact that Google bent before the Chinese government in 2006 and, in the sense of their agreement, filters out every finding, which is contrary to a good.
One of the company’s internal rules is that it never advertises new services in advance, only when they can be used, but it is certain that the expected problems of the coming months and years may be stirring the world in new places: while some believe that Google’s project focused on scanning and archiving books and magazines takes on such proportions that the company will soon have something to do with the U.S. Supreme Court, others are terrified of its health system, because we can build our own
The fact that Google compiles a genetic database can then serve as a good starting material even for subsequent generations of sci-fi writers, Cory Doctorow's famous novel "Scroogled" describes with Orwellian reminiscences the future, in which it is difficult to approach a person easily only with the help of data from Google.
Twie-time Ózd killer helped remove two juveniles
Two juveniles were interviewed by the police in the case of the two-time homicide in Ózd, according to reasonable suspicion they were assisting with the removal of traces of this crime, a spokesperson for Borsod's High Police Headquarters said on Tuesday.
Persons have confessed to the activities from which they were suspected and are currently accused of abetting a crime.
Both juveniles, according to MTI information two girls, are prosecuted at large.
Advocating a crime is punishable by imprisonment for a period of up to 5 years.
Suspected of a double murder in Ózd, a 23-year-old local man, was taken into provisional custody on Sunday.
He is suspected of a criminal offence pre-planned multiple murders for self-interested reasons.
During the investigation, it emerged that the 23-year-old young man owed the 53-year-old man a larger sum.
The suspect did not want to repay his debt, so he lured his creditor last Wednesday to one establishment in Ózdu, where he first killed a man and then his 9-year-old daughter.
According to MTI information, the young man prepared in advance to commit the crime, as evidenced by the fact that he committed the murder with a gun hidden at the scene.
Weapons and the circumstances of its use are, with the involvement of experts, examined by the police.
The Warsaw Stock Exchange made an offer to buy the Prague Stock Exchange
Two hundred million euros - approximately five billion Czech crowns - were offered by the Warsaw Stock Exchange for the purchase of a hundred percent package of shares of the Prague Stock Exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, chairman of the Warsaw Stock Exchange, confirmed the offer made by the Polish side despite the fact that the Czechs had previously excluded the Warsaw Stock Exchange from the circle of possible new owners of the Prague Stock Exchange.
This is due to the fact that the owner of the Warsaw Stock Exchange is the state.
99 percent of the shares are in the hands of the Polish state, which wishes to sell 60 per cent of securities in a few months.
As the Czech economic daily E15 writes, the offer of Poles represents the highest amount that has been offered so far on behalf of the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to Czech newspapers, the Viennese Stock Exchange, the Nasdaq, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and Deutsche Börse have so far submitted an offer.
Petr Koblic, the head of the Prague Stock Exchange, said last week that the Warsaw Stock Exchange would be the worst new owner of all those interested, and did not rule out that this could lead to the disappearance of the Prague stock exchange in the future.
The value of the Prague Stock Exchange is estimated by stock market experts at 5-10 billion.
Under shareholders' agreement, around 90 percent of the shares could be sold off.
The largest owner of the Prague stock exchange is Patria Finance, an investment bank with an approximately 25 percent share, followed by Česká spořitelna with 14.7 and Tiger Holding Four S. a. r. l. with 13.5 percent.
The financial crisis from the point of view of European newspapers
In his Tuesday comments on the deepening international financial crisis, Europe's leading newspapers are already reminiscent of "9/11."
For the global financial disaster, we can thank the American president, raises the accusation of the Roman La Repubblica. The president was not even able to assemble his own party in support of that hastily slapped bill aimed at remedying this desperate situation...
Instead of the new Pearl Harbor, which resembled billionaire and investor Warren Buffett, we could be talking more about the second 9/11.
Also, part of Madrid's press sees the culprit in Washington.
According to El Mundo, it has brought the world economy to the brink of the collapse of the country’s lack of political leadership.
The dangers of the collapse of the U.S. financial system are steadily growing, for which the whole world will ultimately pay.
Bush’s plan wasn’t perfect, but there’s a danger that Washington won’t offer a political tool to solve the crisis.
The world economy hangs in the air - just as one of the greatest transformations in the history of capitalism takes place.
The threat can no longer be overlooked, according to Tages-Anzeiger.
So far, individual states can toss a life-saving circle for staggering money institutions, and taxpayers can save them from collapse.
However, if other banks collapse like dominatrix cubes, then neither money nor further improvisations will be enough.
In the long-term perspective, the Member States of the European Union need to move past their national egoism and unify control over fragmented financial markets.
On Monday alone, stagnant money institutions received more than 100 billion euros, according to the Vienna-based Der Standard, according to which fears that, like Americans, European taxpayers will have to reach deep into their pockets in order to solve the crisis, increasingly real.
Until now, the European Union has not dealt seriously with the crisis that is rolling through the old continent like tsunamis.
"Rescuers don't have to wave hundreds of billions of euros right away, but it would be a good idea if they're fixed, in which case it's reasonable to reach for taxpayers' money in order to avoid even greater damage.
An intelligent car imitates a bumblebee's flight
What would be the best method of developing technologies capable of preventing a collision, if not imitating detectors of obstacles occurring in the world of nature?
Bees, for example.
The initial strategic idea of the surprise project presented at CEATEC in Japan was inspired by bumblebees to Nissan engineers. It was about how to build a new generation of collisioning systems.
In collaboration with the University of Tokyo, Nissan has engineered the BR23C biomimetic robotic control system, a robotic mini-car that is equipped with the characteristics of bees, with the ultimate goal of helping to create a system that can eliminate all collisions.
"During the flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space, which actually closely resembles our safety protective packaging," Kazuhiro Doi, the brand's general manager for technology PR and R&D, explained at the exhibition.
An important role is played by folded eyes, which allow bees to see in all directions at a viewing angle of more than 300 degrees.
To copy the function of the folded eye, engineers reached for the idea of a laser rangefinder.
It identifies obstacles at a distance of up to 2 meters in front of the BR23C in the 180-degree field of view, calculates their distance and sends a signal to the on-board microprocessor, which is immediately transformed into an operation aimed at preventing a collision.
The obstacle is captured within a fraction of a second, then the robotized car begins to imitate the movements of the bee, and by turning the wheels to the extent necessary to prevent a collision, it immediately changes direction.
Unlike any existing system, this protrusional manoeuvre takes place in an absolutely instinctive way.
“If that wasn’t the case, a robotic car wouldn’t be able to react quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Ando, Head of the Mobility Laboratory and the robotic car project.
It must respond instinctively and immediately, as this technology suits the most vulnerable and inneural layer of the safety protective cover, inside which the collision is currently considered inevitable.
The whole process has to reflect what a bee is doing to avoid other bees.
This process must not take longer than the blink of an eye.
However, unlike bees, this movement cannot be directed up, down or diagonally, it can only be two-dimensional and only in the direction in which the wheels can be rotated.
Instead of the endless possibilities offered by bees during avoidance, in the case of a robotic car, only the possibility of revolving motion, acceleration and deceleration was applied.
The BR23C uses lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and have a higher energy density, meaning they have a longer service life than lithium-ion variants.
According to Ando, instinctive, intelligent reaction is important, not the ability to process, much less data storage.
This mechanism must process and respond only to a few incoming signals per second.
There is no need for it to be stored and responded using the intuitive logic based on data from previous manoeuvres, therefore it constantly uses only memory with the size of a few kilobytes.
It doesn't need memoirs with a giga- or megabyte capacity.
This is the first stem to develop a technology used for immediate avoidance, which, according to Nissan's estimate, could reach mass-produced cars within a decade.
Another possibility of application may be the connection of the unit with the GPS satellite navigation system, which, after avoiding in the way of lying obstacles, would lead the motorist to the specified final destination.
A dear child already has a name
The 700-billion-dollar bailout law, which is supposed to be divided into several repayments aimed at buying low-quality banks, is already in writing.
In the first round, half the amount will be used.
After further talks, which didn't end until Saturday after midnight U.S
"We've made significant progress," said Nancy Pelosi, Democratic Speaker of the House of Representatives, according to which all that is left is to draw up an agreement.
Compromises on the package have also been endorsed by both presidential candidates Barack Obama and John McCain.
Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson said: “The programme will work and be effective, but further action needs to be taken.”
However, the written version of the package was published before the Asian markets opened at 8 p.m. Washington time (i.e. at two o'clock in the morning of Central European time), but did not cause any special rise on the stock exchanges.
On Monday, the House of Representatives will vote on a draft legal standard hearing in the name of the Emergency Economic Stabilization Act, which is supposed to establish an agenda.
President George W. Bush spoke with Pelosi on Saturday night, after which news of the deal was also greeted at the White House.
"We are satisfied with the progress made at night, and we very much appreciate the efforts of both parties to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economy," White House spokesman Tony Fratto said.
Moreover, the program differs from the Bush administration's original proposal, in which it was supposed to spend a full sum of 700 billion dollars on the purchase of low-quality mortgage-backed securities.
It is now possible to spend a maximum amount of £700 billion in the first round, so that 250 billion will be used immediately after Congress is expected to decide the matter on Monday, and the Treasury will buy mortgage-backed securities that are rated low-quality, the fate of the remaining 100 billion dollars will then depend on the president's decision.
The program will be controlled by a special corps, of which the chairman of the Federal Reserve Bank will also be a member.
Spending the second half of 700 billion dollars will depend on Congress's next decision.
The Ministry of Finance can apply combined tactics within the meaning of the law: it can simultaneously purchase low-quality loans, mortgages and mortgage-backed securities, or other "poisonous" loans.
The purchase can also take place directly as part of the auctions, the ministry's competent officials have said.
The legal standard will allow the companies concerned to participate in an insurance program in which the Ministry of Finance grants them a guarantee for funds representing a reason for concern, for which companies will then pay a price corresponding to the amount of expected damages.
According to Henry Paulson, the law provides the necessary means to spend up to 700 billion dollars to meet the urgent demands of the U.S. financial system, whether it will be a large-scale purchase of the wrong funds, their insurance, or the prevention of systemic risk associated with the collapse of one of the big money institutions.
"We will take the necessary steps to be prepared to introduce the law as soon as it is signed," he added.
On the contrary, bank bosses who have run into trouble and are waiting to help the state can count on the fact that the government will reduce their severances - which, in overseas slang, is called simply "golden parachute."
There will be a limitation of their payments only in certain cases, in addition, these restrictions will depend on the method of assistance provided by the Ministry.
Restrictions essentially mean that if the ministry buys as part of the auction, a tax premium will be imposed on gold parachutes if fallen bank directors aren't sent straight to retirement.
In the event of a direct purchase of low-quality loans, the government will intervene aggressively in the interest that money for management is not exaggerated.
It should be added that the already existing contracts for gold parachutes will not change, the restriction applies only to new treaties, which is supposed to be another stimulus for participation in the programme.
"This is the first case in U.S. history where the legal restrictions on the remuneration of CEOs will be in effect," said Barney Frank, Chairman of the House Financial Services Committee.
In addition, in the banks participating in the program, the state will have the right to acquire a share, thereby reducing the risk of taxpayers, because once the economy and the rescued bank stand on their feet, they can even profit from this transaction.
The plan includes that in the next five years, the president must submit a proposal to Congress setting out how taxpayer-expected losses from the financial sector can be returned.
Of course, there have also been voices on both sides expressing doubt in connection with the plan.
"That's not what U.S
His Democratic colleague from Ohia, Mary Kaptur, said those responsible for the collapse of Wall Street had urged the passage.
Industrial prices continued to rise
Domestic sales prices in the industry increased by 0.1 percent in August 2008 due to the previous month, compared to August 2007 by 12.9 percent, the Central Statistical Office announced.
Export sales prices in the industry were higher by 1.1 percent compared to July 2008, down 3.9 percent year-on-year.
Production prices in the industry were higher by 0.7 percent compared to the previous month as a cause of changes in domestic and export prices, rising 3.2 percent year-on-year.
Domestic sales prices in the industry have risen only minimally due to the previous month, by 0.1 percent.
Also, domestic sales prices in the manufacturing industries increased slightly, by 0.1 percent, compared to July.
The biggest increase in prices took place in August 2008 in the leather and footwear industry (2.8 percent), mainly due to a month-on-month increase in prices in the bagging and saddlery products sector by 4.6 percent, respectively.
The higher price level of plastic and chemical raw materials caused a significant monthly increase in prices in the chemical and production sector (1.6 percent).
There have been price reductions in the oil-processing sector (1.4 percent), electrical machinery and appliances (0.9 percent), as well as - to a minimal extent - in the wood-processing (0.1 percent) sector.
Prices in paper production and publishing and printing activities have not changed.
In other sectors of the manufacturing industry, we have measured price increases in the range of 0.2 - 0.9 percent.
In the electricity, gas and water production and distribution sector, which carries a lot of weight in the domestic sales of the industry, prices rose by 0.3 percent in August, mainly due to an officially determined increase in gas prices running from 1 July.
In a 12-month comparison of domestic sales in individual sectors of the manufacturing industry, prices increased the most in the professional oil processing sector (32.4 percent).
It was possible to observe 13.2 percent in the automotive production sector, 11 percent increase in prices in the production of food products, beverages and tobacco products, which have a decisive weight.
There have been reductions in the textile and textile and textile production sectors (2.5 percent) and the production of electrical machinery and apparatus (0.3 percent).
In other sectors of the manufacturing industry, the rate of price growth was between 0.6 and 9.1 percent.
Overall, prices in the manufacturing industry have risen by 10.2 percent over the past year.
Prices in the electricity, gas and water production and distribution sectors outperformed prices from the previous year by 18.7 percent.
In terms of subdivision by industry focus, the highest monthly price increase (0.7 percent) was reflected in domestic sales in domestic sales in sectors engaged in tangible asset production.
In the consumer goods sectors, prices increased by 0.3 percent, prices were unchanged in the energy of the processing and further processing sectors due to the previous month.
Due to the same month of the previous year, the biggest price increases could be observed in the energy of the processing and processing sectors (16.8 percent) in August 2008.
In the consumer goods industries, we measured a price increase of 6.8 percent, for tangible assets we measured a price decrease of 1.4 percent.
Export sales prices of industrial products measured in forints increased by 1.1 percent in August compared to the previous month, falling 3.9 percent in the 12-month comparison.
Our domestic currency weakened 1.7 percent relative to the euro in one month, then by 7.1 per cent relative to the dollar, rising 7.5 percent relative to the euro compared to August 2007.
Next year without a visa to the United States?
According to Kinga Göncz, there is a great hope that the U.S.-Hungarian Visa Waiver Agreement will come into force around January 12, the Minister of Foreign Affairs has spoken about it in Chicago before the leaders of the local Hungarian community.
