Barack Obama and McCain are in favor of a bank rescue package
Barack Obama and John McCain, presidential candidates of both major U.S. parties, on Sunday consistently assured the government's 700-billion-dollar bail-out package, but there are also politicians calling for Treasury's resignation.
After a several-day marathon meeting of Congress and administration officials, it was announced early on Sunday morning: a basic agreement was reached on the bank rescue package.
The financial package, which aims to remove the Wall Street crisis and the frozen credit market, has become the central issue of the presidential campaign.
Both Obama and McCain are identically described as more suitable of both candidates to lead the country out of the economic crisis.
According to the latest polls at the same time as they came to the forefront of the economic issue, Obama regained a certain advantage and McCain's lead, which he secured after the Republican convent with Sarah Palin's nominations for the post of vice-presidence.
Obama expressed hope on Sunday at a pre-election rally in Detroit that a 700-billion-dollar package bill would be swiftly passed by Congress.
He stressed that the proposal contained passages relating to the protection of consumers, which it also supports.
"We have to act immediately, responsibly (...) your places, your savings, the entire industry, the stability of the whole economy there is a danger," he said in front of the 35-thousand-dollar crowd.
He didn't miss an opportunity to call McCain's ideas about the economy obsolete.
McCain, in an interview provided by ABC, explained: "It's going to bother everyone a bit (...) but doing nothing is just not possible."
According to the Republican senator, the proposal includes the establishment of a commission supervisinging Treasury secretary and restrictions on severances for the senior officials he insisted on.
Obama told CBS that McCain had not had a more important role in the negotiations, he himself, on the contrary, was in daily contact with the secretary.
House Speaker Nancy Pelosi said on Sunday that the House of Representatives would vote on the bill perhaps as early as Monday.
Overview of the press of the German language area
The right-wing camp is intact, only the voters within it have become more confident - so it analyzes the CSU's weak CSU result in Bavaria Der Spiegel.
The Haiders are ready to rule Austria to prevent a red-and-black coalition.
Der Spiegel (spiegel.de)
In the online edition of the left-wing German news magazine, Franz Walter discusses in an article titled "The Bavarian Revolution devours his fathers" the causes of the dramatic defeat of the CSU.
According to a well-known political scientist, CSU erosion began much before the sad duo Beckstein-Huber took office.
For decades, Christian socialists have been restoring Bavaria - and it is these modern citizens who are now turning away from them.
Alternating the period is the problem of the entire Christian democracy, and thus of Angela Merkel.
The debacle was not entirely surprising to the CSU, but on Saturday the trial ended, which could be assumed for almost exactly ten years: the indisputable hegemony of one state party ended.
A decade ago, in 1998, CSU landed at 47.7 percent, for the first time since the 1950s below the 50 percent threshold.
This was repeated three years ago in the Bundestag elections in 2005, which is still in Stoibero's often commemorated period.
The cause is the modernization that the CSU has consistently carried out in this country and that devours its parents.
Sociologists have predicted this for 40 years, but they were very wrong at pace. However, secularization, educational results, the settlement of Germans from the east and from the north in the developing regions of Bavaria could not have been without consequences for the loyalty of CSU voters.
Modernization is associated with the growth of possibilities, which also has its visible political consequences.
The right-wing camp is intact, only the voters inside it have become more confident, more critical, willful.
It is also clear that in recent weeks the CSU has become the most distanched by classical civic classes; people in the liberal profession, tradesmen, individuals with graduation and with higher education.
By doing so, CSU's support has already shrunk below 40 percent.
Die Presse (diepresse.com)
In its article entitled "Who, with whom? Party presidents are restrained in the issues of the coalition", deals with the confused internal political situation arising as a result of the Austrian elections.
SPÖ wishes to negotiate with ÖVP.
The ÖVP forbids interference by SPÖ in its internal affairs.
