Chairman BZÖ Jörg Haider once again appeared open in all directions.
"In order to prevent a red-and-black coalition, we are prepared to take government responsibility," Haider said in a statement.
It is necessary to break the vicious cycle of poverty and segregation
The European Equality Summit, hosted this year by France’s current presiding state, has begun in Paris today.
Lívia Járóková, a member of the European People's Party for the Fidesz movement, as one of the main speakers of professional debate in her message addressed to the plenary, turned her attention to segregation in education, because it is one of the most serious injustices affecting Romani children, and, moreover, the most serious obstacle to their application.
Xavier Bertrand, French Minister of Labour and Social Affairs and Vladimír Špidla, European Commissioner for Employment, Social Affairs and Equal Opportunities, opened the top conference on behalf of the French Presidency.
They were among the invited representatives of governments, European social partners, the most important European rights organizations and civic associations active in the field of equal opportunities.
According to the press office of Fidesz Járóková, a group of MEPs, pointed out that, in addition to the discrimination that occurs when recruiting the workforce, Romani people are forced out of the European labour market primarily due to their low education.
"The vast majority of young Roma do not have secondary education and, due to the poor quality of primary education, only a small proportion of them can achieve a higher level of education."
Járóková further emphasized that education as a fundamental human right has a key role in the application of other human rights.
Investing in the early education of Romani children can strengthen the economy of the Member States of the European Union.
According to MEPs, it is necessary to confirm the awareness of these contexts in the widestest circles - among both Roma and non-Romani children.
Indeed, the sooner a Romani student starts school, the greater the chance to become a productive member of the labor market; through income taxes and excise taxes, they contribute to the national budget.
"Romani people have the right to ensure the right to quality education and to guarantee the elimination of their low-quality and separate education," said Lívia Járóková.
According to MEPs, the cohesion of the European Union is needed with an emphasis on the objective and negotiations in order to urgently accelerate the processes of desegregation of schools, for which correct compliance with existing and planned anti-discrimination regulations is appropriate.
"It is necessary to break the vicious cycle of poverty, segregation and unemployment, it is necessary to give Romani children the opportunities given by democratic space, so that they, as full citizens, can participate in the future of Europe," Járóková demanded.
The objective of the European Equality Summit, held annually since last year’s meeting in Berlin, is guaranteed to everyone in the "European Year of Equal Opportunities", a stand-alone forum to achieve the objectives of equality and the elimination of discrimination set at the level of the whole of Europe and individual Member States.
CSU: Temporary Grace
Huber-Beckstein will decide the fate of Huber-Beckstein at the end of October
Despite the disgraceful electoral debacle, there have not yet been heads, the debate on personal issues has so far been adjourned by Bavarian CSU leaders.
Party leader Erwin Huber and Provincial Prime Minister Günther Beckstein can still remain in their seats, the question is when this pardon will last.
The fate of tandem, which suffered a catastrophic 17 percent loss of votes, will be decided on the last weekend of October by the party's extraordinary congress.
In any case, the members of the highly criticized duo have been told that they will not cling to their posts with all their strength.
They tried to reassure angry members of the faction and base, who, due to the loss of the absolute majority, demanded a day after the "Bavarian tsunami" to draw personal consequences.
Five years ago - true, still under Edmund Stoiber at the height of his career - the Christian-Social Union gained 60.7 percent, now, a year after a coup against Stoiber, but her strength was only 43.4 percent.
The CSU has been a "self-ruler" in Bavaria producing 18 percent of Germany's GDP for 46 years.
Conservatives in the southern Länder, which is crucial to the Hungarian economy, continue to have a high position, but in the future they will be forced into coalition.
One of the first signs of reorganisation within the party is the fact that a member of the narrow intelligence team, which will start probing negotiations on a possible coalition of the Munich government with the liberal FDP, respectively with the right-wing Free Voters group, will also be the vice-chairman and federal agriculture minister Horst Seehofer, who is considered a possible successor.
It is irony of the fate that Gabriele Pauli, whose appearance against Stoiber was used by Huber-Beckstein to carry out a coup, but then with an ambitious communal politician made a ruthlessly short process.
CSU leaders are now concentrating all their energy on how to stabilize Beckstein.
In the transition period, the active role will also belong to Stoiber, who until now has spun threads from the background, the former provincial prime minister and former CSU chairman on September 28 called the bitterest day of his political career.
On Monday Huber made a few sarcastic remarks to the CDU, according to him they would have deserved much more support from the sister party.
Other CSU politicians have interpreted defeat as a warning for the CDU and Angela Merkel.
After the September 2009 Bundestag elections, Merkel will not be able to become chancellor if the CSU, which played a significant role in national success in 2005, does not stand on its feet.
According to Merkel, who found it hard to hide her disappointment because of the fiasco, the loss of CSU positions will not have a negative impact on Berlin's grand coalition.
Which, of course, is relative: Treating the wounded CSU won't be easier, and the Social Democrat crisis continues.
The SPD achieved its weakest result in Bavaria with 18.6 percent since 1946.
Lukashenko:opposition - 110:0
In Sunday's elections, not a single opposition MP made it to the Belarusian parliament.
According to Russian observers, this can be explained by the well-being and stability prevailing in the country led by Alexander Lukashenko, but according to the Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe (OSCE), the illegalities that occurred during the counting of votes.
The OSCE, which sent 450 observers from 43 countries, did not recognize the election.
Although the 110-member legislature of the 10-million-dollar country has lacked the opposition well for ten years, the opposition united under the name United Democratic Forces (ODS) before yesterday's elections seriously hoped that Lukashenko, who, on the basis of an amendment to the constitution in elections with a debatable course in 2006 and for the fourth time, will "allow" to appear in parliament.
The ODS also abandoned the previously considered boycott.
In the background of the opposition’s expectation, Belarus – which took credit to alleviate Russia’s ever-increasing imports of natural gas to mitigate the ever-increasing impact of Russian natural gas imports, also contributed to bringing Beltransgaz, which owns pipelines passing through the country – wanted to improve relations with the European Union, where, due to the Belarusian political establishment rated anti-democratic Lukashenko and other politicians.
The release had previously pointed out that the OSCE had placed emphasis on positive shifts when evaluating the election campaign, and not on deficiencies - for example, on banning candidacy for a third of opposition candidates.
However, Monday’s OSCE flash report already had reservations about the campaign, during which opposition candidates were basically unable to appear in the press, especially to the fact that the opposition could not be present to count the votes.
At the same time, according to the opposition, improvements can be felt in the political system: political prisoners have been freed, and Minsk is willing to embark on smaller confrontations with Moscow when it continues to refuse to recognize South Ossetia and Abkhazia from Georgia, even though Russia, with which Belarus forms a union state on paper, has considered these two territories independent for a month.
"We have taken two steps forward and one back, but the most important thing is that the dialogue between Belarus and the EU has begun," Alexander Milinkevich told our newspaper.
According to an opposition candidate from the 2006 presidential election, it is desirable for this to remain so, even though "the power has become afraid to enter the insignificant parliament of a single member of the opposition."
Looking at the opposition's results, he doesn't plan protest actions, rather he's trying to deliver his message to voters about the need for economic reforms.
"Of course we have a lot of problems with that, we have to print opposition newspapers in Russia, the distribution puts obstacles by local authorities, but I still see that more and more people, even among the elderly, are supporting the change," Milinkevich said, pointing out that Lukashenko's regime, which considers the creation of social security as its greatest success, does not consider it the right or the most officially supported class of pensioners.
"Due to the indebtedness of the country and the growth of the trade deficit, it is only a matter of time when the social security of the 'inducing silence of the cemetery' collapses," Milinkevich said.
Unlike an opposition politician, Russian observers rated the election as democratic.
Sergei Lebedev, head of the Commonwealth of Independent States mission, told Russian news agency Ria Novosti that "people don't believe in words, but actions," so the absence of opposition is nothing more than a testament to the maturity of Belarusian voters.
Brunetta: "From now breakfast before work"
It is for the first time in the history of Chigi Palace that a revolving door has been established when employees arrive and leave.
However, from now on, this new measure against cunning people will begin to work, against those who inject themselves and then go for coffee during working hours.
A novelty against sloths was introduced today by the Minister of Public Administration, Renat Brunetta, and the Secretary-General of the Presidential Council, Maur Masi.
"Revolving doors - Brunetta says - are a tool for better work, higher performance, more attendances and more services," the minister specifies.
"A new period of responsibility and transparency will now open.
It's about reformism," Brunetta emphasizes.
The Chigi Palace will complete the introduction in all buildings of the council presidency.
For Masi, "the choice of revolving doors is also important from a symbolic point of view."
There were no objections on the part of the staff, assures the Secretary-General: "This was a common procedure with the workers and the cooperation of all".
In short, no more breakfast in business hours; no more coffee and no croissant.
Brunetta smiles and says, "there's nothing better than a proper cappuccino at seven o'clock in the morning."
During one conference at the Higher Vocational School for Public Administration
in Rome, the minister will eventually announce that within three months the absence of public employees for the disease has decreased by 50%, that is, by 60 thousand more people a year at work."
And it will add that it is necessary for the Italian public administration to become more efficient
“Its payout dynamic – the minister explains – has doubled relative to the private sector over the past 8-10 years and is far higher than the inflation percentage.”
In the meantime, thousands of public employees have come together to reject Brunette's decree's norms in accordance with a Codacons-backed application.
“While the Minister for Public Administration is today presenting figures about a lower absence from work due to an illness – he writes a consumer protection association – a nation of ‘diligent sloths’ is steadily growing.”
In Italy, we have thousands of productive and correct employees in public administration who have gathered to declare war on the illegitimate norms contained in the decree."
Tara will be protested primarily against the exclusion of sick leave surcharges, which depends on the activity; the extension of the time in which it is possible to inspect the sick, within what time the control visits will be carried out, and against the other standards included in the new ministerial provisions.
Occupational accidents, Napolitano: "Resolute prevention"
Every day there will be 2,500 accidents at work in Italy, three people die and twenty-seven will remain permanently disabled.
The Inail values confirm that in 2007 we have about 1,200 deaths as a result of accidents at work.
These values testify to the seriousness of the phenomenon, which remains one of the main reasons for death and causes almost twice as many deaths compared to murders.
In order to re-arrange the attention of institutions, social and information about this dramatic issue, Anmil now celebrates the 58th Day of Victims of Accident in All Italian Regions, set for the second Sunday in October.
"Worrying data extended by Anmile and the same tragic news of these days confirm the importance of the issue of occupational safety," confirms President of the Republic Giorgio Napolitano in a report sent to President Anmila, the National Association for Crippled and Disablised because of work, Pietro Mercandelli.
The head of state expresses his vivid admiration for the constant joint efforts for job security, for the protection of injured workers, for assistance to the families of the victims and for the participation of public opinion."
“Naturally the outraging ‘enough’ has been raised, sincerely shared in the face of tragedies, which, by their extent, provoke a stir in the media and the participation of public opinion, but the daily reality once again offers us dramatic cases, even recurring in its dynamics.”
Napolitano does not hide that "there is undoubtedly also a problem of resources here: it is crucial to invest in training and awareness.
Major initiatives promoted in the field of education are of particular importance, so that young people facing the world of labour are given clearer awareness.
It is necessary to draw attention to this phenomenon, not to give up due to its disturbing social seriousness, to apply and to improve legislative norms.
The president of the chamber, Gianfranco Fini, emphasized from his side "how the phenomenon of death as a result of an accident at work creates an absolutely critical social state, how it offends the conscience of each of us and forces all institutions to make a convincing effort towards the necessary termination of this situation."
“As far as occupational accident data is concerned, they appear to be showing some positive turnaround, but it doesn’t deny that the number of deaths due to an accident at work is alarming anyway,” said Anmilu President Pietro Mercandelli.
"We wouldn't just want this "reduction effort" to lead to less attention, because just when the attention stops, tragedy breaks out.
"We believe that, in general, too little is being done to stop the problem of injuries, and there are many activities that can have an impact on this problem.
It is necessary, in our opinion - stressed by Mercandelli - to increase controls and inspections, but also to intervene more strongly in the field of staff training, right from school age."
“There is still a need for further efforts – he added – in terms of communication and complicity of public opinion.
But above all, there is a greater sense of responsibility on the part of employers towards a phenomenon that represents an exceptional social phenomenon."
Mercandelli eventually declared that it was a progressive degeneration of the levels of the right to compensation foreseen for labor victims and the "displacement of supportive care" to which the system has been reaching in recent years."
“The goal, therefore, is to return to the worker the guarantee of insurance – he added – because the fate of those who hurt himself must not depend on spontaneity or endowment from time to time by the legislator.”
Mp3 players accused, expose hearing at risk
Listening loud music for a long time and using portable music devices as Mp3 players can cause permanent hearing damage.
According to the European Commission's announcement published today, which in an official report enlarged in Brussels quotes the opinion of the Committee on the Science of Health Risks, which has recently emerged and been identified (Csrseri).
“From this scientific point of view – we read in our notes – it follows that 5-10% of those who listen to music for at least five years using portable musical instruments, more than an hour a day a week and keep the device at high volume, risk a permanent loss of hearing ability.”
"It worries me that such a large number of young people, who use frequently portable music devices and mobile phones at high acoustic levels, can unwittingly damage their hearing in an irreparable way," admits Meglena Kuneva.
“The scientific findings – continue to lead – show a clear risk and need to be reacted quickly.
The most important thing is that we have to encourage consumers and publish this information.
We must continue to re-examine existing control systems in the light of this scientific finding in order to become completely efficient and to keep pace with the new technology."
Brussels asked the Independent Scientific Committee to investigate this issue due to the high use of portable musical instruments and the increasing number of young people exposed to noise of that origin.
“Scientists – concluded by the Eu Executive Officer – confirm the need to worry, and now the European Commission, together with the other members and parties, will examine the problem, what measures could possibly be implemented and thus better protect children and young people from exposure to noise caused by portable musical instruments and similar devices.”
Currently, there is already a safety European standard that limits the noise level of portable music devices to 100 dB, but there are growing concerns about hearing damage, which may be caused by excessive exposure to similar forms of noise.
It is very possible to limit these damages by looking for solutions such as reducing the level and duration of exposure.
In its conviction, the Eu Scientific Committee notes that users of portable musical instruments, if they use loud music (more than 89 decibels) for just five hours a week, will exceed the currently applicable limits corresponding to the maximum permitted noise in the workplace.
Those who listen to music in such conditions for an extended period of time risk permanent hearing loss after five years.
This problem should affect 5-10% of users of such devices, and their amount can be quantified between 2.5 and 10 million people in Eu.
Rome, Alemanno will leave the commission for the future of the capital
We'll start today, we'll close in March, we'll have sums in June.
Such is the Campidoglius calendar for the "Commission for the Future of the Capital of Rome", which came together this morning at its new mansion behind Caracallo Spa.
At six months, President Antonio Marzano and 49 other members of the commission will put together handbooks for Rome.
When doors are closed in March, Giunta and the municipal council will evaluate the analyses and projects so that in June they can present a strategic plan for Rome with an "official stamp" of Campidoglio and his councillor Gianni Alemann.
"The Commission is completely independent and all political, social and cultural ideas are included there – Alemanno wants to be emphasized – It will have to provide proposals for the development of Rome, which will eventually be used by political arbitrators, by which remain Giunta e Municipal Council.
It is not an exchange of functions, but merely an effort to have some place to process a major project for Rome.
In March, works will be completed and then dissolved, because this institution must have an exact time.
In June – the mayor announces – we will present a strategic plan and use the work carried out by the Commission for the Future of the Capital City of Rome.”
To those who accuse the Commission of useless, given that its opinion will not be binding, Alemanno responds: "It all depends on the work that the Commission will be able to carry out and the quality of their proposals.
If they are of good quality, just like the proposals of the Attali Commission, and I am sure of that in Rome, its contribution will be crucial.
Too often, politics splurges on everyday problems and the urgency that occurs from time to time.
We, on the contrary, want to look longer and further, in a deeper perspective, to work seriously on the development of Rome and its future.
In addition, the mayor of Rome wants to emphasise that "this Commission is neither an unnecessary enterprise nor a bluff: it is completely free, Roman citizens will not stand for even the euro and will serve to reflect and engage the best intelligence that the capital has".
For Alemann, in essence, "it is necessary to have a strategic sketch as a starting point, neither the definitive nor the motionless, in order to allow our city an authentic ascension at the level it badly needs, and to think with a unified spirit about its future."
The future, specified by the mayor of Rome "will not limit himself only to current municipal borders, but will look much further" not only in the optics of the future metropolitan city and its legal statute the capital, but "also include the role that Rome can and must perform as the capital of Europe and the Mediterranean."
Obama? First Anti-U.S. president. It’s already known why
One well-known U.S. president wrote that it's important for him to remain independent: "Barack Obama will be the second anti-American president."
The first was Jimmy Carter, who by his decisions decimated the U.S. economy and maintained relations (as even today as a private citizen) with the enemy Islam."
Attacks against Obama are mounting just as the election date is approaching.
One lady from Miami wrote on the website: "This man is full of ambition.
But Obama has an insolence: he thinks he'll make it to the nation's top post, with two governors and five black senators!"
In fact, these attacks don't say much"; they're attacks clearly of racial origin and they don't respond to a question from voters: will he be a good president? And then we've got polls too; everyone says Obama is going to win and with a big lead.
Even in this case, there are those who act according to the heart and not the brain.
One newspaper from Mississippi, from the bowels of the southern United States, wrote a comment: at least once in an election booth, we know well to whom an American patriot will give his vote.
One side of the American mentality, which remained high until the very last moment.
Say what you want, we'll eventually take care of it for ourselves, who to tick, whether Obama or McCain."
There are people who swore: if Obama were to win, we would leave America.
These are things that have never been said before, it seems almost a certain achievement of the black candidate.
However, in spite of all these considerations for Democrats, George Bush is the main weapon.
They surveyed 1500 people: what mistakes did Bush make in the course of his mandate? Eighty-nine percent replied: Iraq.
America is changing.
In Vietnam, this nation lost fifty thousand soldiers and sended him home with a kick in the ass.
In Iraq, 4,500 soldiers lost their lives, and the Americans were not forced to collapse.
And yet Iraq has become Bush's weakness for most.
Whoever takes the place of Georgia W will have to carry out a huge task.
The economic collapsion of the U.S. has also been felt by journalists: there's a period of inquiry in the newspaper. And many blame the internet, the "killer" of newspapers.
The PEW Research Center asked five hundred journalists about the condition of their profession and the assumption in terms of the future.
Today, almost all journalists are pessimistic about their future.
However, the respondents did not openly say that they had less concerns about credibility.
It doesn't feel isolated like it once did.
The problem for American journalists is money, the money.
For these journalists, the crisis of 2008 is a crisis of a devastated economic model.
In addition, cuts carried out in the newsroom, in the room where editors work to put together newspapers, are a huge problem.
According to respondents, this is a problem that needs to be solved if we do not want traditional journalism to fundamentally change as understood by the general public.
An American journalist permeates clear but complex pessimism.
Six out of ten press journalists and executive seniors are convinced that journalism on a national scale is going the wrong way.
In the local area, the executive group is at 65%.
Printed against the broadcast.
It's not difficult for journalists to see where the website is going.
Bigger publishers make their way to the Internet, which many view as a "killer" of printed matter.
"Economic coercion", which journalists point to, has increased by up to 79%.
Five hundred journalists have acknowledged that they are resorting to technology in the hope of "doing something good" for their journalism.
The biggest gap in American journalism is among those who run newsrooms – that is, leaders – and reporters who work for them.
56% of leaders at the national level are certain that their reporters share their values, while at the local level the figure drops to 23%.
This has something to do for someone with "work certainty."
Leaders who think they're going to lose their jobs within three years are 47% at national level.
When it comes to journalists, they're at 30%.
When it comes to "certain work" involving personnel (our editors, ndr) in America, it's still on a daily basis.
Only 13% of journalists are working on a contract.
In such a vast nation as Usa, with three hundred million inhabitants and thousands of newspapers, magazines, radios, TV and the Internet, employers often offer what they want, and so it's a question to take or leave.
Managers almost always receive huge salaries, and the editor can earn even ten thousand dollars a month outside of paying pension and insurance.
As far as holidays are then concerned, the argument is complicated: a certain number of days after the first year, a certain number of days after the third year to reach fifteen days after a certain number of years.
However, U.S. journalists, according to their own admissions, often get sick within a year.
It's just a question of climate.
UNIPOL: COURT IN ROME, DANILO COPPOLA REMAINS IN GROTTAFERRATA
The president of the Second Criminal Chamber of the Roman Court, Carmelo Rinaudo, has decided to deny filing a businessman with real estate Daniel Coppola, who asked to be able to move from his town, Grottaferrata, to an apartment in Rome.
Coppola is under investigation for bankruptcy in connection with counter-ofference when selling Unipol-Bnl.
During the trial at his own expense, he received his release last week from house arrest, which was converted into forced permanent residence in Grottaferrata.
His attorneys, Michele Gentiloni e Gaetano Pecorella, have asked for a real estate entrepreneur to change his whereabouts from a rural town to the capital.
However, according to the court, this transfer (against which pm) would allow him to influence his society and access the evidence that accuses him.
Elites in Danger
This phenomenon became apparent a few days ago, when the Chamber of Representatives voted against a rescue plan to provide $700bn to the White House: a significant proportion of Americans were more interested and interested in punishing Wall Street than saving economics.
This is confirmed by yesterday's New York Times, which informs us that the current crisis divides the Republican Party, constantly more divided between its old East Coast soul, milder, accommodating to federal institutions and the business community, and its new soul, which, on the contrary, is conservative, hostile to those of Washington» and towards the world of finance, strong especially in states in the middle and south.
The soul that arrogantly manifested itself in the vote I was talking about at the beginning.
But this just-cited thing is merely an example of the changes, the great changes that the current economic earthquake is already showing: not only in the United States, but throughout the West, and perhaps not just here.
Both in Usa and in Europe, this crisis seems to act as an accelerator of phenomena that have been incubated for a long time, which have been solidifying in the new climate and finally coming to light.
The first of these phenomena is reactualization, an extraordinary revival of the market, a double category State-power in relation to a certain renationalization of economics.
Indeed, the crisis is a crisis for the banking of financial institutions, which, of course, have strong bonds abroad, but in which, above all, the budgets of people and families living in a single country, in a single state, are included to the greatest extent.
This has created at the same time a political problem for those who run it: that is, how to respond to the difficulties and protests of people and families, which, among other things, are voters.
In short, the crisis appears economically global, but politically almost exclusively national.
Political internationality appears to be substantially out of play or has nothing much to add: the European Union, which is divided, as always, between different interests and different state strategies and which is unable to opt for one common political line, has given obvious evidence.
And so everyone awaits the intervention of the government of each individual state, rescue plans, market recovery, new rules and, most importantly, the division of funds: at the moment when we get into distress, only states have economic resources, an amount of financial resources to be able to try to put everything right.
He also owns the necessary means and authorities to use them: two sources of immense value, under certain circumstances, by which perhaps no market and any international organization will ever be able to dispose to a similar degree.
Moreover, this new emphasis, which the crisis puts on a state-national element, is in complete harmony with the ever-increasing importance which attempts to attribute the latest economic advances to one factor closely associated with it: territorial affiliation.
Paradoxically, while we were convinced that we had entered the kingdom of site, increasingly sophisticated technology, imaterial, while we were convinced that global finances were already destined to rule the world, out of nowhere we realized that our future, on the contrary, would have to deal more and more with such few immaterial things such as water, grain, oil.
However, all the things that he said can be transported from one place on the ground to another, but they are, in any case, united, absolutely, to the bounded area, territory.
So, whoever becomes their ruler certainly has a lot more cards up his sleeve than who he is not, and who's unlucky to live in a place without grain, without diesel and without water.
However, I get to the second news, which foresees a break.
Whoever talks about the territory, the state, the government, inevitably talks about politics, that is, about leadership.
Taking into account the economic crisis, two convergent phenomena appear to form.
On the one hand, the realisation that there is an objective need for authoritarian leadership, a request for someone who could seize the situation.
On the other hand, a wave of discreditedness towards existing leadership, especially economic ones, which have proved equally unsatisfactory and full of dark places.
And together something even deeper, and in some sense disturbing: discreditedness, impatience, a sense of incompetence and absence that seeks to dominate the entire ruling class in more and more Western countries.
It seems to spread more and more in the broad masses of the population’s beliefs than that their actions be the same as the ideas of the still popular elite, as their way of feeling and being, as their culture in the true sense of the word, that the elite has already surpassed their time and is indifferent to the views of the majority.
The demand for leadership simply nourishes one branch from the underground, but a strong movement for the removal of the ruling classes and the traditional political order manifests itself in the sudden electoral "resists"» or in the unexpected changes of collective sentiment in recent and most recent times, from the conservative rise in Austria-Bavaria to the fall of Labour in the United Kingdom, to the revolt of the American congressmen against Bush (and perhaps).
We have the impression that traditional elites, their parties, their programs, but also their ceremonies, their newspapers, their accredited intelligence, someone would say their format, have more and more problems to understand, i.e. to represent, which, not from today, is being created in the deep strata of Western societies, and that the economic crisis is strengthening again, it is growing, potentially overcoming.
Before this, it would be exaggerated to talk about a permanent "mass rebellion".
But we can't say it means having your eyes open properly.
Such glue for walking on the walls
Overcome the laws of gravity thanks to super glue and in the future to walk on walls like Spiderman.
This is the objective of various U.S. research centers, and now a team of four institutes has come very close to it.
Scientists managed to keep the hundred-kilogram body glued to the vertical wall using a sticker of just 2.5 centimeters square.
STUDIUM – The American journal Science published the discoveries of some University researchers in Dayton, Akrone, the Georgian Institute of Technology and the Air Force Research Laboratory.
The material invented by a team of researchers is the development of materials already existing, which use a special carbon structure, which is named carbon dwarf tubes.
«Basis glue can be imagined as a series of pipes laid like an arranged pile of spaghetties» explains Zhong Lin Wang of the Georgia Institute of Technology in Atlanta.
The researchers were inspired by the legs of the gecone, covered with microscopic hairs, which allow the animal to ensure a perfect adhesion.
Tests conducted on various materials, such as glass, Teflon and glass paper, have shown that the new glue is ten times thicker than the feet of these reptiles, and at the same time "it simply peels off the wall» explains Liming Dai, a researcher at the University of Daytone.
WALK ALONG THE WALLS? -"This material could be useful as makeshift glue» will supply Liming Dai.
Perhaps walking on walls like Spiderman? Perhaps they do, but scholars are thinking about far less fascinating use as a combination of electrical devices, because dwarven carbon pipes are conductors of heat and electricity.
Other applications could relate to aerospace engineering .
For now, no super heroes.
FURTHER RESEARCH – The University of Berkley, California, is also engaged in the research of a new glue.
In one article in the American Chemical Society journal, California researchers introduced micro-fibre-formed glue as hair that cleans itself from dust by moving across the surface to still maintain perfect snuggliness.
Medications: never with juices
Beware of grapefruit, orange and apple juice.
If you take these drinks, which are consumed primarily in the summer, because they are refreshing and full of vitamins, together with some life-saving medicines, their ingestion changes the amplification or decrease in effectiveness with serious health consequences.
In order to highlight the risks of both ready-to-fresh and fresh juices combined with the ingestion of certain drugs at the ongoing 263th National Meeting of the American Chemical Society in Philadelphia: just one glass of grapefruit, orange and apple juice to reduce the absorption of certain life-saving drugs, annulling their beneficial effects, and putting patient health at risk.
Grape juice has already been accused of being responsible for increasing up to a toxic level the effects of certain antiarithmic drugs, antibiotics, anxiolitics, antihistamine and neurological drugs.
DRUGS IN RISK – This was reported by the Italian Medical Agency itself in a newsletter two years ago, arousing doctors and patients not to underestimate the dangers arising from this mixture and to pay attention to what they drink in the event that they take drugs, such as Diazepam or Methadone.
NOT JUST GRAPEFRUIT – According to experts, orange and apple juice also contain substances that dampen the drugs.
A long list of the effects suppressed by grapefruit and other juices.
From there, Michele Carruba, director of the pharmacological department of the State University of Milan: “Such discoveries are usually immediately announced to various international and national pharmacy agencies, which will eventually arrange and exchange drug information instructions.
So always read the illustrative leaves of the drugs that you get and if you come across something unclear, don’t worry, but ask your doctor or pharmacist for clarification» has highlighted an expert for Ansa.
The Nobel for Economics by Paul Krugman
The Nobel Prize was awarded to American Paul Krugman for his studies of commercial models and the localization of economic activities.
Krugman is a 55-year-old Novokenian who teaches at the University of Priceton and who is a well-known editor of the New York Times.
Krugman is one of the most important critics regarding the Bush administration's foreign, economic and political actions.
A few months after the assassinations in September 2001, in the days when Enron crashed, Krugman predicted in one comment published in the New York Times that the second event would, in the sense of the consequences on the history of the United States, be much more important than the first.
A foreclosure that provoked a few controversies and which had been reproached for years, but which turned out to be accurate.
«I'm surprised and shocked at the speed with which collapses like Enron and Worldcom disappeared out of public interest, Krugman said at the end of 2002.
According to Lorenzo Bini Smaghi of the Executive Committee of the Central European Bank, Krugman is an "economist of great importance", but also a popularizer who has "a very aggressive style, especially critical against the excesses of the Bush Liberalist administration»».
Open Office, we have the third edition of the "anti-Microsoft" package»
It’s Open Office.org 3.0, an open-source personal production package that tends to penetrate more and more users, from home to office, from experts to public administration, as a free alternative to the better-known Microsoft Office.
After about an year’s work by volunteers controlled by SUN Microsystem, the new Open Office is shown to the general public with a variety of innovations: the welcome screen and the revised and improved opening window will be joined by more, far more substantial, such as compatibility for the Apple Mac OS X operating system and support for the new ODF (Open Document Format) 1.2, the latest ISO standard version.
Emphasis is also placed on collaborating with the Office 2007, Open Office package is able to open the document even with the new Microsoft extension (.docx, .xlsx, .pptx).
Interesting are the special features of some models such as what allows you to reduce the dimensions of a PowerPoint presentation, useful for example for electronic transmission, or the ability to transfer a PDF folder to a graphic design model and maintain the original appearance of the page.
From this point of view, we can still create "PDF hybrids" documents, which are similar to typical Adobe graphics and have the characteristics of a spreadsheet or word program.
Collaboration is an element that has received many improvements inside application programs: in excellence it is possible to work together among different users and avoid conflicts on changes, in a text program, each collaborator is assigned one color to the comments, so that there is a clearer identification in the revision phase.
Some companies are also working on transferring some Open Office features to the Web (on the cloud), and they are expected to deliver a definitive result in the next few weeks.
The Italian version can be downloaded at http://it.openoffice.org/download/3.0.0/download300.html, it has been added to the English version, which is located on the mirror from Friday.
In addition to Windows and Mac OS, as usual, the package is also available for the Linux operating system.
Also, the installation cd can be sent home or to the workplace on request.
And if the topic of freedom can be a draw for expansion in households, it is precisely on the business area of the Open Office that the package bets a lot, and it can already count on a variety of projects implemented in public administration and in schools.
And there are also offers of trainings, consultations and assistance services ideal for small and medium-sized companies, which could open up business perspectives.
Open Office consists of five main models: Writer, for video writing replacing Word; Calc is a spreadsheet processor that aims to replace Excel; Impress for presentations similar to Powerpoint; Dbase, as in the case of more famous Access, is a program that allows you to work on databases; Draw, eventually, is a graphical model.
The goal for 2008 is to achieve five million downloads, and when we consider that to date we are at almost 3 million and 600 thousand, with the release of a new version, it is possible that this predicted number can also be surpassed.
Kohla's results are also positive, it's the fourth case of doping
A new positive case at the last Tour de France: this is Austrian Bernhard Kohl, from the Gerolsteiner team, the third classified and t-shirt winner of the best climber.
The report was quoted by L'Equipe's website.
The 26-year-old Kohl is the fourth racer positive on Cera, the third-generation Epo, after Italians Riccardo Riccò and Leonader Piepoli and the Germans Stefan Schumacher, as well as him from Gerolsteiner.
Kohla's blood-sampling results fall into those carried out in recent weeks by Losanna and Chatenay-Malabra.
If the control analyses confirm the positivity on Cera, the Austrian will lose third place on the Tour and will have to face two years of disqualification.
The report on the positivity of Kohl at the third-generation Epo is confirmed by the head of the French anti-doping agency Pierre Bordry, who told the Associated Press that it had already reported on the outcome by Austrian government authorities.
The 26-year-old Austrian placed on the podium behind Spaniard Carlos Sastro and American Cadel Evansema, who also won the polka dot T-shirt of the best climber.
IMMIGRANTS:NEW YORK TIMES ASKS, IS ITALY RACIST?
Italy "as a country of emigrants has only in the last house been transformed into the priority goal of immigrants" adopted with resistance.
Is it turning a beautiful country into a nation of racists? That's how it's asked in one long New York Times article that devoted this article to a debate on the topic of racism in Italy, and began with the case of a young Abdul William Guibre originally from Burkina Faso, killed last month in Milan for stealing a box of biscuits.
This case makes visible what the New York Times defines the "paradox of the north": the northern regions are at the same time those where "more integration and more work" is registered, but also "the strongest anti-immigration mindset in the country and the Northern League's strongest support."
In order to confirm rising tensions between Italians and immigrants, the NYT cites instances of intolerance from Milan, Parma, Rome, Ciampina and Castel Volturna.
It's not just that.
Also, the new legislation demanded by Lega, which seeks to hamper immigration and facilitate the expulsion of illegal immigrants, is cited as an example of Italy's changing climate.
“Giorgio Napolitano, after meeting Benedette XVI in Quirinale, asked the state and the church to work together to defeat racism.”
Students victims of expensive rents - up to 900 euros per room
The renting market for university students doesn't know the crisis.
According to Sunia's latest research, unions for tenants, to rent a single room can cost a student in Milan up to 900 euros, in Florence 700, in Naples 650, in Rome 600.
“The phenomenon has already reached such proportions that entire layers of students have lost their right to study because of the overall too high prices that their families would have to endure – Laura Mariani, the head of Sunia’s study office, explains – in this city a great demand on the part of students distorted the market in such a way that it initiated the process of price rises, which has also spread to residents.”
In most cases, rent offers contain several offenses: contracts in a free manner, unregistered, without limited rent, associated with subleases.
An exceptional intervention is needed for Sunji, which could regulate prices thanks to a tax review.
If we look at the details of the research, we will see that in Milan, in the Brianza area, about 450 euros is needed for one bed, for a single room is paid from a minimum price of 650 (Bande Nere zone) through a median value of 800 (Lambiate, Udine and Fiera zone) to a maximum of 900 (in the Vittoria zone).
In Florence, one bed costs an average of 350/400 euros, one room about 700.
In Bologna, where students concentrate in zones near the university, 250/280 euro per bed in a double room, from 370 to 500 in a single.
In Rome in the central university zones (San Lorenzo, piazza Bologna) they want about 600 euros for a single room, 450 beds in a double.
Values are slightly lower (550 euros per single) in zones close to other universities (Ostiense and Cinecittà).
We will save only if we move to peripheral areas: 300 euros per bed and 450 for a single room zones Prenestina, Centocelle and the like.
In Napolia there is 300/450 euros for a bed, for a room from 400 to 600 euros, with the highest prices in Policlinico, Vomero and Colli Aminei zones.
In Bari they want 250/350 euros for a bed, at least 350 for a single room.
In smaller cities, prices are definitely lower, but they have greater impacts on citizens' economies.
In cities such as Perugia, where the most common phenomenon is rent for students from outside the European Union, one other anomaly is finally recorded: an increase of about 25-30% of the fee they want from foreign students, compared to Italian ones.
Good-bye to the old Paradiso cinema, cities are disturbed by historical halls
Cinematographic historical halls are closed in favor of the Multiplex.
"A city without cinemas is like the blind, it's like a face without eyes."
The image offered by director Giuseppe Tornator successfully documents what is happening in large Italian city centres, as well as rural towns.
The process of limiting cinemas, the birth of large multi-halls in the suburbs, and the subsequent demise of monosals in the central zones of cities, is one of the events that seem inevitable and irreversible, but also has all the hallmarks of true cultural crime.
It's often historical places full of memories," it's as if we'd lost a friend, an acquaintance with whom we shared a long way and a lot of emotions», feels the director of the New Cinema Paradiso, a film that twenty years ago won the Tornator Oscar and the Grand Jury Prize in Cannes, and who told the poetry of one village cinema and sadness because of a way of life.
Values obtained by Anec, the main association of operators, are clear: in the last five years it has closed the doors of 316 cinemas of the traditional tip, and the highest bill was paid primarily by halls in the most important cities: about 10 fewer in Florence, about 9 in Milana, Naples, Bologna and Rome, 6 fewer in Torin, about 5 in Palermo.
Emilia-Romagna is among the regions most affected, with the loss of 82 cinema halls, followed by Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
And proof that the first responsible for their disappearance is the onset of the multiplex is precisely these three Italian regions, which registered the highest number of new cinema multi-halls: 195 more canvases in Lombardy over the past five years, 81 in both Tuscany and Emilia-Romagna.
«Italian procedure has evolved unrationally» claims Paolo Protti, president of Anecu, "it is useful to create new structures, but it would be necessary to guarantee that the already existing ones will survive, because when one cinema closes in the city, not all of its visitors are willing to relocate to the multiplex.
Then let's not even talk about the insufficient subsidy for the fund in favour of the operators: the previous sovereign debt is about 10 million euros.
From a moral and ethical point of view, it is an embarrassment that the government does not respect existing laws.
It would take between 16 and 18 million euros per year to support the sector».
Interesting is the bologue case, Italy's first city to worry about closures and which has decided to find a starting point by signing a protoculus between the municipality and Aneco, the first of this type in Italy to protect and promote small cinemas both in historic centers and in the suburbs.
Among the planned standards are steps of tax breaks in favour of operators and benefits for the audience in terms of mobility: spectators will be able to drive into the restricted transport zone and will have special prices to stop and in some car parks.
«I like this initiative» says Giuseppe Tornatore "and I hope that other cities will follow the example of Bologna with the introduction of other measures».
However, it is certain that in order to oppose the great power of the multiplex, traditional halls must improve the quality of sound and image, because today's audience is more demanding.
Defending a traditional cinema is a theme that has always been important: the closure of Super Cinema in Bagheria, the place of my childhood and adolescence, was a spark that convinced me to establish the "New Paradiso Cinema," which I've been thinking about for 10 years.
I’ve been living in Rome for 25 years, and when I’m walking in front of the already closed Rialt or Quirinal in Nazionale Street, where I’ve shared so many emotions so that I don’t have to look at inscriptions blackened with dust, I turn to the other side».
The connection between politics and mafia group 'ndrangheta, mayor of Gioie Tauro in prison
"The mayor of Gioie Tauro was willing to adjust the elections of the municipal authority according to the interests of the Piromalli mafia group."
Allegations brought by the Anti-Mafia in Calabria against mayor and vice-mayor Gioi Tauro Giorgio Dal Torrione e Rosario Schiavone, and mayor Rosarno Carlo Martelli, imprisoned this morning for external cooperation with a mafia organization, are tough.
In handcuffs also Gioacchino Piromalli, an old intermediary between the clan and the public administration, and his grandson attorney, the chief's namesake, already accused of committing crimes of a mafia character, sentenced to the payment of 10 million euros.
"They have ignored all legal and moral norms, as well as common sense," prosecutors write, when two local administrations expressed a willingness to pay consultations to lawyer Piromalli.
“In this way, the Mafia group would be allowed – written by judges – to officially join the municipal authorities and facilitate the already enormous possibilities of control and management of public administration.”
Calabrian regional commission president Agazio Loiero doesn't hide that "there are a lot of secret agreements between local institutions and the organized criminal underworld in his county: "There are areas in which mafia's concentration is stronger than in others, where collusion agreements are unavailable.
But we also have a lot of local politicians who resist the pressures of 'ndrangheta, heroes who are still immaculate."
Imprisonments from this morning follow the imprisonment of July last year, by which police showed that the mafia group Piromalli was given the final intervention.
Since the end of April last year - the City Council of Gioii Tauro has been dissolved since 1991, the reason at that time was the anticipated connection between organized crime and the local administration - local politicians were deprived of functions in the municipal administration.
Giorgio Dal Torrione, president of the Christian Democrats, who at the moment of dissolution was elected the head of the right-center coalition after a closer election in May.
He shouted over the scandal when Prefecture ordered the dissolution of the municipal council.
He called the responsibility of then-deputy Interior Minister Marco Minniti, accusing him of orchestrating everything for political reasons, but judges in an imprisonation order signed this morning explain that the mafia group had even come so far as to request a change to the A3 motorway project.
From further interrogations ordered against Dal Torrion, public prosecutors write, it has come to light "willingness to move the demands of an entrepreneur, representatives of a larger group very close, as is generally known, Piromalli, and to adapt the elections of public administration to the interests of the mafia group."
When the energy is born from the waves, the big ones come into play.
It's an endless deposit that covers more than 70% of the Earth's surface, and it's now starting to be discovered by the world's major energy companies.
Waves, currents, tides are new sources of inexhaustible energy, on which the eyes of colossuses such as Chevron, who are now trying to lay their hands on these sources.
The ability to generate energy from sea movements has been studied for more than a hundred years, and in the 1970s in connection with the first oil crisis, the research intensified.
When the price of oil declined around 1980, the idea of using marine energy went aside. But now an environmental organization and a new increase in the price of oil has given the sector a new impetus.
The World Energy Council (WEC) in London said in 2007 that around 15 percent of the world's electricity consumption could be covered using the movement of sea waves.
this amount would be twice the energy that is currently produced in nuclear centers.
Moreover, there are currents and tides.
Giants are attacking.
The company that bet on marine energy was Enersis, which agreed in 2005 to build facilities in northern Portugal.
When this intention was announced, it looked like a huge revolutionary step.
After two years of betting on marine energy, more and more companies are betting on sea energy rather than traditional energy.
“Chevron is studying ocean energy technology – a spokesperson for a company that is connected to the United States oil colossus in the Wall Street Journal’s economic daily newspaper – and they are thinking about the possibility of working with us.”
The collaboration we are considering relates to obtaining permission from the U.S. Energy Commission, which is necessary to start a marine energy project in Alaska.
We cannot underestimate this signal, especially considering that it is in Alaska the U.S
In addition to Chevron, Verdant Power Inc. from New York and Ocean Power of New Jersey are also tracking the trail of marine energy, and the sums invested so far, although not known in detail, have shown increasing interest, according to observers.
The technology that needs to be developed.
Experts are talking about the technology, which is still in the beginning, because it is not known what impact the use of marine energy will have on the environment.
For now, facilities in Portugal and some in Scotland are small dimensions and don't seem to cause major ecosystem problems.
However, for greater energy production it is necessary to use technologies with a greater impact on the environment.
Methods are different: Ocean Power, for example, uses a fear system that is connected to each other.
The strength of the waves, which moves with buoys up and down, is changed with the help of cylinders and cylinders to hydraulic pressure inside these buoys.
The pressure spins the turbine, which delivers energy to the generator.
The energy reaches shore by undersea cables.
So far, there are four devices of this type in the project phase, which are waiting only for federal approval, and part of the energy could be used for military purposes.
Verdant Power, on the other hand, is already producing energy for a mall and a car park using six seaside turbines in New York's East River.
The movement of water during tide and tide is caused by the rotation of the turbines, which feed the generator with this revolving movement.
Verdant said they have a long list of clients waiting for the necessary authorizations due to the possibility of using this energy.
After the waves, the tide and the tide.
Particular interest at this moment deserves the potential of tides, they say at the Institute for Electricity Research.
Although it is an incoherent factor, tides are more predictable than wind, sun or waves, and therefore more reliable as a source of energy.
It was into the energy of influctions and tides that states such as New York, Maine and Alaska, and other coastal states had already invested 7 and a half million dollars in 2008 and predicted investments of up to US$35 million for 2009.
